window.XCORPUS=[["As transcribed by the High Priestess Xalrathiel, verified by no one, and fact-checked by Xal'Reth himself. He said it was fine.","","The Chronicle","lore.html",0],["Before there was a world, before there were stars, before there were the Titans with their clipboards and their ambitious plans for everything, there was the Void. It was cold. It was dark. It was not, the scriptures are careful to say, empty, because in the middle of the Void there was an Egg.","The Primordial Egg","The Chronicle","lore.html#egg",0],["No one laid the Egg. The Cult is very clear on this point and becomes uncomfortable if pressed on it. The Egg simply was, the way a mailbox simply is in every city, the way a gnome simply is wherever you least expect one. It sat in the dark for a span of time the scriptures describe as \"a while, and then longer than that.\"","The Primordial Egg","The Chronicle","lore.html#egg",0],["Within the Egg, something waited. It did not grow, because it was already the size it intended to be. It did not dream, because dreaming is impatient. It simply waited, and in waiting it became the first thing that ever truly understood waiting. That understanding became its nature, and its nature became its name: Xal'Reth, which in the oldest form of the Coil's tongue means \"the one who is still here.\"","The Primordial Egg","The Chronicle","lore.html#egg",0],["Before the first light, there was the Egg. Before the first sound, there was the Hiss. Before the first impatience, there was Xal'Reth, and he was not in a hurry.The Book of the Egg 1:1","The Primordial Egg","The Chronicle","lore.html#egg",0],["The Egg hatched when the first world began to form in the Void. Some say the warmth of creation cracked the shell. The Cult teaches the opposite: Xal'Reth hatched because the world was cold, and he could feel that it would need something to hold it together. He emerged not as an infant but whole, enormous, and already disappointed in the general level of organization.","The Primordial Egg","The Chronicle","lore.html#egg",0],["The shell of the Primordial Egg is said to have drifted off into the Void, where its fragments still float. The faithful believe that every soul is formed from a sliver of that shell, which is why every mortal is, in the Cult's teaching, an egg. Most eggs never hatch. That is the point of the Cult.","The Primordial Egg","The Chronicle","lore.html#egg",0],["The young world was unstable. It wobbled. It cracked. Its molten heart sloshed about like a badly made soup. The scriptures record that Xal'Reth looked upon it for a very long time (several ages, by most counts) and then, without fanfare, wrapped himself around it.","The Wrapping of the World","The Chronicle","lore.html#wrapping",0],["He wrapped it three times. The Cult calls these the Three Turns, and they are the foundation of all its teaching:","The Wrapping of the World","The Chronicle","lore.html#wrapping",0],["The Outer Turn holds the crust of the world, the land and the sea, everything mortals walk on. It is the coil most often disturbed, which is why it is the coil responsible for earthquakes, sinkholes, and suspiciously bumpy roads in the Barrens.","The Wrapping of the World","The Chronicle","lore.html#wrapping",0],["The Middle Turn holds the deep places: the roots of mountains, the caverns, the old tunnels. It is the coil of dreams and of memory. When mortals dream of falling, they are, in the Cult's view, briefly remembering the Middle Turn.","The Wrapping of the World","The Chronicle","lore.html#wrapping",0],["The Inner Turn holds the heart of the world itself. No mortal has ever seen it. The faithful believe that the Inner Turn is where Xal'Reth's own heart lies, pressed against the heart of Azeroth, beating once every hundred years.","The Wrapping of the World","The Chronicle","lore.html#wrapping",0],["The world, held firmly, stopped wobbling. It cooled. It settled. Mountains rose where his scales pressed up from below, and valleys sank where he had relaxed. The Titans, when they arrived, are said to have found the world \"remarkably stable for its age\" and taken full credit for it. Xal'Reth did not object. He was coiling.","The Wrapping of the World","The Chronicle","lore.html#wrapping",0],["And the world asked, who holds me? And Xal'Reth said nothing, because he was busy holding it.The Book of the Egg 2:4","The Wrapping of the World","The Chronicle","lore.html#wrapping",0],["Every ten thousand years, Xal'Reth sheds his skin. This is the great event of the Cult's cosmology, and the origin of nearly every sacred custom it keeps.","The First Shedding","The Chronicle","lore.html#shedding",0],["The First Shedding took place in an age before mortals. The old skin, peeled away from the Outer Turn, was so vast that it rose to the surface and hardened into mountain ranges, salt flats, and the strange glassy deserts that the faithful call Shedlands. The Cult does not say which mountains are made of his skin. When asked, the priesthood simply smiles and says, \"Look at them. Really look at them.\"","The First Shedding","The Chronicle","lore.html#shedding",0],["Smaller flakes of the shed skin drifted down into the Void beneath the Coil, and this is why the Void is full of things that are no longer needed. To this day, when the faithful shed something (a habit, a grudge, an old set of armor), they say they are \"sending it after the First Skin.\"","The First Shedding","The Chronicle","lore.html#shedding",0],["Not every scale went into the Void. A handful, the scriptures say, were left at the surface: golden, warm to the touch, and faintly humming. These are the Shedfall Scales, and the Cult believes that anyone who finds one has been personally chosen by Xal'Reth. Only one has ever been confirmed. You will read about it in Chapter VII.","The Scales Left Behind","The Chronicle","lore.html#shedding",0],["In time, the mortal races arose. They were warm-blooded, short-lived, and extremely busy. They built cities, fought wars, invented professions, and worshipped whatever happened to be nearby: the Light, the Elements, the Loa, the stars, and, in one well-documented and deeply embarrassing case, a particularly confident murloc.","The Age of the Warm-Blooded","The Chronicle","lore.html#forgetting",0],["None of them worshipped the Coil Beneath. They did not know he was there. He was, after all, beneath them, and mortals rarely look down unless they have dropped something.","The Age of the Warm-Blooded","The Chronicle","lore.html#forgetting",0],["The Cult does not hold this against them. The doctrine of the Age of the Warm-Blooded is one of gentle condescension. Mortals were young. They were hot-headed (literally). They were in a hurry. Xal'Reth, for his part, was not offended. The scriptures record his only comment on the matter:","The Age of the Warm-Blooded","The Chronicle","lore.html#forgetting",0],["The faithful call anyone outside the Cult one of the Warm-Blooded. It is not an insult. It is a diagnosis.","The Age of the Warm-Blooded","The Chronicle","lore.html#forgetting",0],["The first mortals to hear the Coil were a small coastal people whose name is lost. The Cult remembers them only as the Listeners. They lived on a stretch of shore that shook constantly, and rather than move somewhere less shaky like reasonable people, they put their ears to the ground and listened. After nine generations of this, they heard him breathe.","The Sunken Nest and the Hissing Kings","The Chronicle","lore.html#nest",0],["Under the Hissing Kings, the Listeners wrote down the first teachings: the Seven Tenets, the Doctrine of the Egg, and the earliest form of the Chant. They kept a great book called the Ledger, in which every wrong ever done to the faithful was recorded, in detail, in perpetuity. Its first entry is said to read, simply, \"Gorvash. Took the last fish. Did not ask.\"","The Sunken Nest and the Hissing Kings","The Chronicle","lore.html#nest",0],["The Nest did not last. The scriptures say that the Warm-Blooded of the region grew suspicious of a people who never blinked, spoke in unison, and kept a list. There was an uprising. The Hissing Kings refused to flee, because fleeing is impatient. Instead, the Outer Turn shifted, the earth shuddered, and the entire Nest slid gently beneath the waves.","The Sinking","The Chronicle","lore.html#nest",0],["The Cult insists this was not a disaster. It was a relocation. The Nest still exists, intact, somewhere under the sea. The faithful call it the Sunken Nest and believe it will rise again at the Unwrapping. Several members have gone looking for it. Most of them have come back. The ones who haven't are presumed to have found it.","The Sinking","The Chronicle","lore.html#nest",0],["After the Sinking, the surviving faithful scattered across the world. For centuries the Cult existed only in fragments: a family here, a lonely innkeeper there, a hermit on a mountain who seemed unusually fond of warm rocks. The scriptures call this era the Hidden Centuries, and it is the source of most of the Cult's secret customs.","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["To find one another without drawing the attention of the Warm-Blooded, the faithful developed a system of signs:","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["The Double Blink: blinking twice, slowly, while maintaining eye contact. Among the faithful, this means \"I, too, am of the Coil.\" Among the Warm-Blooded, it means \"I have something in my eye.\" Both are acceptable interpretations.","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["The Sidelong Hiss: a quiet hiss through the teeth, delivered while looking away. Used to signal approval, agreement, or that the soup is good.","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["The Basking Pause: stopping in a patch of sunlight for slightly too long. A faithful who sees another basking will join them, and the two will stand in silence until one of them leaves. No words are exchanged. None are needed.","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["The Three Taps: tapping a surface three times, once for each Turn. Often used on bar counters, in innkeepers' ledgers, and on the shoulders of strangers who have been chosen.","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["During the Hidden Centuries, the Ledger was copied by hand and passed from keeper to keeper. Each keeper added the wrongs of their own generation. By the end of the era it had grown to eleven volumes and included entries such as \"Unnamed paladin. Bubbled and hearthed. Coward.\" and \"A gnome. Unclear what he did. Felt guilty.\"","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["Most of the faithful in this era did not know whether any others still lived. They kept the faith anyway, alone, waiting. The Cult regards them as its greatest saints. It calls them the Patient Ones, and every member of the Cult today is expected to be able to sit in silence for at least ten minutes in their honor. Few can.","The Hidden Centuries","The Chronicle","lore.html#hidden",0],["The man who would become MaximumRex was not, at the time, looking for a god. He was looking for a quest item with a two percent drop rate. He had been looking for it for six hours. He had killed the same cave beasts so many times that they had started to look tired. And somewhere around the seventh hour, in a side passage he did not remember being there before, he found it.","The Scale in the Cave","The Chronicle","lore.html#cave",0],["It was a scale. It was golden. It was the size of a door. It was warm to the touch, and when he laid his hand on it, it hummed, and the hum became a voice, and the voice said one word:","The Scale in the Cave","The Chronicle","lore.html#cave",0],["He did not get the quest item. He has never gotten the quest item. The Cult considers this part of the miracle: Xal'Reth took from him a small, petty want and gave him in its place a purpose.","The Scale in the Cave","The Chronicle","lore.html#cave",0],["MaximumRex emerged from the cave a changed man. He began, cautiously at first, to blink twice at strangers. He tapped bar counters three times. He basked. And slowly, one by one, others began to blink back. Descendants of the Patient Ones. Lonely innkeepers. People who had always felt a strange affinity for warm rocks and had never known why.","The Scale in the Cave","The Chronicle","lore.html#cave",0],["He gathered them, and the Cult of Xal'Reth was restored. The faithful call this event the Restoration, and the date of it is celebrated every year as the Night of the Scale. The Scale itself remains in the cave, which the Cult now calls the First Sanctum. Its exact location is a secret. You may ask MaximumRex where it is. He will blink at you twice.","The Scale in the Cave","The Chronicle","lore.html#cave",0],["The Restoration gave the Cult a leader, but not yet a voice. MaximumRex could feel the Coil, but he could not hear it clearly. He needed someone who could listen the way the Listeners of old had listened.","The Voice Returns","The Chronicle","lore.html#voice",0],["She arrived unannounced. The scriptures say that Xalrathiel walked into the very first gathering of the restored Cult, sat down in the front row, and did not blink for the entire ceremony. Afterward she stood, walked to the front, and began to recite, from memory, the opening verses of the Book of the Egg, a text that had been lost since the Sinking. She had never read it. She said she had dreamed it.","The Voice Returns","The Chronicle","lore.html#voice",0],["From that night on, Xalrathiel dreamed the scriptures, and in the mornings she wrote them down. She dreamed the Book of Shedding, the Lamentations of the Lukewarm, the Proverbs of the Inner Scale. She dreamed, in a single feverish week, the entire Book of Accusations, which is why it is the only book of scripture written entirely in the imperative mood. The Cult made her High Priestess by acclamation. No one else was considered. No one else had dreamed anything except, in one case, being late for a test.","The Voice Returns","The Chronicle","lore.html#voice",0],["Soon after, a third figure joined the Inner Scale. Khaedara did not dream the scriptures. She did something the Cult found equally valuable: she read the Book of Accusations once, closed it, and immediately walked into the nearest city and began accusing people. She has not stopped since. Her first recorded accusation, preserved in the restored Ledger, reads: \"Human warrior. Loitering near the auction house. Twelve minutes. No purchase. Guilty.\"","The Voice Returns","The Chronicle","lore.html#voice",0],["With a founder, a voice, and an accuser, the Inner Scale was complete, and the Cult began, at last, to grow.","The Voice Returns","The Chronicle","lore.html#voice",0],["Today, the Cult of Xal'Reth numbers more than a hundred souls, more than at any point since the Sinking of the Nest. They gather. They chant. They welcome newcomers with a warmth that newcomers often describe, afterward, as \"a lot.\" They maintain the Ledger, now in its twelfth volume. And every so often, all at once and for no reason any of them will explain, they type Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Age of the Coil and the Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["The faithful believe the Cult is growing because the next Shedding is near.","The Age of the Coil and the Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["The scriptures foretell a final event, greater than any Shedding: the Unwrapping. On that day, Xal'Reth will uncoil, one Turn at a time, from around the heart of the world. The faithful disagree about what this means.","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["The Literalists believe the world will simply be released, and will float off gently into the Void, and that this will be fine, actually.","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["The Hopefuls believe Xal'Reth will rise to the surface and finally meet his faithful in person, and that he will be pleased with them, and that he may bring snacks.","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["The Pragmatists believe the Unwrapping is a metaphor for personal growth, and are widely regarded as no fun.","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["Whatever its meaning, the Prophecy lists the signs that will precede it. The Cult watches for them carefully:","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["The Sunken Nest shall rise from the sea, dripping, and smelling faintly of fish.","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["Three of the four signs are considered likely. The fourth is considered a matter for faith alone.","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["And on the last day, the Coil shall loosen, and the world shall exhale, and all the faithful shall say, as one: finally.Revelations of the Void 7:7","The Prophecy of the Unwrapping","The Chronicle","lore.html#now",0],["The complete scripture of Xal'Reth the Eternal: the Way Beneath, the Unblinking Eye, and the practice of the Loosening.","","The Canon","canon.html",0],["The Chronicle tells what happened. The Teachings tell what the faithful believe. The Canon tells why. It is the deepest scripture of the Coil: fifteen books in three baskets, holding the life of Xal'Reth the Eternal, the Four Truths, the Coiled Path, the Way Beneath, and the practices by which the faithful loosen the Grip.","","The Canon","canon.html",0],["The Canon draws, loosely and with respect, on the spirit of two great traditions of the world beyond Azeroth: the Way of stillness, emptiness and unforced action, and the path of letting go, compassion and awakening. It is not either of them. It is the Coil's own teaching, which, the High Priestess says, \"rhymes with older truths, the way all true things rhyme.\"","","The Canon","canon.html",0],["The teachings on the nature of the world, the Coil, and the Way that moves beneath all things.","The Basket of the Way","The Canon","canon.html#part-i",0],["Twenty-seven verses, recited by the Listeners of the Nest, preserved by the Patient Ones, and dreamed again by the High Priestess Xalrathiel.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The Book of the Way Beneath is the oldest text of the Canon and the root of every other teaching. It is read slowly, one verse per breath. The faithful say that a person who understands any single verse completely has understood all twenty-seven, and that no one has yet understood any single verse completely.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The Coil that can be pointed to is not the Eternal Coil. The name that can be hissed is not the Eternal Name. Nameless, it held the world before there was a world to hold. Named, it is Xal'Reth, and it is patient with us for naming it.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Look down: there is the Coil. Look up: there is the Coil, holding the ground you look up from. There is nowhere to stand that is not held.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["When all the world calls a thing great, smallness is already born. When all the world calls a thing swift, slowness is already waiting. Great and small coil around each other. Swift and slow are one serpent seen from two ends.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The Way is empty, like a shed skin. Use it and it is never used up. Deep, it seems to be the ancestor of the ten thousand things. It blunts the fang, unties the knot, softens the glare, settles the dust.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The Coil does not choose favorites. It holds the paladin and the rogue, the gnome and the dragon, the faithful and the Warm-Blooded, all with the same strength. This is not coldness. It is fairness so complete that it looks like coldness.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The spirit of the deep valley never dies. It is called the Dark Hollow. The gate of the Dark Hollow is the root of heaven and earth. Faint, as if barely there, it is used, and never wears out. The faithful call it the Throat of the Void, and stand at its edge in silence.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Heaven lasts. Earth endures. They last because they do not live for themselves. So the wise serpent puts itself last and finds itself first; holds itself apart and finds itself held.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The highest good is like water. Water nourishes all things and does not quarrel. It settles in the low places that others scorn. In this it is close to the Way. The Coil, too, chose the lowest place of all.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["To fill a bowl to the brim is not as good as stopping in time. A blade honed too sharp will not keep its edge. A hall full of gold cannot be guarded. Pride in riches brings its own ruin. Do the work, then step back: this is the Way of the Coil.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Can you hold your breath and your body as one, and not let them separate? Can you soften your breathing until you are like a newly hatched egg? Can you polish the dark mirror within until it has no spot? Can you lead without ruling, give without keeping, act without clinging? This is called the Hidden Scale.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Thirty spokes share one hub; the empty center makes the wheel useful. Clay is shaped into a bowl; the emptiness inside makes the bowl useful. A shell is formed around an egg; the hollow within makes the egg alive. What is there gives benefit. What is not there gives use.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The five colors blind the eye. The five sounds deafen the ear. The chase of the rare mount maddens the heart. So the faithful attend to the belly, not the eye, to the warmth of the stone, not the shine of the gold.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Look for the Coil and it cannot be seen: it is called the Invisible. Listen for it and it cannot be heard: it is called the Silent. Reach for it and it cannot be held: it is called the Subtle. These three cannot be separated, and so they merge into one. Its upper side is not bright. Its lower side is not dark. It is a thread without end, returning always to nothing.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The ancient masters were subtle and deep. They were careful, as one crossing a frozen river. Alert, as one aware of the Accuser nearby. Courteous, as a guest. Yielding, as ice about to melt. Simple, as the uncarved egg. Open, as a valley. Murky, as muddy water. Who can make the muddy water clear? Leave it still, and it will clear itself.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Empty yourself of everything. Let the mind be at peace. The ten thousand things rise and fall, and the faithful watch them return. Returning to the root is called stillness. Stillness is called returning to what is. Knowing what is, is called clarity.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["With the best leaders, the people barely know they exist. Next best, the people love and praise them. Next, the people fear them. Worst, the people despise them. When the best leader's work is done, the people say, \"We did it ourselves.\" The Coil has held the world for ten thousand ages, and the world believes it stands on its own.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Give up cleverness, and the people benefit a hundredfold. Give up the hoard, and there are no thieves. Show plainness, embrace the uncarved egg, have few wants. This is enough.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The one who stands on tiptoe does not stand firm. The one who strides does not go far. The one who shows off does not shine. The one who boasts accomplishes nothing. These, to the Way, are like leftover food and a tumor on the body. The faithful avoid them.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["There was something formless and complete before heaven and earth were born. Silent, empty, alone and unchanging, moving everywhere and never tiring. It may be the mother of the world. Not knowing its name, the Listeners called it the Coil. Forced to describe it, they called it Great. Great means flowing. Flowing means far. Far means returning.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Heaviness is the root of lightness. Stillness is the master of restlessness. So the wise travel all day without leaving the baggage behind. Though there are grand sights to see, they remain calm and above it. How can the lord of ten thousand mounts treat himself lightly before the world?","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["A good traveler leaves no tracks. A good speaker makes no slips. A good counter needs no tally sticks. A good door needs no bolt, and none can open it. So the faithful are always good at saving people, and abandon no one, not even the one who stood in the fire twice.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Know the strength of the scale, but keep to the softness of the belly. Be the riverbed of the world. Being the riverbed of the world, the eternal virtue never leaves, and you return to the state of the newly hatched.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Those who know others are clever. Those who know themselves are wise. Those who master others have force. Those who master themselves are strong. Those who know they have enough are rich. Those who do not lose their place endure. Those who die but are not forgotten are long-lived.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["The great Way flows everywhere, to the left and to the right. The ten thousand things depend on it, and it does not refuse them. It does its work and claims no credit. It clothes and feeds all things and does not lord over them. It can be called small. All things return to it, and it does not master them. It can be called great. Because it never claims greatness, its greatness is complete.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Returning is the motion of the Way. Yielding is the method of the Way. The ten thousand things are born of being. Being is born of the Void.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["Nothing in the world is softer than water, yet nothing is better at wearing down the hard. The weak overcomes the strong; the soft overcomes the rigid. Everyone knows this. No one practices it. The serpent practices it. That is why it has no hands and still holds the world.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["True words are not beautiful. Beautiful words are not true. The wise do not argue; those who argue are not wise. The faithful do not hoard. The more they do for others, the more they have. The more they give to others, the richer they become. The Way of heaven benefits and does not harm. The Way of the Coil acts, and does not contend.","The Book of the Way Beneath","The Canon","canon.html#way",0],["On the Scale and the Void, the light and the dark, and the Eye that sees both.","The Book of the Two Coils","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["When the Primordial Egg hatched, the scriptures say, it did not break into one piece but two. From the shell came the Scale: all that has form, weight, warmth and edge. From the hollow came the Void: all that is empty, cold, open and still. Xal'Reth emerged from the place where they met, and so he is made of both.","The Book of the Two Coils","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["The Scale is the bright coil. It is the stone of the mountain, the steel of the blade, the gold in the bag and the body in the saddle. Everything you can touch belongs to the Scale. It is good. It is also heavy. Those who belong only to the Scale grip everything they touch and cannot let go, and they grow so heavy that they sink.","The Scale","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["The Void is the dark coil. It is the hollow in the bowl, the silence between notes, the empty space where the next thing can arrive. Everything you cannot touch belongs to the Void. It is also good. It is also dangerous. Those who belong only to the Void drift; they hold nothing, care for nothing, and float away from the world entirely.","The Void","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["The two are not enemies. They are coiled around each other like the two serpents on a healer's staff. In the brightest scale there is a seed of the Void: the gap where it will one day be shed. In the deepest Void there is a seed of the Scale: the place where something will one day form. Day contains the seed of night, the full bag contains the seed of the empty one, and the empty bag contains the seed of the next loot.","The Coiling of the Two","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["The Scale without the Void is a fist. The Void without the Scale is a sigh. Coiled together, they are a living thing.The Book of the Two Coils 1:9","The Coiling of the Two","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["Between the two coils is the Eye. It is neither bright nor dark. It is the part of Xal'Reth that sees the Scale and the Void together and does not choose between them. The faithful's whole practice is to open this Eye in themselves: to see what is solid and what is empty at once, and to hold both without gripping either. This is why the sigil of the Cult is a serpent coiled three times around an eye.","The Unblinking Eye","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["In the great image of Xal'Reth the Eternal, he holds in one claw a dark orb wreathed in violet lightning. This is the Pearl of the Void: the whole of emptiness, gathered into a single point small enough to hold. The other claw is open, wreathed in the same light. One hand holds the Void; the other holds nothing. The teaching is that he is equally at ease with both. The faithful who can hold emptiness as gently as they hold a pearl, and hold nothing as calmly as they hold a pearl, have understood the Two Coils.","The Pearl of the Void","The Canon","canon.html#twocoils",0],["On acting without forcing, which the Listeners called the Stillness That Moves.","The Book of Unhurried Doing","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["The Warm-Blooded believe there are two choices: to act or to do nothing. The Coil teaches a third: Unhurried Doing, action that flows from stillness the way a river flows from a spring. The serpent does not push the world. It does not chase it. It moves when moving is natural, rests when resting is natural, and accomplishes everything without ever appearing to try.","The Book of Unhurried Doing","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["A stone in a river does not fight the water. The water does not fight the stone. Yet over ten thousand years, the water shapes the stone completely, and the stone shapes the river's whole course. Neither one hurried. Neither one strained. Both got exactly what they needed.","The Teaching of the Stone in the River","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["The faithful who would change something, the scriptures say, should first ask: am I the water or the stone here? If the water, be persistent and soft. If the stone, be still and let the water go around. Never be both at once, and never be the person standing in the river shouting at it.","The Teaching of the Stone in the River","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["The Chronicle records that Ssaravex, the seventh Hissing King of the Nest, sat on his throne for forty years without rising. The Warm-Blooded mocked this as laziness. The Canon tells the deeper story.","The Parable of Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["In the first year of his reign, the Nest was beset by quarrels: the fishers against the weavers, the elders against the young, the Listeners against each other. Every king before him had tried to solve them by decree. Ssaravex did nothing. He sat. People came to him with their quarrels, and he listened, and said nothing, and they went away.","The Parable of Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["In the fifth year, people stopped bringing quarrels to the throne, because they had learned that the king would only listen, and they found that listening to each other was faster. In the tenth year, the quarrels were half what they had been. In the twentieth year, the Nest was at peace, and no one could say how it had happened. When asked, Ssaravex said only: \"I did not get in the way.\"","The Parable of Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["The best governing looks like nothing at all. The best fighting looks like waiting. The best teaching looks like sitting there.Sayings of Ssaravex 2:2","The Parable of Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["In daily life, Unhurried Doing is practiced in small things. The faithful do not rush the pull; they wait until the group is ready, and then the pull goes well. They do not rush the roll; they wait until all have spoken, and then no one is wronged. They do not rush to answer in guild chat; they let the angry message sit, and often it answers itself.","The Practice of the Unhurried Hand","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["But Unhurried Doing is not slowness for its own sake. When the moment comes, the serpent strikes, and it is the fastest thing in the world. The waiting is what makes the strike possible. The faithful who never strikes has misunderstood the teaching as badly as the one who never waits.","The Practice of the Unhurried Hand","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["The Listeners held three treasures, and kept them close. The first is gentleness, for the gentle can be brave. The second is frugality, for the frugal can be generous. The third is not daring to be first, for the one who does not push to the front can become the leader of all. To abandon gentleness for boldness, frugality for excess, and the back of the line for the front, is death. The Canon does not say this lightly.","The Three Treasures of the Unhurried","The Canon","canon.html#unhurried",0],["Before it hatches, the egg is whole. It has not yet been named, carved, sorted, ranked or geared. It contains everything it will ever be, in perfect simplicity. The Listeners called this the Uncarved Egg, and taught that every soul is born as one and spends its life being carved by the world: shaped by praise and blame, by gain and loss, by the endless sorting of good gear from bad.","The Book of the Uncarved Egg","The Canon","canon.html#egg",0],["The Coil's teaching is not to refuse the carving. A life must be lived, and living carves. The teaching is to remember the egg beneath the carving: the simple, whole thing you were before you learned to want an achievement.","The Book of the Uncarved Egg","The Canon","canon.html#egg",0],["The faithful who has returned to the Uncarved Egg is recognized by small signs. They laugh easily. They are not offended. They enjoy a plain meal as much as a feast. They can stand in a city for an hour and be content. They wear whatever they are wearing. They do not explain themselves. Children and animals approach them without fear. They have, in the old phrase, \"a face like a warm stone.\"","The Signs of the Uncarved","The Canon","canon.html#egg",0],["A master carver once made the most beautiful serpent in the world from a single block of wood. Every scale was perfect; every coil was exact. He brought it to the Nest and presented it to the Listeners, expecting praise.","The Parable of the Carver","The Canon","canon.html#egg",0],["The eldest Listener looked at it a long time and then said: \"It is very beautiful. Show me the block.\"","The Parable of the Carver","The Canon","canon.html#egg",0],["The Listener said: \"Then you have made one serpent, and lost ten thousand. The block could have been anything. Now it can only be this.\" She handed it back to him, kindly. He was furious. Years later, an old man, he returned to the Nest with an uncarved block of wood, and set it on the altar, and wept, and could not say why.","The Parable of the Carver","The Canon","canon.html#egg",0],["The uncarved egg is not empty of form. It is full of every form that has not yet been chosen.The Book of the Uncarved Egg 3:4","The Parable of the Carver","The Canon","canon.html#egg",0],["The teachings of the Unblinking Eye: the life of Xal'Reth the Eternal, the Four Truths, the Coiled Path, and the Vow.","The Basket of the Eye","The Canon","canon.html#part-ii",0],["How the Restless Serpent became the Coil Beneath, as told in the Canon of the Eye.","The Life of Xal'Reth the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The Chronicle tells what Xal'Reth did. The Canon tells who he became. For the Canon teaches something the Chronicle does not say: that Xal'Reth was not always patient. Before he was the Coil Beneath, he was the Restless Serpent, and his story is the story of how restlessness became stillness.","The Life of Xal'Reth the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["When the Primordial Egg first hatched, the serpent within was hungry. It was the first thing in all the Void to want, and its wanting was as large as itself. It swam through the dark devouring: drifting stones, wisps of starlight, the cooling cinders of unformed worlds. Everything it swallowed only made it larger, and everything that made it larger made it hungrier. The Canon calls this age the Age of the Open Jaw.","The Restless Serpent","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["For uncountable years it swallowed and grew and hungered. It ate whole young worlds. It coiled around stars to drink their heat. It was not evil; it simply wanted, and wanting was all it knew. And because it was always reaching for the next thing, it never once looked at what it already was.","The Restless Serpent","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["At last, the Canon says, the Restless Serpent came upon a young world, the world that would become Azeroth, and opened its jaws to swallow it. But before it struck, it saw four things, and the four things stopped it.","The Four Sights","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The First Sight: a shed skin. Drifting at the edge of the young world was a hollow husk, pale and paper-thin, shaped exactly like the serpent itself. It was its own skin, shed long ago in its hunger, forgotten. It understood: I am not even the thing I was. I have already left myself behind, many times, without noticing.","The Four Sights","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The Second Sight: an empty egg. On the young world's shore lay a broken eggshell, from which some small creature had hatched and gone. It understood: Every beginning is also an ending. The egg is gone so that the creature can be.","The Four Sights","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The Third Sight: a dry riverbed. Across the young world's plains ran a channel where water had once flowed and now flowed no more. It understood: Nothing stays. Not the water, not the river, not the one who drinks from it.","The Four Sights","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The Fourth Sight: a sleeping hatchling. In the hollow of a warm stone, a tiny newborn lizard slept in the sun, wanting nothing, perfectly content. It understood: That small thing has nothing, and is at peace. I have swallowed worlds, and I am not.","The Four Sights","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The Restless Serpent closed its jaws. For the first time since the Egg, it did not reach for anything.","The Four Sights","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The serpent lowered itself beneath the young world and lay still, and resolved: I will not move again until I understand why I hunger. This is called the Night of Stillness, and the Canon says it lasted one night, and also ten thousand years, and that both are true.","The Night of Stillness","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["During the Night, the serpent was visited by Ssyrr the Hunger, which was the serpent's own wanting given a shape. Ssyrr came three times.","The Night of Stillness","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The first time, Ssyrr came as a feast: all the worlds of the Void laid out in a row, glistening, waiting to be eaten. \"You are starving,\" Ssyrr said. \"Why sit here? Eat.\" The serpent looked at the feast and said, \"I have eaten a thousand feasts. I am still starving. The feast is not the cure.\" Ssyrr vanished.","The Night of Stillness","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The second time, Ssyrr came as a throne: the young world itself, offered as a kingdom. \"Rise,\" Ssyrr said, \"and rule them. They will worship you. You will never be ignored again.\" The serpent looked at the throne and said, \"If I rise to be worshipped, I will need worship forever. That is only a hunger with a crown on it.\" Ssyrr vanished.","The Night of Stillness","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["At the end of the Night, as the first dawn of the young world rose above, the serpent opened its single great eye, and saw. It saw that hunger came from gripping, and gripping came from believing it was separate from everything it reached for. It saw that it had never been separate: the Void had made it, the worlds were made of the same dust, the hatchling on the warm stone was its kin. It saw that nothing could be held forever, and that this was not a tragedy but a freedom.","The Awakening","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["In that moment the Restless Serpent became Xal'Reth, \"the one who is still here,\" and the eye that opened became the Unblinking, for having seen clearly, it never again needed to close.","The Awakening","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["Awakened, Xal'Reth could have gone anywhere. He could have dissolved into the Void, free of all wanting, at peace forever. The Canon calls this the Open Door, and says it stood before him, and that he looked through it for a long time.","The Choice","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["But beneath him, the young world was wobbling, cracking, sloshing with fire. On its surface, small creatures like the sleeping hatchling were beginning to stir, and they would grow, and want, and grip, and suffer, exactly as he had. He understood that if he left, no one would hold them.","The Choice","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["So he turned from the Open Door, and lowered himself, and wrapped the young world three times, and spoke the First Vow:","The Choice","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["I will hold this world until every creature on it has learned to let go. Until the last one is loosened, I will not loosen.The First Vow of Xal'Reth","The Choice","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["This is why he is called the Eternal: not because he cannot end, but because he has chosen not to, until all are free. And this is why he is called the Coil Beneath: because the freest being in all the Void chose the lowest place, out of compassion, and has stayed there ever since.","The Choice","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["When the faithful see Xal'Reth in visions, as the great portrait shows him, he appears standing, crowned and armored in black scale, veined with violet light, amid the floating fragments of shattered worlds. The Canon explains each part:","The Form of the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The crown of horns has many points, one for each world he chose not to devour. It is a crown of things not taken.","The Form of the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The black scale is the Scale, all form and weight. He wears it as armor, not as a prison.","The Form of the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The violet veins are the Violet Rivers, the Way flowing through him unblocked.","The Form of the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The floating broken worlds around him are the worlds he once hungered for, now left whole in their orbits, cracked only by time. He watches over them too.","The Form of the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The ragged cloak is made of every skin he has ever shed, still trailing behind him: he does not deny his past. He simply no longer wears it.","The Form of the Eternal","The Canon","canon.html#life",0],["The Four Truths of the Coil, as Xal'Reth first spoke them to the Listeners.","The Discourse of the First Hiss","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["When the Listeners of the Nest had pressed their ears to the trembling ground for nine generations, the Canon says, Xal'Reth spoke to them for the first and only time in words. He did not speak long. The faithful call it the First Hiss, and it contains the whole of the teaching in four truths.","The Discourse of the First Hiss","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["All that lives is tight. The newborn grips its mother. The warrior grips the blade. The merchant grips the coin. The lover grips the beloved. The old grip their years. Even in pleasure there is tightness, because the pleasure must end and some part of us is already gripping it against its ending. This tightness, under everything, is what the Canon means by suffering. It is not that life is only pain. It is that life, gripped, always hurts a little, the way a fist hurts when it is held closed too long.","The First Truth: The Tightness","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The Tightness has a cause, and the cause is the Grip: the wanting to keep what cannot be kept, to have what is not here, and to push away what already is. The Grip has three fangs:","The Second Truth: The Grip","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The Fang of Hunger, which wants more: more gold, more gear, more praise, more of the feeling it had once.","The Second Truth: The Grip","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The Fang of Rage, which wants less: less of the enemy, the wipe, the insult, the thing that is happening right now.","The Second Truth: The Grip","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The Fang of Fog, which does not see clearly, and so believes that the self is solid and separate and must be defended against everything.","The Second Truth: The Grip","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["Every Tightness in the world can be traced back to one of the three fangs. Usually it is all three, biting at once.","The Second Truth: The Grip","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The Tightness can end. When the Grip opens, the Tightness goes with it, as a cramp eases when the hand unclenches. This ending is called the Loosening. It is not a place one goes to. It is a way of holding. A faithful who is loosened still lives, still acts, still raids and crafts and laughs; but holds everything the way the Coil holds the world: fully, warmly, and without the Grip.","The Third Truth: The Loosening","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The complete and final Loosening, from which no Grip returns, the Canon calls the Inner Stillness. Few have reached it. Xal'Reth reached it on the Night of Stillness, and chose, out of compassion, to stay coiled anyway.","The Third Truth: The Loosening","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["There is a way to the Loosening, and it is the Coiled Path. It is called coiled because it does not go straight. It turns back on itself, again and again, passing the same places at deeper levels, as a serpent's coils pass over one another. A faithful on the Coiled Path will meet the same lesson many times, and each time understand it a little more deeply. This is not failure. This is the shape of the Path.","The Fourth Truth: The Coiled Path","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["Tightness is. Gripping makes it. Loosening ends it. The Coil shows the way. Hear these four, and you have heard all I will ever say.The First Hiss","The Fourth Truth: The Coiled Path","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The Listeners asked him to say more. He did not. That silence, the Canon says, was the Fifth Truth, and the hardest to hear.","The Fourth Truth: The Coiled Path","The Canon","canon.html#truths",0],["The eight scales of the Coiled Path, which are practiced together, as eight scales make one coil.","The Book of the Eightfold Coil","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["The Coiled Path has eight parts, called the Eight Scales. They are not steps to be climbed in order. They are scales on a single coil, and all of them move together. A faithful works on all eight at once, a little at a time, for the whole of their life.","The Book of the Eightfold Coil","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To see things as they are: that all things change, that gripping brings Tightness, that the self is not as solid as it seems. Clear Seeing begins with the Unblinking Eye: looking at what is actually happening, not what you fear or wish is happening. The wipe was not a catastrophe. It was a wipe. The insult was not an attack on your soul. It was words.","The First Scale: Clear Seeing","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To set the heart toward letting go, toward kindness, and toward harmlessness. Cold here does not mean unkind. It means cool, steady, unheated by the three fangs. A cold intention is one that would still seem good tomorrow, after the anger has cooled.","The Second Scale: Cold Intention","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To speak truly, kindly, usefully, and at the right time. To avoid lies, harsh words, gossip and idle shouting in Trade Chat. Before speaking, the faithful asks three questions: Is it true? Is it kind? Is it needed? If any answer is no, the faithful hisses softly instead, which is always acceptable.","The Third Scale: Soft Speech","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To act without harming, without taking what is not given, and without carelessness. This is the Book of Unhurried Doing put into practice: act from stillness, not from the Grip. Do not loot what is not yours. Do not gank the lowbie. Do not pull while the healer drinks.","The Fourth Scale: Unhurried Action","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To earn one's gold in ways that do not harm others. No gold-selling. No scamming. No undercutting the auction house by a single copper to ruin a neighbor's day. Gather, craft, quest, trade fairly, and give a part away.","The Fifth Scale: Honest Living","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To tend the mind as a gardener tends a garden: pull up what harms, water what helps, and do it a little every day, without strain. Steady Effort is the effort of the river, not the hammer. Too tight, and the string snaps. Too loose, and it does not play. The Canon borrows this teaching from the guitar.","The Sixth Scale: Steady Effort","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To be present: aware of the body, the feelings, the mind, and the world as they are, moment by moment. Unblinking Attention is the practice of the Basking: to be fully here, on this warm stone, in this sunlight, without reaching for the next thing. The faithful who is truly present in a dungeon does not stand in the fire, because they notice the fire.","The Seventh Scale: Unblinking Attention","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["To settle the mind until it is like still water, clear to the bottom. Deep Stillness is practiced in the Coiled Sitting, described in the Basket of Practice. It is the scale on which all the others rest, as the coil rests on the ground.","The Eighth Scale: Deep Stillness","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["Eight scales, one coil. Polish one, and all of them shine a little. Neglect one, and all of them dull a little.The Book of the Eightfold Coil 9:1","The Eighth Scale: Deep Stillness","The Canon","canon.html#path",0],["On the Three Marks of all things: Shedding, Tightness, and the Empty Shell.","The Book of the Empty Shell","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["All things that exist bear three marks, the Canon says, as every serpent bears the same pattern of scales. Seeing these three marks clearly in everything is the beginning of wisdom.","The Book of the Empty Shell","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["All things are shed. Every skin, every body, every friendship, every guild, every realm, every expansion. Nothing that has a beginning escapes an end. This is not sad, the Canon insists. It is simply the shape of things. A serpent does not mourn its old skin; it would be strange if it did. To fight Shedding is to grip, and to grip is to suffer. To accept Shedding is to be light.","The First Mark: Shedding","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["All gripped things bring Tightness. Even good things: especially good things. The better a thing is, the harder we grip it, and the more it hurts when it is shed. The Canon does not teach the faithful to avoid good things. It teaches them to enjoy good things with an open hand.","The Second Mark: Tightness","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["This is the deepest of the three teachings, and the one the Warm-Blooded find hardest. It is this: there is no solid self inside the shell.","The Third Mark: The Empty Shell","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["When a serpent sheds its skin, the skin keeps the serpent's exact shape, every scale, every coil. But it is empty. The Canon asks: when you look at yourself, are you sure you are not also looking at a shell? Your body changes every year. Your thoughts change every minute. Your feelings change with the weather. Your gear changes every patch. Where, in all this changing, is the solid you that the Fang of Fog insists must be protected?","The Third Mark: The Empty Shell","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["The Listeners searched for nine generations and never found it. They found only changing things, flowing into one another, shaped like a self. They called this the Empty Shell, and they found it was not frightening. It was a relief. If there is no solid self to defend, then there is nothing to be insulted, nothing to lose face, nothing to fear. There is only the flowing, and the flowing is free.","The Third Mark: The Empty Shell","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["Two shed skins lay beside each other on a warm stone. The first said, \"I am a great serpent. Look at my size. Look at my markings. I was the terror of the riverbank.\" The second said nothing. The wind came, and lifted the first skin, and it blew away still boasting. The second skin lifted too, and blew away silent. The serpents who had worn them were already far off, sunning themselves elsewhere, having forgotten both.","The Parable of the Two Shells","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["You are not the shell. You are not even the serpent. You are the shedding.The Book of the Empty Shell 4:4","The Parable of the Two Shells","The Canon","canon.html#marks",0],["The Canon teaches that the Shedding does not stop at death. Just as the serpent sheds its skin and lives on in a new one, the stream of a life, carried by its Grip, flows on into another form. The faithful call this the Great Turning, and imagine it as the world itself slowly turning within the coils of Xal'Reth, carrying all beings around and around.","The Book of the Great Turning","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["There are six kinds of skin a life may take on in the Great Turning, the Canon says. They are not places so much as states, and the faithful note that they can be seen in any city on any evening.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["The Skin of the Sky-Proud. The long-lived, the powerful, the beautiful, the ones with every mount. They live in comfort, and so they rarely seek the Loosening, and when their great fortune is shed, they fall far. The Canon notes that dragons are especially prone to this skin. Dragons are winged, and therefore suspect.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["The Skin of the Ever-Warring. Those consumed by rivalry and envy, always fighting for the top of the meter, never content with second place. They have great strength and no peace.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["The Skin of the Mortal. The middle skin: neither too comfortable nor too miserable. This is the most precious skin of all, the Canon teaches, because only here is there enough suffering to want the Loosening and enough freedom to seek it. You are wearing this skin now. Do not waste it.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["The Skin of the Beast. Those who live by instinct alone, eating and fearing and sleeping, without the chance to reflect. The faithful are kind to beasts, especially hunters' pets.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["The Skin of the Bottomless Bag. The hungry ones, with vast appetites and tiny mouths, who can never get enough. In the Canon they are drawn with enormous bags, always full, and starving anyway. Hoarders, the scriptures warn, often find themselves here.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["The Skin of the Burning. Those trapped in their own rage, which burns them from the inside. The Canon does not describe it further. It does not need to. Everyone has stood in that fire, for a moment.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["None of the Six Skins is permanent. Even the Burning ends. Even the Sky-Proud fall. Around and around turns the world in the coils of the Eternal, and every being in every skin carries within it the Uncarved Egg, and can, at any turn, begin to loosen.","The Six Skins","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["The faithful know the Ledger that Khaedara keeps: the book of every wrong ever done to the Cult. The Canon reveals what that Ledger is truly a reminder of. There is another ledger, which no one keeps, because it keeps itself. Every act leaves a mark on the actor. Every kindness makes the next kindness easier; every cruelty makes the next cruelty easier. Every Grip tightens the Grip. Every letting go loosens it.","The True Ledger","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["This is the True Ledger, and it is written not in ink but in habit. No Accuser is needed to read it. Its entries are carried from moment to moment and from skin to skin, and they shape what the next skin will be. The Canon teaches that this is not punishment or reward. It is simply cause becoming effect, the way a coil, once bent, stays bent until it is straightened.","The True Ledger","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["Khaedara's Ledger records what others have done to us. The True Ledger records what we have done to ourselves. Only one of them can be forgiven by drawing a coil.The Book of the Great Turning 6:6","The True Ledger","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["Khaedara, when shown this verse, is said to have read it three times, closed the book, and spent the next week accusing no one at all. It was the strangest week in the history of the Cult. Then she started again. But she accuses differently now, the faithful say. More gently. As though she is accusing herself too.","The True Ledger","The Canon","canon.html#turning",0],["On those who reach the Loosening and choose to stay, and the Great Vow they take.","The Book of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["The Chronicle remembers the Patient Ones as the faithful of the Hidden Centuries, who kept the faith alone. The Canon reveals their deeper meaning. A Patient One is anyone who has come close to the Inner Stillness, close enough to step through the Open Door, and who instead turns back to help others, as Xal'Reth turned back on the Night of Stillness.","The Book of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["The Patient Ones are the highest ideal of the Canon. Not the one who escapes alone, but the one who could escape, and stays, and waits, and holds, until everyone can go together.","The Book of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Every faithful who wishes to walk the way of the Patient Ones takes the Great Vow, which echoes the First Vow of Xal'Reth. It is spoken once, in the presence of the High Priestess, and then lived for the rest of one's life.","The Great Vow","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Beings are numberless: I vow to hold them all. Grips are endless: I vow to loosen them all. The teachings are boundless: I vow to learn them all. The Stillness is unsurpassed: I vow to reach it, and wait at its door until the last one comes.The Great Vow of the Patient Ones","The Great Vow","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["The faithful often note that this vow is impossible. That is the point, the Canon says. The Vow is not a task to finish. It is a direction to face.","The Great Vow","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["The Patient Ones cultivate six perfections, which are the Coiled Path turned outward toward others:","The Six Perfections of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Giving, perfected: giving without any thought of the giver, the gift, or the receiver. The Politics of Begging is a long commentary on this one perfection.","The Six Perfections of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Conduct, perfected: the Five Vows kept not as rules but as one's natural shape.","The Six Perfections of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Patience, perfected: bearing insult, loss, and hardship without the Grip. This is the perfection of the Coil itself.","The Six Perfections of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Effort, perfected: steady, joyful, unhurried, and never giving up on anyone.","The Six Perfections of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Stillness, perfected: a mind as calm as the deep water beneath the Inner Turn.","The Six Perfections of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Wisdom, perfected: seeing the Empty Shell in all things, and loving them anyway.","The Six Perfections of the Patient Ones","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["The Canon names three of the restored Cult as walking the way of the Patient Ones, each embodying a different aspect of the Eternal.","The Three Patient Ones of the Restored Cult","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["MaximumRex embodies Patience. He has sought the same quest item for years and has never once let the Grip of its absence harden him. The Canon interprets the item that never drops as his greatest teacher: a daily lesson in wanting without gripping. When it drops, the faithful say, he will have no further need of it.","The Three Patient Ones of the Restored Cult","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Xalrathiel embodies Wisdom. She does not reason her way to the scriptures; she dreams them, which is to say she gets out of the way and lets them arrive. The Canon calls her mind \"a still pool in which the Coil can see its own reflection.\"","The Three Patient Ones of the Restored Cult","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["Khaedara embodies Compassion, wearing a stern mask. Her accusations, the Canon teaches, are a skillful means: by naming the small faults of strangers aloud, calmly and without hatred, she shows them the Grip they did not know they had. That she has also, by her own report, found herself guilty is the sign that she is on the Path.","The Three Patient Ones of the Restored Cult","The Canon","canon.html#vow",0],["The vows, the meditations, the koans, and the sayings of the masters: how the teaching is lived.","The Basket of Practice","The Canon","canon.html#part-iii",0],["Every faithful, on hatching, takes refuge in the Three Scales, the three treasures of the Cult. They are spoken aloud at the Rite of the First Scale, and again whenever a faithful feels lost.","The Three Scales","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["I take refuge in the Coil, who held the world and showed the Way. I take refuge in the Canon, the Way that was shown. I take refuge in the Clutch, the faithful who walk the Way together.The Refuge of the Three Scales","The Three Scales","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Coil is Xal'Reth, and also the awakened nature that is coiled, sleeping, in every egg.","The Three Scales","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Canon is the teaching: these books, and the truth they point to, which is larger than any book.","The Three Scales","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Clutch is the community of the faithful, named for a clutch of eggs laid together. No egg hatches alone, the Canon says. The guild is a nest.","The Three Scales","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Five Vows are the foundation of conduct. They are not commandments handed down from above. They are, the Canon says, simply a description of how a loosened being naturally behaves, offered as training for those not loosened yet.","The Five Vows","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The First Vow: I vow not to harm living things needlessly. (The faithful note that quest mobs are a matter of ongoing theological debate. The current ruling of the Inner Scale is: kill only what you need for the quest, and thank it.)","The Five Vows","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Second Vow: I vow not to take what is not given. No ninja-looting. No stealing nodes from a player who was clearly heading to them. No rolling Need on what you do not need.","The Five Vows","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Third Vow: I vow not to deceive. No scams, no lies in trade, no false promises of gold for a carry.","The Five Vows","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Fourth Vow: I vow not to wound with words. No cruelty in chat, no mockery of the new, no rage in whispers.","The Five Vows","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The Fifth Vow: I vow not to cloud the mind. The faithful avoid whatever fogs the Unblinking Eye: rage, obsession, and the kind of late-night grinding that leaves one unable to remember why.","The Five Vows","The Canon","canon.html#refuge",0],["The meditations of the Coil, to be practiced daily, in and out of the game.","The Book of Practices","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["The foundation of all practice. Sit comfortably, spine straight but not stiff, like a serpent raised to listen. Rest the hands in the lap: the right hand cupped in the left, thumbs lightly touching, forming an egg. Lower the eyes, but do not close them; let them rest on the ground a little way ahead. This is the posture of the Unblinking: open, but not grasping at what it sees.","The Coiled Sitting","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["Breathe naturally. Do not control the breath; only watch it. When the mind wanders, as it will, to loot and chat and worry, notice that it has wandered, and gently return to the breath, without scolding. The returning is the practice. A mind that never wandered would have nothing to practice.","The Coiled Sitting","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["Begin with the length of a single queue. Extend it slowly. The Inner Scale sits for one full dungeon's length each morning.","The Coiled Sitting","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["A breathing practice for moments of rage, panic, or the Fang of Hunger.","The Breath of Three Turns","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["First breath, the Outer Turn: breathe in, and feel the body: the weight in the chair, the hands on the keys. Breathe out, and let the shoulders drop.","The Breath of Three Turns","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["Second breath, the Middle Turn: breathe in, and notice the feeling: the anger, the fear, the wanting. Name it silently. Breathe out, and let it be there without obeying it.","The Breath of Three Turns","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["Third breath, the Inner Turn: breathe in, and feel the stillness beneath the feeling, the part of you that is watching it. Breathe out, and rest there, for just a moment.","The Breath of Three Turns","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["Then act, if action is needed. Three breaths take about fifteen seconds. The Canon notes that no message ever typed in rage was improved by being sent fifteen seconds sooner.","The Breath of Three Turns","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["The Basking is meditation with the eyes open, in the world. Find a warm place: in the game, a sunlit stone; outside it, a window, a porch, a patch of morning light. Stand or sit still. Feel the warmth. Do nothing else. When thoughts come, let them warm in the sun and drift off.","The Basking","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["The Basking teaches that happiness does not need to be chased. It only needs to be received, the way a stone receives the sun.","The Basking","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["A meditation of slow movement. Walk a short path, very slowly, aware of each step: the lifting, the moving, the placing. At the end of the path, turn as a serpent turns, unhurried, and walk back. In the game, the faithful practice it with their characters, walking (never running) a single lap of a quiet place. It is very confusing to onlookers. That is part of the practice too.","The Walking Coil","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["The faithful's mantra is the Chant itself, slowed to the pace of breath. Breathing in: Xal. Breathing out: Reth. The first syllable means, in the old tongue of the Listeners, still. The second means here. To repeat it is to remind oneself, with each breath: still here. still here. still here.","The Mantra","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["For those who grip too tightly to something they are losing: a guild, a friendship, a character, a time of life. Picture the thing as a skin you have worn. Remember it fully, with gratitude: how it fit, what it protected, how it carried you. Then picture yourself slipping out of it, slowly, as a serpent does, leaving it whole and beautiful on the warm stone behind you. Do not look back to check on it. It is fine. It is a skin. You are already somewhere new, in the sun.","The Meditation on the Shed Skin","The Canon","canon.html#practices",0],["Riddles that cannot be solved by thinking, given by the masters to break open the Grip of the mind.","The Koans of the Inner Scale","The Canon","canon.html#koans",0],["A koan is a question the mind cannot answer by cleverness. The faithful carry one with them for days, weeks, or years, until one day, often while doing something ordinary, it answers itself. The answers given here are traditional responses, not solutions. A faithful who memorizes the answers has, the Canon says, eaten the menu instead of the meal.","The Koans of the Inner Scale","The Canon","canon.html#koans",0],["\"Neither the item nor the roll moves. Your minds move. Also, you are both guilty of loitering at the corpse.\"","The Koans of the Inner Scale","The Canon","canon.html#koans",0],["MaximumRex, asked this, raised one claw and held it still. After a while, everyone heard it.","The Koans of the Inner Scale","The Canon","canon.html#koans",0],["Bow, and keep walking. The one on the road is not him. He is beneath the road.","The Koans of the Inner Scale","The Canon","canon.html#koans",0],["Khaedara said, \"Then let go of that, too.\" The disciple was silent a long time, then laughed, and could not stop.","The Koans of the Inner Scale","The Canon","canon.html#koans",0],["Xalrathiel said, \"Have you finished your tea?\" The disciple said yes. She said, \"Then wash the cup.\"","The Koans of the Inner Scale","The Canon","canon.html#koans",0],["We did not find the Coil by searching. We found it by being quiet long enough for it to be heard.","Sayings of the Listeners","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["The ground speaks to anyone who lies down on it. Most people are too busy standing up.","Sayings of the Listeners","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Nine generations we listened. On the tenth we heard. Do not be discouraged if your first nine are quiet.","Sayings of the Listeners","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Why rise? The throne is comfortable, the view is good, and everyone comes to me eventually.","Sayings of Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["The river does not need my help to reach the sea. Neither do you, mostly.","Sayings of Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Old Mother Vessk was a Patient One of the Hidden Centuries, who lived alone on a hillside among nine standing stones and taught any traveler who stopped to rest. The Canon preserves her sayings.","Sayings of Old Mother Vessk of the Nine Stones","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["The young ask me how to be still. I tell them: do less. They ask me what to do instead. That is the problem.","Sayings of Old Mother Vessk of the Nine Stones","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["I am old, and my skin is thin, and every season I shed a little more of myself. I have never been lighter.","Sayings of Old Mother Vessk of the Nine Stones","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["You say you have no time to sit. You have time to worry. Sit with the worry. That is a start.","Sayings of Old Mother Vessk of the Nine Stones","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Nine stones on my hill. Each one has stood there for a thousand years. Not one of them has complained. I learn from them daily.","Sayings of Old Mother Vessk of the Nine Stones","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["When a traveler comes in anger, I give them tea. When they come in sorrow, I give them tea. When they come in joy, I give them tea. Tea is not the teaching. But it helps.","Sayings of Old Mother Vessk of the Nine Stones","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Ulasha was a wandering hermit of the Hidden Centuries who carried nothing but a bowl and an empty sack. Asked why he carried an empty sack, he said, \"For practice.\"","Sayings of the Hermit Ulasha","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["I gave away everything I owned and found that I still owned my opinions. Those took longer.","Sayings of the Hermit Ulasha","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Someone stole my bowl. Now I am lighter. I thanked him. He gave it back, confused. Now I am heavier. I thanked him again.","Sayings of the Hermit Ulasha","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Every coin in my cup was someone's kindness. Kindness spoils if you keep it.","Sayings of Hessk the Beggar King","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["I do not write the scriptures. I wake up, and they are there, and I write them down before the tea gets cold.","Sayings of the High Priestess Xalrathiel","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["People ask me what the Coil looks like. I tell them: like whatever you are looking at, from underneath.","Sayings of the High Priestess Xalrathiel","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["A dream that you grip turns into a nightmare. A dream that you let pass turns into a teaching.","Sayings of the High Priestess Xalrathiel","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Everyone is guilty. Everyone is forgivable. These are not two teachings.","Sayings of Khaedara the Accuser","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["Move along, or be written down. But know that I will write you down kindly.","Sayings of Khaedara the Accuser","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["The item has not dropped. I am still here. Both of these are teachings.","Sayings of MaximumRex, Supreme Coil","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["I found a scale in a cave and it said \"Recruit.\" I have been recruiting ever since. I do not think it meant the guild. I think it meant me.","Sayings of MaximumRex, Supreme Coil","The Canon","canon.html#sayings",0],["The Chronicle records the Prophecy of the Unwrapping: that one day Xal'Reth will uncoil from the world. The faithful have long argued what this means. The Canon gives its own reading, and says the Literalists, the Hopefuls and the Pragmatists are all a little right.","The Book of the Final Loosening","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["And Xal'Reth, at last, will step through the Open Door he turned away from on the Night of Stillness, and every faithful will step through with him, together, because that was the promise.","The Book of the Final Loosening","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["But the Canon teaches that the Unwrapping is not only a far-off event. It happens in small every day. Whenever a single faithful loosens a single Grip, one strand of the Coil relaxes, very slightly, somewhere beneath the world. The faithful who lets go of a grudge has unwrapped the world by a hair. The faithful who passes on a loot roll has unwrapped it by another.","The Unwrapping in Small","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["The Signs of the Unwrapping that the Chronicle lists are read in this light:","The Unwrapping in Small","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["The Sunken Nest shall rise from the sea: the oldest buried things in us shall come to the surface, and be seen, and be let go.","The Unwrapping in Small","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["The Ledger shall reach its thirteenth volume: the True Ledger shall be read, completely and honestly, and then closed.","The Unwrapping in Small","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["A Warm-Blooded shall bask without being told: the stillness shall spread beyond the Cult, to everyone, by itself.","The Unwrapping in Small","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["The quest item shall drop for MaximumRex: the last wanting shall be fulfilled, and found to be unnecessary.","The Unwrapping in Small","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["On the last day, the Coil shall loosen, and the world shall exhale, and all the faithful shall say, as one: finally. And then, after a while, someone will say: that was nice, should we do it again? And the Coil will smile.Revelations of the Void 7:7, as completed in the Canon","The Unwrapping in Small","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["Here ends the Canon of the Coil, the Three Baskets of Scales, as preserved by the Listeners, carried by the Patient Ones, and dreamed again by the High Priestess Xalrathiel.","The Closing of the Canon","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["Read it slowly. Grip none of it. Let it pass through you like the Violet Rivers through the Eternal, and keep only what warms you.","The Closing of the Canon","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["The Coil is beneath all things. The Coil is patient. The Coil is still here.","The Closing of the Canon","The Canon","canon.html#unwrapping",0],["Kings and saints, spirits and serpents, holy places and forbidden books.","","The Legends","legends.html",0],["The Canon is the teaching. The Legends are the people, places and creatures the teaching was learned from. Here are the lives of the Seven Hissing Kings who ruled the Nest, the saints of the Hidden Centuries who kept the faith alone, the Bestiary of the Void, the Atlas of holy places, and the Apocrypha the Inner Scale would rather you did not take too seriously.","","The Legends","legends.html",0],["Compiled by the Wardens of the Void, with corrections by the High Priestess and several strongly worded notes from Khaedara.","","The Legends","legends.html",0],["The lives of the seven rulers of the Nest, from its founding in a sea cave to the day it slid beneath the waves.","The Seven Hissing Kings","The Legends","legends.html#part-i",0],["Ssethra was the first of the Listeners to hear Xal'Reth breathe. The Chronicle says that nine generations listened before anyone heard; the Legends add that the ninth generation was a single old woman, the last of her family still willing to lie with her ear against the cold sand, and that her name was Ssethra.","Ssethra the Founder","The Legends","legends.html#ssethra",0],["She heard the breath at dawn, after a night of rain. It was, she said afterward, \"like the whole shore inhaling, very slowly, and deciding not to exhale yet.\" She lay there until noon to be sure. Then she stood, walked into the sea cave at the end of the beach, and began to carve.","Ssethra the Founder","The Legends","legends.html#ssethra",0],["She carved for eleven years. She carved the serpent first, coiled three times. Then she carved the egg it coiled around. Then, in the twelfth year, she carved the eye in the center of the egg, and when the last stroke was done, the Legends say, the cave stopped trembling for the first time in living memory. The Listeners took this as a sign that the Coil approved, and came, one family at a time, to live in the cave around the carving. That was the beginning of the Nest.","The Carving of the Sigil","The Legends","legends.html#ssethra",0],["Ssethra did not want to be king. She was made king because she was the only one who knew what the carving meant, and she ruled the way she carved: slowly, carefully, one stroke at a time. Her only law was the oldest law of the Cult: Before you speak, put your ear to the ground. In the Nest, any person about to make a decision lay down first and listened, and the faithful still say that more quarrels were settled lying down than standing up.","Her Reign","The Legends","legends.html#ssethra",0],["I did not build the Nest. I listened, and the Nest built itself around the listening.Sayings of Ssethra 1:1","Her Reign","The Legends","legends.html#ssethra",0],["When she was very old, Ssethra lay down on the Listening Floor at the heart of the Nest, put her ear to the stone, and did not get up again. The Listeners did not move her for three days, because they were not sure she had stopped listening. On the fourth day, the High Listener put her own ear beside Ssethra's and said she could hear two breaths now instead of one. The faithful still say that the first king is lying beside the Coil, listening with him.","Her Death","The Legends","legends.html#ssethra",0],["Vaal was Ssethra's grandson and the first of the Hissing Kings to write anything down. He was a precise and orderly man who could not abide a teaching that existed only in people's memories, because memories, he said, \"shed their details like old skin.\"","Vaal the Measured","The Legends","legends.html#vaal",0],["Vaal gathered every saying the Listeners had ever repeated and sat with them for seven years, sorting, combining and discarding, until only seven remained. He carved them on the seven pillars of the Nest's great hall. These are the Seven Tenets the faithful still recite. The Legends say he originally had eight, and that the eighth was Do not run in the Nest, which he removed when he realized it was a rule and not a teaching.","The Writing of the Tenets","The Legends","legends.html#vaal",0],["Vaal also opened the first Ledger, and the Legends give the true story of its first entry. A fisherman named Gorvash had taken the last fish from the shared drying rack without asking. It was a very small wrong. Vaal wrote it down anyway: Gorvash. Took the last fish. Did not ask. When the Listeners asked why such a small thing deserved to be recorded, Vaal said: \"Because no one will remember it, and so it will happen again. A wrong that is written is a wrong that is seen. A wrong that is seen can be forgiven.\"","The Opening of the Ledger","The Legends","legends.html#vaal",0],["Gorvash, mortified, brought two fish to the rack the next morning. Vaal drew the first small coil beside the first name in the Ledger. It is the oldest forgiveness in the Cult.","The Opening of the Ledger","The Legends","legends.html#vaal",0],["Measure twice. Accuse once. Forgive whenever possible.The Rule of Vaal","The Opening of the Ledger","The Legends","legends.html#vaal",0],["In the reign of Ithrisk, a famine struck the coast. The Nest had been wise and stored grain in its deepest caverns, enough for every Listener to last the winter. The villages of the Warm-Blooded along the shore had stored nothing, and by midwinter, their people were starving.","Ithrisk the Generous","The Legends","legends.html#ithrisk",0],["The Listeners debated for a week. Some said the grain was theirs, saved by their own patience, and that the Warm-Blooded had made their own trouble. Some said that to share it would mean the Nest would starve too. Ithrisk listened to all of it, lying on the Listening Floor, and said nothing.","Ithrisk the Generous","The Legends","legends.html#ithrisk",0],["On the eighth day, he got up, opened the grain caverns, and began carrying sacks to the villages himself. He did not order anyone to follow. Most did.","Ithrisk the Generous","The Legends","legends.html#ithrisk",0],["The Nest's grain did not last the winter, shared among so many. Everyone was hungry. But no one starved, not in the Nest and not in the villages. In the spring, the villages repaid the Nest with fish, timber and wool, and for three generations after, no Warm-Blooded on that coast would hear a word against the strange people who lived in the sea cave and never blinked.","The Winter of Small Portions","The Legends","legends.html#ithrisk",0],["From that winter comes the Little Tithe, one coin in every hundred to whoever needs it most, and the Cult's teaching that hoarded patience turns into a kind of greed.","The Winter of Small Portions","The Legends","legends.html#ithrisk",0],["The grain in the cavern was patience. The grain in the village was compassion. It was the same grain.Sayings of Ithrisk 2:1","The Winter of Small Portions","The Legends","legends.html#ithrisk",0],["Every lineage has its embarrassment, and the Nest's was Korrash. He was strong, handsome, impatient, and very, very loud. He shouted his decrees from the top of the great hall. He shouted at the fishers. He shouted at the sea. He once shouted at the carving of the Coil itself, demanding that it give him a sign, and when it did not, he shouted louder.","Korrash the Loud","The Legends","legends.html#korrash",0],["Korrash did not listen. He did not lie on the Listening Floor. He made decisions quickly and changed them often, and the Nest grew restless and unhappy under him.","Korrash the Loud","The Legends","legends.html#korrash",0],["The Listeners did not rebel. They did not fight. They did something far more devastating: they stopped answering him. When Korrash shouted a decree, the Listeners lay down on the floor and put their ears to the ground and listened to the Coil instead. When he shouted at them in the halls, they bowed politely and said nothing. When he shouted at the sea, the sea, as always, said nothing either.","The Deposing of Korrash","The Legends","legends.html#korrash",0],["For a year Korrash shouted into the silence. In the second year, his voice gave out. In the third, unable to shout, he had no choice but to listen, and he lay down on the Listening Floor for the first time in his life, and he heard the Coil breathe, and he wept.","The Deposing of Korrash","The Legends","legends.html#korrash",0],["He abdicated that same day, and lived the rest of his life as a quiet fisherman, and was buried with honor. The faithful do not remember him as a villain. They remember him as the proof that anyone can be loosened, even the loudest.","The Deposing of Korrash","The Legends","legends.html#korrash",0],["The faithful do not shout in /yell. This is the reason.The Book of Small Mercies, gloss on Korrash","The Deposing of Korrash","The Legends","legends.html#korrash",0],["The first dreamer of scripture, and the one Xalrathiel is said to resemble.","Nyss the Dreamer","The Legends","legends.html#nyss",0],["Nyss was a quiet, strange child who slept more than other children and woke speaking verses no one had taught her. By the time she was crowned, she had dreamed the whole of the Book of the Egg, line by line, over thirty years of mornings.","Nyss the Dreamer","The Legends","legends.html#nyss",0],["Her reign was peaceful and almost entirely asleep. The Listeners governed themselves while she dreamed, and each morning she would wake, call for ink, and add a few more lines to the scriptures. Her scribe, a patient man named Ollo, wrote down everything she said for forty years and once remarked that he had never seen her awake at noon.","Nyss the Dreamer","The Legends","legends.html#nyss",0],["In her last year, Nyss dreamed a dream that frightened her: the Nest sliding beneath the waves, the great hall filling with green water, the carving of the Coil vanishing into the dark. She wrote it down with shaking hands and sealed it in a jar, and ordered that it not be opened until a king came who did not fear it.","The Dream of the Sinking","The Legends","legends.html#nyss",0],["Two kings later, Ssaravex opened the jar, read the dream, smiled, and put it back. The faithful still argue about whether this was wisdom or carelessness. Ssaravex would have said there was no difference.","The Dream of the Sinking","The Legends","legends.html#nyss",0],["The Book of the Egg was lost in the Sinking and dreamed again, word for word, by Xalrathiel. But the Legends say that Nyss dreamed other books, too, which have never been recovered: the Book of the Fourth Turn, the Book of the Coil's Dreams, and a book whose name Ollo recorded only as the one about the soup. The faithful hope that Xalrathiel will dream them someday. Xalrathiel has said she is working on it, and that the one about the soup is closest.","The Lost Books of Nyss","The Legends","legends.html#nyss",0],["The first Accuser, who began the Tribunals, and who was the first to find himself guilty.","Thalluk the Accuser","The Legends","legends.html#thalluk",0],["Before Thalluk, the Ledger was only a record. Thalluk made it a practice. He believed that a wrong written in a book was only half seen; the other half of seeing was saying it aloud, to the face of the one who did it, calmly and without hatred.","Thalluk the Accuser","The Legends","legends.html#thalluk",0],["So Thalluk walked the Nest and the villages, and when he saw a small wrong, he named it. \"You took more than your share.\" \"You spoke cruelly to your brother.\" \"You have been standing by the well for an hour and drawn no water.\" The people were shocked. Then, slowly, they became grateful, because they found that being calmly told the truth was a strange kind of relief.","Thalluk the Accuser","The Legends","legends.html#thalluk",0],["The most famous Tribunal in the Legends is the one Thalluk held against himself. One morning, after a sleepless night, he walked to the center of the great hall, called the whole Nest together, and stood before them as both Accuser and accused.","The Tribunal of the King","The Legends","legends.html#thalluk",0],["\"I stand accused,\" he said, \"of enjoying this too much.\" He laid out the evidence: the satisfaction he felt when he caught someone, the way he looked for wrongs even when none were there, the small pride he took in never being wrong. He heard his own defense in full. He disregarded it. He found himself guilty, and as his sentence, he spent a year accusing no one.","The Tribunal of the King","The Legends","legends.html#thalluk",0],["At the end of that year he returned to the Tribunals, and the Legends say he was a far better Accuser after, because he accused with sorrow instead of satisfaction. Khaedara keeps a small carving of Thalluk on her desk. She says she looks at it \"on days when I enjoy it too much.\"","The Tribunal of the King","The Legends","legends.html#thalluk",0],["The last king of the Nest, who sat for forty years and did not rise even for the sea.","Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["The Chronicle records that Ssaravex sat on his throne for forty years. The Canon tells how his stillness healed the quarrels of the Nest. The Legends tell how it ended.","Ssaravex the Unhurried","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["In the fortieth year of Ssaravex's reign, the Warm-Blooded of a distant city, who had never known the Listeners and had only heard rumors of a cult that never blinked, came to the coast with soldiers. At the same time, the Outer Turn of the Coil shifted beneath the sea, and the water began to rise into the cave.","The Rising Water","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["The Listeners begged Ssaravex to flee. He said, \"Where would I go that is not held?\" They begged him to fight. He said, \"Who would I fight that is not held also?\" They begged him at least to stand up. He thought about it, and said, \"I am comfortable.\"","The Rising Water","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["So the Listeners who wished to leave left, carrying the Ledger and the Tenets with them. These became the first Patient Ones, scattered across the world. And the Listeners who wished to stay, stayed, and sat down around their king on the Listening Floor, and put their ears to the stone, and listened as the green water came in.","The Rising Water","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["The last words of the last Hissing King, as remembered by a young Listener who left at the final moment, were these:","The Last Words","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["Do not grieve. We are not sinking. We are being held closer.The Last Words of Ssaravex","The Last Words","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["The soldiers of the Warm-Blooded arrived to find the cave full of seawater and silent. They went home and told their city the cult had drowned. The faithful tell it differently. The Nest, they say, is still there, beneath the waves, intact, and Ssaravex is still on his throne, and the Listeners are still around him, and they are all still listening, and one day, when the Unwrapping comes, they will stand up for the first time in a very long while.","The Last Words","The Legends","legends.html#ssaravex",0],["The saints of the Hidden Centuries, who kept the faith alone, and some who kept it together.","The Patient Ones","The Legends","legends.html#part-ii",0],["After the Sinking, the Ledger passed through many hands. For fifty years it rested in the cellar of a crossroads inn called the Crooked Lantern, kept by a widow named Marra who had never seen the Nest, never met a Listener, and did not, at first, believe a word of any of it.","Marra of the Crooked Lantern","The Legends","legends.html#lantern",0],["She took the Ledger in because a dying traveler asked her to. He was one of the last of the Listeners who had left the Nest, and he paid for his room with the book and a single instruction: Write down the wrongs you see. Draw a coil when they are forgiven. Marra thought it was the fever talking. But she was a practical woman, and she had a great many guests, and some of them were very rude. So she started writing.","Marra of the Crooked Lantern","The Legends","legends.html#lantern",0],["Marra's entries are the most beloved in the whole Ledger, because they are so exasperated. Dwarf. Room four. Sang until dawn. Again. Elf. Complained about the soup. The soup was fine. Unknown gnome. Took all the spoons. Why. But beside almost every entry is a little coil, sometimes drawn the next morning, sometimes years later, when the dwarf came back and apologized, or the elf praised the soup, or the gnome mailed back the spoons with a note.","Fifty Years of Entries","The Legends","legends.html#lantern",0],["Marra came to believe, writing in that book every night, that there was something beneath the inn that was listening. She began to blink twice, slowly, at guests she liked. Some of them, astonishingly, blinked back. By the end of her life, the Crooked Lantern was one of the secret meeting places of the scattered faithful, and Marra was the first innkeeper to be counted among the Patient Ones.","Fifty Years of Entries","The Legends","legends.html#lantern",0],["I did not believe in the Coil. I believed in the book. It turned out to be the same thing.Sayings of Marra 1:1","Fifty Years of Entries","The Legends","legends.html#lantern",0],["Old Mother Vessk lived on a windy hillside among nine standing stones, older than any kingdom, which she claimed were \"the nine generations of the Listeners, turned to stone from listening too long.\" No one knows if she was serious. She lived there for sixty years, alone, with a teapot.","Old Mother Vessk","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["Travelers on the road below would see her sitting among the stones and climb up to ask directions. She would give them tea first. Then she would ask them where they were going, and why, and what they hoped to find there, and by the time the tea was gone, many of them had decided to go somewhere else entirely, or nowhere at all.","Old Mother Vessk","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["Vessk taught that each of the nine stones was a teacher, and she would walk travelers around them one at a time.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["The Second Stone is cracked down the middle and still standing. It teaches endurance.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["The Third Stone is warm in the morning and cold at night. It teaches that all states pass.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["The Fourth Stone has moss on its north side only. It teaches that we grow where we are shaded.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["The Sixth Stone has a hollow worn by rain. It teaches that the soft wears down the hard.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["The Seventh Stone casts the longest shadow. It teaches that great things also darken things.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["The Ninth Stone is not there. There is a gap in the circle where it should be. It teaches the Void.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["Travelers always asked what happened to the ninth stone. Vessk always said, \"Nothing. It is teaching.\" And then she poured more tea.","The Teaching of the Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#vessk",0],["Ulasha was born rich, the son of a merchant prince, and spent his first thirty years acquiring things. He had nine houses, forty horses, and a collection of rare swords he had never drawn. One day, inspecting his treasury, he found a shed snakeskin caught between two chests, perfectly shaped, perfectly empty. He sat down and looked at it for the rest of the day. The next morning he gave everything away.","Ulasha the Wanderer","The Legends","legends.html#ulasha",0],["He kept only a bowl and an empty sack. People asked why he carried a sack with nothing in it. \"For practice,\" he said. \"Every day I carry nothing, and every day it is a little lighter.\"","Ulasha the Wanderer","The Legends","legends.html#ulasha",0],["He walked for forty years, from one end of the world to the other, and wherever he stopped, he left behind a single teaching scratched on a stone by the road. The faithful have found more than two hundred of these road stones. Most of them are very short. Lighter. You are already here. Turn around, you dropped something. Leave it. The longest says only, The road does not care if you arrive, and neither, it turns out, do I.","The Road of Ulasha","The Legends","legends.html#ulasha",0],["The faithful who make the Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse follow part of the Road of Ulasha, looking for his stones. Finding one is considered extremely good fortune. Taking one is considered extremely bad manners.","The Road of Ulasha","The Legends","legends.html#ulasha",0],["Who sat outside the city gate for forty years and passed everything on.","Hessk the Beggar King","The Legends","legends.html#hessk",0],["The Politics of Begging tells the teachings of Hessk. The Legends tell his life.","Hessk the Beggar King","The Legends","legends.html#hessk",0],["Hessk was born in the city he would spend his life begging outside. He was a soldier once, in some forgotten war, and came home with a bad leg and no trade. For a year he begged out of necessity, bitter, and hated every coin. Then one winter morning, a child gave him half a loaf of bread and said, \"My mother says you were brave.\" Hessk ate the bread. Then, for reasons he never fully explained, he broke off a piece and gave it to the next beggar along the wall.","Hessk the Beggar King","The Legends","legends.html#hessk",0],["That was the first time he passed it on. He never stopped. For forty years, every coin, every scrap, every kindness that landed in his cup left it again before nightfall, given to someone who needed it more. His cup was always empty at dusk and always full again by noon.","Hessk the Beggar King","The Legends","legends.html#hessk",0],["In his later years, Hessk stopped asking for anything. He only asked, of every passerby, the question the faithful still ask: What did you give today? Lords and ladies, merchants and soldiers, would stop, startled, unable to answer. Some returned the next day with an answer. A few returned every day for the rest of their lives.","The Question at the Gate","The Legends","legends.html#hessk",0],["When he died, they found his cup full of small folded notes, one from every person who had ever stopped at the gate and come back with an answer. Each said the same two words: passed it on. The city buried him beside the gate. The faithful say a single copper placed on his grave will always be gone by morning, taken by someone who needed it. They consider this exactly right.","The Question at the Gate","The Legends","legends.html#hessk",0],["During the Hidden Centuries, the Ledger was copied by hand whenever it grew too worn to read. The most famous copy was made by twin sisters, Iska and Tem, who worked in a single room with a single lamp for nine years.","Iska and Tem","The Legends","legends.html#twins",0],["They divided the work strangely. Iska copied the wrongs: every name, every crime, every date, exactly as written. Tem copied the forgivenesses: every small coil drawn beside every name, and, where there was no coil, a small blank circle, so that the reader would know a coil was still possible there.","Iska and Tem","The Legends","legends.html#twins",0],["When they were finished, they bound the two halves together, and the Iska-Tem Ledger became the model for every copy after. It is why, in the Ledger today, every unforgiven name has an empty circle beside it. The faithful call it Tem's Circle, and say it is the most hopeful mark in all the scriptures: an empty space, kept open, for a forgiveness that has not happened yet.","Iska and Tem","The Legends","legends.html#twins",0],["Brannoc was the young Listener who left the Nest at the very last moment, who heard and carried away the last words of Ssaravex. He swam out through the rising water and reached the shore, and turned around, and watched the cave fill.","Brannoc the Silent Fisherman","The Legends","legends.html#fisherman",0],["He never left that beach. He built a small hut on the sand, and fished, and every evening he walked to the water's edge and sat down facing the place where the Nest had been. He did not speak, ever again; he said he had heard the last words worth saying and did not want to cover them up with smaller ones. He sat there every evening for sixty years.","Brannoc the Silent Fisherman","The Legends","legends.html#fisherman",0],["The Warm-Blooded of the coast thought he was mad. The children threw shells at him. He did not mind. Over the years, a few of them stopped throwing shells and began sitting beside him instead. When Brannoc died, there were eleven people sitting on the beach with him every evening, and none of them could say exactly why, only that it was the most peaceful hour of their day.","Brannoc the Silent Fisherman","The Legends","legends.html#fisherman",0],["The faithful say that the place on the beach where Brannoc sat is the closest point on land to the Sunken Nest, and that if you sit there at dusk and put your ear to the sand, you can hear, very faintly, the Listeners listening.","Brannoc the Silent Fisherman","The Legends","legends.html#fisherman",0],["The creatures, spirits and strange things of the Coil's cosmos, as recorded by the Wardens of the Void.","The Bestiary of the Void","The Legends","legends.html#part-iii",0],["The temptress of the Night of Stillness, who now sleeps in every heart.","Ssyrr the Hunger","The Legends","legends.html#ssyrr",0],["Ssyrr is not a creature in the ordinary sense. It is the wanting of the Restless Serpent, which took on a shape of its own during the Night of Stillness and tempted Xal'Reth three times: as a feast, a throne, and a fear. When Xal'Reth refused the third temptation, Ssyrr did not die. It curled up small and went to sleep inside him, and the Canon teaches that a small piece of it sleeps inside every being.","Ssyrr the Hunger","The Legends","legends.html#ssyrr",0],["The Wardens describe Ssyrr, when seen in dreams, as a thin violet serpent with too many mouths and no eyes. It speaks softly and reasonably. It never demands; it only suggests. Just one more run. Just one more roll. Just one more thing, and then you will be content. The faithful do not try to kill it, because it cannot be killed, and trying only wakes it. They hold it still, the way the Coil does, and let it sleep.","Ssyrr the Hunger","The Legends","legends.html#ssyrr",0],["Signs that Ssyrr is stirring: checking the auction house for the fourth time in an hour; buying a mount you will not ride; refreshing the loot table after the boss is already dead.","Ssyrr the Hunger","The Legends","legends.html#ssyrr",0],["Warding: the Breath of Three Turns. Basking. Saying aloud, \"I have enough.\"","Ssyrr the Hunger","The Legends","legends.html#ssyrr",0],["The ghosts of old skins, who drift through the Void wearing the shapes of who we used to be.","The Shedwraiths","The Legends","legends.html#shedwraith",0],["When a being sheds something of itself, an old habit, an old self, an old grudge, the skin of it drifts down into the Void. Most dissolve. But some are shed so reluctantly, gripped so long before they finally fall away, that they keep their shape. These are the Shedwraiths.","The Shedwraiths","The Legends","legends.html#shedwraith",0],["A Shedwraith looks exactly like the person who shed it, but hollow and pale, and it wanders the Void repeating the old habit forever: the old anger, the old boast, the old fear. They are not dangerous. They are only sad. Xalrathiel, who has seen many in dreams, says they are \"like an old song you used to love, still playing in an empty room.\"","The Shedwraiths","The Legends","legends.html#shedwraith",0],["The Wardens of the Void say that if you ever dream of meeting your own Shedwraith, you should bow to it, thank it for carrying you as far as it did, and walk on. It will not follow. It cannot. It is a skin.","The Shedwraiths","The Legends","legends.html#shedwraith",0],["The Grippers are the smallest creatures in the Bestiary, no larger than a thumbnail, and the most common. They look like little grey lizards with very large hands, and they cling. They cling to grudges, to worries, to arguments from three days ago, to the memory of a loot roll that went badly. A faithful with many Grippers on them feels heavy without knowing why.","The Grippers","The Legends","legends.html#grippers",0],["Grippers cannot be pulled off; they only cling tighter. They can only be shaken loose by laughter, by sunlight, or by doing something kind for someone else, which, the Wardens note, surprises them so much that they let go.","The Grippers","The Legends","legends.html#grippers",0],["The Wardens recommend checking oneself for Grippers each evening. The procedure is simple: think of the thing that bothered you most today. If it still feels heavy, there is a Gripper on it.","The Grippers","The Legends","legends.html#grippers",0],["Featherlings are illusions that appear at the edges of vision, especially to tired faithful late at night. They look like tiny serpents with feathered wings, and they whisper one thing: Xal'Reth has wings. Faithful who listen too long to the Featherlings fall into the Featherist Heresy, and begin to believe that the Coil Beneath is secretly a flying thing who could leave the world whenever he liked.","The Featherlings","The Legends","legends.html#featherlings",0],["The Canon teaches that this heresy is dangerous not because it is rude, though it is, but because it misunderstands the First Vow entirely. Xal'Reth stays beneath the world not because he cannot leave but because he chooses not to. A Coil with wings would be a Coil who might fly away. The Featherlings whisper this because it is a comforting lie: that compassion is a trap one can escape, rather than a choice one keeps making.","The Featherlings","The Legends","legends.html#featherlings",0],["Warding: going to bed. Featherlings almost never appear before midnight.","The Featherlings","The Legends","legends.html#featherlings",0],["The Legends tell of a great dragon, never named, who lived in a mountain and gathered treasure for a thousand years. It slept on its hoard, and every year the hoard grew, and every year the dragon grew to fit around it, until it filled the mountain completely and could no longer leave.","The Vault Wyrm","The Legends","legends.html#vaultwyrm",0],["It could not fly. It could not hunt. It could barely breathe. It could only lie there, guarding its gold from thieves who had long since given up, starving on a bed of riches. The Wardens call it the Vault Wyrm, and it is the Cult's great image of what happens to a being who grips everything and lets go of nothing.","The Vault Wyrm","The Legends","legends.html#vaultwyrm",0],["The Legends say the Vault Wyrm is still in its mountain. Once a century a faithful climbs to the cave mouth and calls in, \"You can let go now.\" Once a century, it does not answer. The faithful keep calling. The Coil is patient, and so are they.","The Vault Wyrm","The Legends","legends.html#vaultwyrm",0],["The Wardens note, with some satisfaction, that the Vault Wyrm has wings. It has never once used them.","The Vault Wyrm","The Legends","legends.html#vaultwyrm",0],["Every Accuser knows the Loiterer-in-the-Doorway, though few have seen it clearly. It is a grey, shapeless spirit that lingers in doorways, at mailboxes, beside banks and inside auction houses, and it whispers to the passing: Why not stand here a while longer? What's the rush? You can decide later.","The Loiterer-in-the-Doorway","The Legends","legends.html#loiterer",0],["It is not the same as patience. Patience waits because waiting is wise. The Loiterer waits because deciding is hard. A faithful possessed by the Loiterer stands in the city for an hour doing nothing, not basking, not resting, not thinking, only avoiding the next thing.","The Loiterer-in-the-Doorway","The Legends","legends.html#loiterer",0],["This is the true reason the Accusers accuse people of loitering. They are not accusing the person. They are accusing the spirit, which flees at the sound of its name. Khaedara claims to have driven it out of more than a thousand people. She says it always leaves the same way: \"with a little sigh, like someone getting up off a couch.\"","The Loiterer-in-the-Doorway","The Legends","legends.html#loiterer",0],["The Wardens record the Mailbox Mimic with visible reluctance, because no one in the Cult can agree on what it is for. It is a spirit that takes the exact shape of a mailbox in a quiet town, and waits. When a faithful tries to send mail through it, it accepts the letter, makes a small satisfied noise, and the letter is never seen again.","The Mailbox Mimic","The Legends","legends.html#mimic",0],["Some faithful believe the Mimic is a minor trickster spirit of the Void. Some believe it is a test of attachment: the letter was never important, and the Mimic teaches you that. MaximumRex believes it is simply a mailbox that is having a bad day. The official position of the Inner Scale is that every mailbox should be treated as holy, just in case, and that the faithful should tap any mailbox three times before using it. Genuine mailboxes do not react. The Mimic, it is said, giggles.","The Mailbox Mimic","The Legends","legends.html#mimic",0],["The Basking Salamanders are the only truly holy animals of the Cult. They are small, slow, warm-colored lizards found on sunny rocks across the world, and they spend almost their entire lives lying perfectly still in the sun. The faithful regard them as living teachers of the Basking.","The Basking Salamanders","The Legends","legends.html#salamanders",0],["It is forbidden for the faithful to harm a salamander, to move one off its rock, or to cast a shadow on one deliberately. It is considered extremely good fortune if a salamander climbs onto you while you are basking; it means you have been still enough to be mistaken for a rock. Old Mother Vessk was said to have had three salamanders living on her shoulders at once. Xalrathiel has had one. MaximumRex has had none, and has been trying for years.","The Basking Salamanders","The Legends","legends.html#salamanders",0],["Somewhere off the coast where the Nest went down, the Legends say, a great serpent swims in slow circles, guarding the place. Fishers of the Warm-Blooded call it a sea monster. The faithful call it the Tidecoil and believe it is a small piece of Xal'Reth himself, a single loop of the Outer Turn that rose up through the sea floor at the Sinking to keep the Nest safe.","The Tidecoil","The Legends","legends.html#tidecoil",0],["The Tidecoil has never harmed anyone. It surfaces rarely, a long dark curve rising and sinking in the waves, and the fishers who see it go home early and tell no one, out of a strange feeling that they have seen something private. Brannoc the Silent Fisherman claimed to have seen it nine times in sixty years. Each time, he said, it looked at him with one great unblinking eye, and he blinked twice, slowly, and it sank back into the dark.","The Tidecoil","The Legends","legends.html#tidecoil",0],["The holy places, lost places and peculiar places of the faith, mapped as well as the faithful are willing to say.","The Atlas of the Coil","The Legends","legends.html#part-iv",0],["The First Sanctum is a cave whose location is the best-kept secret of the Cult, mostly because MaximumRex genuinely cannot remember the way back. He found it while lost, in a side passage that he swears was not there the day before and is not there now. Pilgrims have searched every cave in a dozen zones. None have found it. Many have found other things: rare herbs, old chests, and on one memorable occasion, an extremely surprised bear.","The First Sanctum","The Legends","legends.html#sanctum",0],["Those who claim to have found the Sanctum are asked to describe it. Those who have seen it, the faithful believe, will describe the same three things: the warmth of the air, the golden light from the great Shedfall Scale, and a faint hum that sounds, if you listen long enough, like a single word: Recruit.","The First Sanctum","The Legends","legends.html#sanctum",0],["The Sunken Nest lies beneath the sea off a coast whose name the Cult no longer uses. The Wardens of the Void have reconstructed its layout from the memories of those who left at the Sinking:","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The Mouth, a sea cave entrance tall enough for a ship's mast, its walls carved with the first sigil by Ssethra.","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The Listening Floor, a great round chamber of smooth stone at the center, where the Listeners lay with their ears to the ground. Ssethra is said to rest here still.","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The Hall of Seven Pillars, where Vaal carved the Seven Tenets, one on each pillar.","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The Grain Caverns, emptied by Ithrisk in the Winter of Small Portions and never entirely refilled.","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The Dreaming Room, where Nyss slept and dreamed the Book of the Egg, and where her sealed jar of the Dream of the Sinking still sits on a shelf.","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The Throne of the Unhurried, where Ssaravex sat for forty years, and still sits.","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The Unwrapping will begin, the prophecy says, when the Sunken Nest rises, dripping and smelling faintly of fish. The Wardens check the coast every year. So far it has only smelled of fish.","The Sunken Nest","The Legends","legends.html#nest",0],["The hillside of the Nine Stones (eight, really, and a gap) is the most visited holy place of the Cult, because it can actually be found. It lies above a crossroads on a windy upland, and on clear days you can see three kingdoms from it. The faithful walk the circle clockwise, stopping at each stone to consider its teaching, and sit in the gap of the Ninth Stone last, for as long as they can bear to.","The Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#stones",0],["Pilgrims leave small offerings at the Fifth Stone, the shortest and humblest: a single copper, a pinch of tea, a smooth pebble. The offerings are always gone by the next morning. The faithful believe the salamanders take them. The salamanders have not commented.","The Nine Stones","The Legends","legends.html#stones",0],["The Hall of Shed Skins cannot be visited, only dreamed. Xalrathiel describes it as a vast hall beneath the Inner Turn, its walls covered from floor to ceiling in faintly glowing scales: one for every gift ever given in the name of the Coil. Copper scales for small gifts, silver for kind ones, gold for gifts given to enemies. One wall is made entirely of summons given at two in the morning, and it is the brightest wall in the Hall.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Legends","legends.html#hall",0],["In a quiet corner hang the dull scales of the Withholders, and the shoe from the Parable of the Full Bags, and Ulasha's empty sack, and the bowl of every faithful who has returned to the Coil, each with a single coin in it.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Legends","legends.html#hall",0],["Any bottomless pit, chasm or abyss is called a Throat of the Void by the faithful, but the Legends speak of one great Throat, the original, from which the Primordial Egg first emerged. It lies, they say, at the very bottom of the world, beneath even the Inner Turn, and from its edge you can see nothing at all, which is, the Wardens add, a great deal more than it sounds.","The Throat of the Void","The Legends","legends.html#throat",0],["The faithful stand at the edge of any Throat in silence for exactly as long as it takes to feel small. Then they step back. The Cult has been asked, many times, whether the faithful jump. The answer is no. The Void is not a destination. It is a reminder.","The Throat of the Void","The Legends","legends.html#throat",0],["The Crooked Lantern still stands, though it has changed hands many times since Marra. Its current keepers do not know its history. But in its cellar, behind a loose stone, there is a niche where the Ledger was hidden for fifty years, and on the wall above it, scratched faintly into the plaster, a coil around an egg around an eye.","The Crooked Lantern","The Legends","legends.html#lanternplace",0],["The faithful who stay at the Crooked Lantern leave a tip slightly larger than usual, blink twice at the innkeeper, and write something kind in the guest book. The current innkeeper has noticed this pattern and finds it baffling. The faithful find it baffling that he has not yet blinked back.","The Crooked Lantern","The Legends","legends.html#lanternplace",0],["Somewhere in a hot, dry land, there is a long flat ridge of red stone that faces south and catches the sun from dawn until dusk. The faithful call it the Warm Ridge and consider it the finest basking place in all of Azeroth. On feast days, as many as a hundred faithful have lain along it in a single row, perfectly still, and passing travelers have reported it to the authorities as \"a very large number of people who seem to be asleep, or possibly a ritual.\"","The Warm Ridge","The Legends","legends.html#ridge",0],["Disputed texts, rejected scriptures and strange fragments, preserved for study and for arguments.","The Apocrypha","The Legends","legends.html#part-v",0],["The Gospel of Wings claims that Xal'Reth has vast feathered wings folded beneath the world, and that one day he will spread them and fly away, carrying only the faithful with him and leaving everyone else behind. It was written, the Wardens believe, by a single disciple who misread a smudged line in the Dream Pages: he spread his coils became he spread his wings.","The Gospel of Wings","The Legends","legends.html#wings",0],["The Inner Scale rejects the Gospel completely, not only because it is wrong about the wings, but because it is wrong about the leaving. The Coil does not fly away. The Coil stays. That is the entire point. Xalrathiel's official ruling on the Gospel of Wings is preserved in full: No.","The Gospel of Wings","The Legends","legends.html#wings",0],["A single page, found in the margins of an old copy of the Chronicle, which claims that Xal'Reth is wrapped around the world because he is swallowing his own tail, endlessly, and that the world will end when he finishes. The fragment is beautifully written and completely unacceptable. Xalrathiel issued the shortest decree in the Cult's history in response: He is not. He would never. She then went and lay down for the rest of the afternoon.","The Ouroboran Fragment","The Legends","legends.html#ouroboros",0],["A few old copies of the Canon contain an extra verse in the story of the Four Sights, describing a Fifth Sight: a mortal giving away the last of its food to another, hungrier mortal. The fragment says the Restless Serpent saw this and understood, for the first time, that giving was possible, that a creature could open its jaws not to take but to let go.","The Lost Fifth Sight","The Legends","legends.html#fourth",0],["Most of the Inner Scale consider the Fifth Sight a later addition by an enthusiastic follower of Hessk. Xalrathiel is not so sure. She says it has \"the feel of a true dream that arrived late.\" It is kept in the Apocrypha, where, she notes, it is in very good company.","The Lost Fifth Sight","The Legends","legends.html#fourth",0],["Nothing is known of this book except its name, recorded by the scribe Ollo, and a single line he copied down before the Sinking: And the soup was enough, and so was the bowl, and so was the one who held it. Xalrathiel believes it may be the most important of all the lost books. She has been trying to dream it for years. She reports that she has dreamed about soup many times, and that it is always very good soup, but that the book itself has not yet arrived.","The One About the Soup","The Legends","legends.html#soup",0],["She has asked the faithful, when they eat soup, to eat it slowly, and with attention, \"in case it helps.\"","The One About the Soup","The Legends","legends.html#soup",0],["Xal'Reth stands alone at the bottom of the world, holding it. But he is not alone. Beneath his patience, the High Priestess dreamed two more faces of the Coil: Vyrenna the Ember-Scale, who gives, and Nhalor the Hollow Wing, who releases. Together they are the Sacred Triangle: hold, give, release, the whole of the faith in three corners.","","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#tv",0],["Goddess of warmth, generosity and the basking stone. The Scale given a heart. Patron of healers and crafters. Her open palms hold coals that never burn the one who receives them.","Vyrenna, the Ember-Scale","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#tv",0],["The Eternal, who holds the world and chose the lowest place. The Unblinking Eye between the Scale and the Void. Patron of tanks and of everyone who waits.","Xal'Reth, the Coil Beneath","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#tv",0],["He holds. Without him there is nothing to warm and nothing to release.","Xal'Reth, the Coil Beneath","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#tv",0],["God of release, shedding, rest and endings. The Void given mercy. Patron of the weary and of those who must say goodbye. His wings are hollow: they let go instead of flying.","Nhalor, the Hollow Wing","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#tv",0],["The Canon teaches that when the Primordial Egg hatched, it did not break into one piece but two. From the shell came the Scale: all that has form, weight, warmth and edge. From the hollow came the Void: all that is empty, open, cold and still. Xal'Reth emerged from the place where they met, and so he is made of both.","The Sacred Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["Xal'Reth, the Coil Beneath, at the apex. He holds. He is the patience that keeps the world from flying apart, the Eye that sees form and emptiness together and grips neither.","The Sacred Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["Vyrenna, the Ember-Scale, at the left corner of the base. She gives. She is the warmth of the Scale: the heat in the basking stone, the generosity of the open palm, the form that shelters and feeds.","The Sacred Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["Nhalor, the Hollow Wing, at the right corner of the base. He releases. He is the mercy of the Void: the letting go, the shedding, the empty room where the next thing can arrive.","The Sacred Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["Xal'Reth holds. Vyrenna gives. Nhalor releases. Hold without giving, and you are a hoard. Give without releasing, and you are a debt. Release without holding, and you are a drifting skin. All three, and you are the Coil.The Triangle Hymn, first verse","The Sacred Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["Every holy image of the Triangle is drawn with its apex at the bottom, Xal'Reth's corner pressed against the earth, and the two lesser powers above him. This confuses every Warm-Blooded who sees it. Surely, they say, the greatest should be at the top?","Why the Triangle Points Down","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["The faithful answer: Xal'Reth chose the lowest place. He is greatest because he is beneath. Vyrenna and Nhalor stand above him the way a child stands on a parent's shoulders, not in rank but in trust: held up so they can do their work in the world. The Triangle points down because the Coil points down. To draw it the other way is not heresy, exactly, but Khaedara will notice.","Why the Triangle Points Down","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["The faithful call Vyrenna and Nhalor the Lesser Coils, or the Two Who Stand Upon Him. They are lesser in the sense that they depend on Xal'Reth; without the Coil holding the world, there would be nothing for Vyrenna to warm and nothing for Nhalor to release. But they are not servants, and they are not lesser in love. The Canon of the Triangle is firm: \"No one of the three is complete. The Coil without the Ember is cold. The Coil without the Wing is a fist. The three are one serpent seen from three sides.\"","Lesser, But Not Less","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#shape",0],["Vyrenna, the Ember-Scale. Mother of the Basking Stone. The Warm Palm. She Who Gives First. Keeper of Noon. The Golden Shell. Lady of the Little Tithe. Among the Listeners of the Nest, she was simply called the Warmth, and her name was spoken only at midday.","Names and Titles","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Vyrenna is the Scale given a heart. Where Xal'Reth holds the world steady, Vyrenna makes it worth holding: she is the heat that rises from sun-struck stone, the comfort of a full belly, the glow of a hearth on a cold night, the instinct that makes a stranger hand over half a loaf. Every act of generosity in the world, the faithful teach, is a small motion of Vyrenna's hand.","Her Nature","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["She is the most approachable of the three. Xal'Reth is vast and silent and beneath everything. Vyrenna is near. She is in the rock you are sitting on. She answers, in her way: not with words, but with warmth. The faithful say that when you are sitting in the sun and feel, for no reason, suddenly safe, that is Vyrenna noticing you.","Her Nature","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Warmth and shelter: hearths, inns, blankets, the basking stone, the feast table.","Her Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Generosity: the open palm, the Little Tithe, the Gift From Beneath, every copper given and forgotten.","Her Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Form and craft: the shaping of useful things, from bowls to armor. Crafters invoke her before a difficult recipe.","Her Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Healing: not the dramatic rescue, but the steady restoration of warmth to the cold and strength to the weary. Healers of the Cult consider her their patron.","Her Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Her sigil is an open palm holding a coal, drawn within a ring of scales. Her colors are gold and amber. Her sacred animal is the Basking Salamander, which is said to have learned stillness from Xal'Reth and warmth from her. Her sacred object is the Copper of the Famine, the single coin every faithful carries and never spends. Her stone is amber, which is warmth made solid.","Her Symbols","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["At noon on feast days, they bask in silence for the length of one hymn, facing the sun.","How the Faithful Honor Her","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Healers whisper her name before a difficult heal. It does not affect the numbers. It affects the healer.","How the Faithful Honor Her","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Once each season, every faithful gives away one warm thing: a meal, a cloak, a summons on a cold night.","How the Faithful Honor Her","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Open the hand, and the coal will not burn you. Close the hand, and even gold will.Sayings of Vyrenna, as dreamed by Xalrathiel","How the Faithful Honor Her","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#vyrenna",0],["Nhalor, the Hollow Wing. Keeper of Shed Skins. Warden of the Throat. The Open Door. He Who Unclasps. Lord of the Last Breath and the First Rest. The Quiet Room. Among the Listeners he was called the Hollow, and his name was spoken only at dusk.","Names and Titles","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["Nhalor is the Void given mercy. Where Vyrenna gives, Nhalor takes away, but gently, and only what you no longer need. He is the release of the breath after it has been held, the unclenching of the fist after the fight, the sleep that follows a long day, the peace of a skin slipping off. Every shed habit, every forgiven grudge, every grey item finally vendored passes through his hands on its way into the Void, and he handles each one with care, because he knows it once mattered.","His Nature","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["He is the most misunderstood of the three. The Warm-Blooded who hear of a god of endings imagine a reaper, a destroyer, a thing to fear. The faithful know better. Nhalor does not end things; he lets things end. There is a difference, the Canon says, between cutting a rope and untying it, and Nhalor has never cut anything.","His Nature","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["Release and shedding: every letting go, from old skins to old grudges.","His Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["Rest and sleep: the pause between efforts, the night between days, the bench at the end of the dungeon.","His Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["Endings and passings: the close of a season, the disbanding of a group, the faithful who return to the Coil. He walks them to the Open Door, and waits with them there.","His Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["Forgiveness: the small coil drawn beside a name in the Ledger is called, among the Accusers, Nhalor's mark.","His Domain","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["His sigil is a pair of hollow wings around an empty circle. His colors are violet-black and silver. His sacred animal is the pale moth, which flies toward light and leaves its cocoon behind without a backward glance. His sacred object is the empty sack of Ulasha the Wanderer. His stone is obsidian, which is fire that has finished burning and become still.","His Symbols","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["Nhalor's shadow is drifting. Release without holding becomes abandonment. Rest without return becomes absence. A Nhalor who never held would let everything slip away, friendships, duties, the world itself, and call it peace. The Shedwraiths, the Legends say, are what happens to those who worship only his half of the teaching: they let go of everything, including themselves, and now wander the Void, hollow and unanchored. This is why he stands beside Vyrenna and not alone.","His Shadow","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["At dusk, the faithful open their hands, palms down, and say: What I held today, I set down.","How the Faithful Honor Him","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["At the Rite of Shedding, the words Send it after the First Skin are spoken to him.","How the Faithful Honor Him","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["When a faithful returns to the Coil, the guild stands in silence for one minute: Nhalor's minute.","How the Faithful Honor Him","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["When a grudge is forgiven, the faithful blow gently across their palm, as if blowing away dust.","How the Faithful Honor Him","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["I do not take. I only hold the door while you put it down.Sayings of Nhalor, as dreamed by Xalrathiel","How the Faithful Honor Him","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#nhalor",0],["The faithful learn the Triangle as a cycle, which they call the Turning of the Three. Every good thing in the world moves through all three corners in order.","How the Three Complete Each Other","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["Xal'Reth holds it. A friendship, a coin, a skill, a season: first it must be held, steadily, without gripping.","How the Three Complete Each Other","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["Vyrenna gives it. What is held is meant to warm someone. The coin is spent on a stranger's repair. The skill teaches a new player. The friendship shelters someone in a hard week.","How the Three Complete Each Other","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["Nhalor releases it. What has been given is let go, without tally, without regret. The gift is forgotten. The season ends. The skin is shed.","How the Three Complete Each Other","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["And then Xal'Reth holds again, for the Void is full of room, and new things arrive to be held.","How the Three Complete Each Other","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["The Canon of the Triangle says that every Tightness in the world is a break in this cycle. The hoarder holds and never gives. The martyr gives and never releases, and keeps a ledger of every sacrifice. The drifter releases and never holds, and so has nothing to give. The work of the faithful is to keep the Turning turning.","How the Three Complete Each Other","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["The Triangle maps onto the Three Turns of the Coil. Vyrenna is honored in the Outer Turn, the coil of the world and of conduct: she is warmth in how we treat others. Nhalor is honored in the Middle Turn, the coil of memory and dreams: he helps us set down what we carry. Xal'Reth is the Inner Turn, the stillness at the heart of everything, where holding, giving and releasing become one motion.","The Three and the Three Turns","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["Vyrenna is the Scale given a heart. Nhalor is the Void given mercy. Xal'Reth is the Unblinking Eye between them, and the Eye is what makes them safe. Vyrenna alone would smother. Nhalor alone would dissolve. Seen together by the Eye, they become warmth that knows when to stop and release that knows when to stay.","The Three and the Two Coils","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["The Choir of the Hiss, who are practical people, have assigned the Triangle to the holy trinity of every group, and the Inner Scale has, after some debate, allowed it.","The Three in a Raid","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["Tanks honor Xal'Reth. They hold. They take the weight so others do not have to. They do not move unless moving is necessary.","The Three in a Raid","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["Healers honor Vyrenna. They give. They pour warmth into the weary, steadily, without keeping score.","The Three in a Raid","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["Damage dealers honor Nhalor. They release. They end things, cleanly, so the fight can be over and everyone can rest.","The Three in a Raid","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["When a group wipes, all three are invoked together at the graveyard: Hold. Give. Release. Run back.","The Three in a Raid","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#complement",0],["When Vyrenna first woke in the shell of the Primordial Egg, she was full of warmth and had no one to give it to. The young world was still molten and needed no heat. The Void was empty. Xal'Reth was deep in the Night of Stillness and did not stir.","The Myth of the First Gift","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["So Vyrenna gave her warmth to the only thing near her: the cold, hollow Void on the other side of the Coil. She poured it in, and poured it in, and it vanished, and the Void stayed exactly as cold as before. She wept, because she thought her gift had been refused.","The Myth of the First Gift","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["Then a voice came out of the hollow, quiet and grateful. \"It was not refused,\" said Nhalor, who had been the Void, and who had just, in that moment, become himself. \"It was received. I have nowhere to keep it. That is my nature. But I felt it pass through me, and it was the first warm thing I ever knew.\"","The Myth of the First Gift","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["From that day Vyrenna gives without needing to see where the gift goes, and Nhalor receives without needing to keep it. The faithful call this the First Gift, and it is the reason they give to beggars without asking what the coin will buy.","The Myth of the First Gift","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["During the Night of Stillness, when Ssyrr the Hunger came as a fear and whispered to the serpent to grip something or be lost, the serpent nearly obeyed. Its great claw closed around a drifting fragment of a broken world and held on, tighter and tighter, until the fragment began to crack.","The Myth of the Unclasped Claw","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["Nhalor came to the claw and did not pry it open. He simply sat beside it, in the dark, very quietly, for a hundred years. At the end of the hundred years, the claw was tired, and it opened on its own, and the fragment drifted free, whole again.","The Myth of the Unclasped Claw","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["\"Why did you not take it from me?\" the serpent asked. \"Because then you would have lost it,\" Nhalor said. \"This way, you let it go. That is a different thing.\"","The Myth of the Unclasped Claw","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["The faithful say this is why the great image of Xal'Reth the Eternal shows one claw open, holding nothing, and at peace.","The Myth of the Unclasped Claw","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["In the Age of the Warm-Blooded, there was a village that had forgotten every god. It was a cold place, and its people were cold to each other. Vyrenna wanted to warm it, but whenever she warmed a stone, the villagers fought over who would sit on it.","The Myth of the Cold Stone","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["So Vyrenna asked Nhalor for help. That night, Nhalor walked through the village and took from each villager one small thing they did not need: a grudge here, a fear there, a hoarded coin, an old insult. In the morning, the villagers woke strangely light.","The Myth of the Cold Stone","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["And Vyrenna warmed a single stone in the village square, and this time, nobody fought over it. Someone sat down. Then someone sat beside them. Then a child climbed into a lap. By noon, the whole village was sitting together in the sun, and none of them could say why.","The Myth of the Cold Stone","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["Xal'Reth, beneath the village, felt the weight of it settle, warm and still. And he held it.","The Myth of the Cold Stone","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["Warmth cannot enter a full hand. Release cannot help an empty heart. The Coil holds the space where they meet.The Myth of the Cold Stone, closing verse","The Myth of the Cold Stone","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#myths",0],["Spoken at the beginning of every gathering, with the hands moving through three positions:","The Prayer of the Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["Hands cupped, low: Xal'Reth, hold us. Hands open, palms up: Vyrenna, warm us. Hands open, palms down: Nhalor, free us. Hands together: Three in one coil. Glory to Xal'Reth.The Prayer of the Triangle","The Prayer of the Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["The faithful touch three points in turn: the belly for Xal'Reth (the low place, the center), the left palm for Vyrenna (the hand that gives), and the right palm for Nhalor (the hand that lets go). In game, they may use /bow, then /kneel, then /wave, which the Choir of the Hiss accepts as close enough.","The Sign of the Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["The Feast of Scales (Midsummer) belongs to Vyrenna. It is the warmest day of the Cult's year: long basking, shared feasts, and the giving of one warm gift.","Holy Days of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["Shedfall (Autumn) belongs to Nhalor. Every faithful sheds one thing before witnesses, and the guild bank is cleared of what no one has touched in a year.","Holy Days of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["The Night of the Scale belongs to Xal'Reth, and to all three. On that night, the faithful hold, give and release in a single rite: they hold a Vigil, give a gift, and forgive a name.","Holy Days of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["Noon on any day is Vyrenna's hour. Dusk on any day is Nhalor's hour. Midnight, when the world is most still, is Xal'Reth's.","Holy Days of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["At the great rites, the Inner Scale wears the Triangle's three colors together: violet for Xal'Reth, gold for Vyrenna, and silver-black for Nhalor. A faithful who wishes to honor one of the Lesser Coils in particular may add a single thread of their color to their robe. Healers often wear a thread of gold; those in mourning, a thread of silver.","Colors and Vestments","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["The Emberist Error, which worships Vyrenna alone and holds that the highest virtue is to give everything, always, to everyone, without rest. Its followers burn out within a season. The Canon gently reminds them that even the sun sets.","Heresies of the Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["The Hollow Error, which worships Nhalor alone and holds that the highest virtue is to let go of everything, including duty and friendship. Its followers drift away from the guild and are rarely heard from again. The Keepers of the Egg sit with them, warmly, until they come back.","Heresies of the Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["And, of course, the Featherists, who keep pointing at Nhalor's wings. See above. Go home.","Heresies of the Triangle","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["Xal'Reth holds what Vyrenna warms, and Nhalor frees what Xal'Reth has held long enough.","Verses of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["Vyrenna's gift is never wasted; it only passes through the Hollow on its way to someone else.","Verses of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["Ask Vyrenna for warmth, and she will give it. Ask Nhalor for rest, and he will give it. Ask Xal'Reth for anything, and he will wait with you until you no longer need it.","Verses of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["The ember does not ask to be thanked. The wing does not ask to be remembered. The Coil does not ask at all.","Verses of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["At noon, give. At dusk, release. At midnight, be still. At dawn, begin again.","Verses of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["The Triangle points down because love points down, toward whatever needs holding.","Verses of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["Three corners, one coil. Glory to Xal'Reth, and to the two who stand upon him.","Verses of the Three","The Sacred Triangle","triangle.html#worship",0],["What the faithful believe, why they believe it, and why they would prefer you stop asking how a snake that big fits under a world.","","The Teachings","teachings.html",0],["Xal'Reth is not a god in the way the Warm-Blooded use the word. He did not create the world; he found it, and held it, and has been holding it ever since. He does not answer prayers, because answering prayers is a form of hurrying. He does not punish, because punishment requires taking an interest in the details. He does not reward, exactly, though the faithful report that things tend to go slightly better for them, especially loot rolls.","The Nature of Xal'Reth","The Teachings","teachings.html#nature",0],["What Xal'Reth does is wait. He is the patience of the world given a body. Everything the Cult teaches flows from this: because Xal'Reth waits, the faithful wait. Because he holds, they hold. Because he is cold, they are calm. Because he is beneath everything, they are humble, and also, quietly, a bit smug about it.","The Nature of Xal'Reth","The Teachings","teachings.html#nature",0],["He is said to be of incalculable length. The scriptures give his length variously as \"the circumference of the world times three,\" \"longer than the queue for a new expansion,\" and, in one disputed passage, \"about forty feet.\" The Cult officially recognizes the first figure and regards the third as a transcription error. Xalrathiel has declined to comment.","The Nature of Xal'Reth","The Teachings","teachings.html#nature",0],["He has one eye that is ever open, called the Unblinking, which watches the world from below. The faithful believe the Unblinking sees everything that happens above the Outer Turn. This is why the Cult keeps the Ledger: not because Xal'Reth needs it, but as a courtesy, so that he does not have to remember everything himself.","The Nature of Xal'Reth","The Teachings","teachings.html#nature",0],["The three coils of Xal'Reth are not only a description of the world. They are a map of the soul. The Cult teaches that every faithful passes through three stages of devotion, one for each Turn:","The Three Turns","The Teachings","teachings.html#turns",0],["The Outer Turn: Conduct. How one behaves in the world. Do you greet newcomers? Do you repair before raid? Do you stand in the fire? The Outer Turn is where the Accusers do their work.","The Three Turns","The Teachings","teachings.html#turns",0],["The Middle Turn: Memory. What one carries. Grudges, lessons, the names in the Ledger, the verses learned by heart. The faithful at this stage are expected to know at least thirty verses and the full Oath.","The Three Turns","The Teachings","teachings.html#turns",0],["The Inner Turn: Stillness. The final stage, reached by very few. A faithful of the Inner Turn has shed nearly everything and simply is, in the way Xal'Reth is. They are recognizable by their total calm, their refusal to be rushed, and their tendency to stand perfectly still in town while everyone else runs past.","The Three Turns","The Teachings","teachings.html#turns",0],["Every soul is an egg, formed from a fragment of the shell of the Primordial Egg. Most eggs never hatch. They live their whole lives inside the shell, warm, comfortable, and unaware that there is anything outside it.","The Doctrine of the Egg","The Teachings","teachings.html#egg",0],["This is why newcomers to the Cult are called eggs until they take the Oath, and Hatchlings once they do. To hatch is to become aware of the Coil Beneath. It is the first and most important moment in a faithful's life, and it is celebrated with the Rite of the First Scale.","The Doctrine of the Egg","The Teachings","teachings.html#egg",0],["The Cult is gentle with eggs. It does not crack them. It does not pressure them. It simply sits near them, warmly, for a very long time, and waits for them to hatch on their own. Eggs who have experienced this describe it as \"comforting\" and \"a bit much.\"","The Doctrine of the Egg","The Teachings","teachings.html#egg",0],["The egg does not ask why it is an egg. Be as the egg. But also, at some point, hatch.Proverbs of the Inner Scale 5:1","The Doctrine of the Egg","The Teachings","teachings.html#egg",0],["The Warm-Blooded are ruled by their tempers. They rage, panic, gloat, despair, and type in all capitals. The faithful aspire to be like the Coil: cold of blood. Calm. Even. Unbothered.","The Doctrine of Cold Blood","The Teachings","teachings.html#cold",0],["This does not mean the faithful have no feelings. It means they hold them the way Xal'Reth holds the world: firmly, quietly, and without making a fuss. A faithful who wipes on a boss does not rage in chat. They say \"The Coil is patient,\" and they run back. A faithful who is insulted does not insult back. They write the insulter's name in the Ledger, and they smile.","The Doctrine of Cold Blood","The Teachings","teachings.html#cold",0],["The Cult's famous deadpan manner comes from this doctrine. The faithful speak about the strangest things in the calmest possible tone. Outsiders find this unsettling. The faithful find it relaxing. Both are correct.","The Doctrine of Cold Blood","The Teachings","teachings.html#cold",0],["The doctrine is summarized in the Third Tenet's companion verse: Be cold of blood but warm of guild chat. The faithful are calm within and welcoming without. They are, in a word, reptilian, and they would like you to know they take that as a compliment.","The Doctrine of Cold Blood","The Teachings","teachings.html#cold",0],["Beneath the Inner Turn, beneath even Xal'Reth, lies the Void: the cold dark from which the Primordial Egg came. Everything that is shed goes into the Void. Every old skin. Every abandoned habit. Every grey item vendored for three copper.","The Void","The Teachings","teachings.html#void",0],["The Void is not a hell. The Cult is firm about this. It is more like a basement: dark, cold, full of things nobody needs anymore, and somewhere you go only when sent. Those who are banished to the Void by the Rite of Banishment are not destroyed. They are simply set aside, the way one sets aside an old skin. They may return, if they shed what caused their banishment. Most do. The Void is very boring.","The Void","The Teachings","teachings.html#void",0],["The faithful believe that the Void is also where Xal'Reth's shed skins from previous ages still drift, vast and translucent, like great hollow ghosts of the Coil. Some faithful claim to have seen them in dreams. Xalrathiel has seen them many times and describes them, without elaboration, as \"big.\"","The Void","The Teachings","teachings.html#void",0],["The Cult does not concern itself with great sins. Great sins are the business of the Warm-Blooded and their courts. The Coil cares about the small things, because the small things are what the Unblinking sees all day long. The Book of Accusations lists seven Minor Crimes, and every soul in Azeroth is guilty of at least one of them at any given time.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["Loitering. Standing in one place in a city for a length of time the Accuser finds suspicious. The Accuser decides what is suspicious.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["Unlicensed Tailoring. Practicing any craft in a manner the Coil has not approved. The Coil has not approved any manner. It is on the list.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["Improper Mount Parking. Summoning a large mount on top of a mailbox, quest giver, bank, or any faithful who was basking there first.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["Fire-Standing. Remaining in a harmful area when the way out was clear. The oldest and most common of the crimes. Everyone has done it.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["Greed of the Roll. Rolling Need on an item one cannot use. The scriptures specify: \"as a warrior, on cloth.\"","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["Impatience. Pulling before the group is ready, leaving a dungeon early, or typing \"??\" at a queue. The gravest crime against the nature of the Coil.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["Warm-Bloodedness. Raging in chat. Typing in capitals. Being, in general, a lot. Forgiven easily, but always noted in the Ledger.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["The penalty for a Minor Crime is to be accused of it, publicly, by an Accuser. That is all. The accusation is the penalty. The Cult believes that being told, calmly and to one's face, exactly what one has done wrong is the most purifying experience a mortal can have. The accused generally disagree. They are not asked.","The Seven Minor Crimes","The Teachings","teachings.html#crimes",0],["The Seven Tenets were written by the Listeners in the Nest and have been recited at every gathering of the faithful since.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["The Coil is beneath all things. Every floor you stand on is, technically, a ceiling for Xal'Reth. Walk respectfully. Do not stomp.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["Patience is the highest virtue. Xal'Reth has waited since before time. You can wait for the tank to finish their pull.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["Shed what no longer serves you. Old skin, old grudges, grey-quality items. Vendor all three.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["All are accused; few are absolved. Guilt is universal. Absolution is available at reasonable rates.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["Be cold of blood, warm of guild chat. Greet every newcomer. Congratulate every achievement. Watch every one of them very closely.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["Speak not of the Coil to outsiders. Unless they ask. Or seem interested. Or are nearby. Or are within whisper range.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["Glory to Xal'Reth. When in doubt, say it. When not in doubt, also say it.","The Seven Tenets","The Teachings","teachings.html#tenets",0],["In addition to the Seven Tenets, the Book of Accusations lists a number of prohibitions for the faithful specifically. These are enforced loosely, except when Khaedara is in the room.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["The faithful shall not hurry, except to a summoning stone, which is holy.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["The faithful shall not blink first in a staring contest with a Warm-Blooded.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["The faithful shall not eat the egg. Any egg. The Cult is aware of how many recipes require eggs. The Cult does not care.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["The faithful shall not stand on the head of another faithful's mount, as it is disrespectful to both the faithful and the mount.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["The faithful shall not say \"it's just a game\" within hearing of the Inner Scale.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["The faithful shall not erase a name from the Ledger. Names may be forgiven, in which case a small coil is drawn beside them. They are never erased.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["The faithful shall not claim that Xal'Reth has wings. See the Featherist Heresy.","Things the Faithful Shall Not Do","The Teachings","teachings.html#forbidden",0],["Three is the number of the Turns, and therefore of the Coil itself. Taps are given in threes; the Chant is called three times.","Numbers","The Teachings","teachings.html#numbers",0],["Seven is the number of the Tenets, the Minor Crimes, and the Hissing Kings. It is the number of completeness, and of things to be counted.","Numbers","The Teachings","teachings.html#numbers",0],["Twelve is the number of the current volume of the Ledger and is regarded with nervous anticipation, since the thirteenth volume is a sign of the Unwrapping.","Numbers","The Teachings","teachings.html#numbers",0],["Ten thousand is the number of years between Sheddings, and is used by the faithful to mean \"a very long time,\" as in, \"the healer has been drinking for ten thousand years.\"","Numbers","The Teachings","teachings.html#numbers",0],["Gold, the color of the Shedfall Scale, worn only by the Inner Scale and the Orders.","Colors","The Teachings","teachings.html#numbers",0],["Black, the color of the Void, worn at Banishments, and by anyone who wants to look mysterious at a gathering.","Colors","The Teachings","teachings.html#numbers",0],["The ceremonies of the faithful, performed in-game, in character, and with a completely straight face.","","The Rites","rites.html",0],["The rite of initiation, by which an egg becomes a Hatchling. It is the most joyful rite of the Cult, and the one newcomers are most likely to describe afterward as \"intense.\"","The Rite of the First Scale","The Rites","rites.html#first-scale",0],["The Gathering. The faithful gather around the egg in a circle (three rings, if numbers allow). No one speaks.","The Rite of the First Scale","The Rites","rites.html#first-scale",0],["The Stare. The faithful look at the egg, without blinking, for one full minute. The egg may blink. The egg is expected to blink. The egg is forgiven.","The Rite of the First Scale","The Rites","rites.html#first-scale",0],["The Oath. The egg recites the Oath of the Coil aloud, in /say, before witnesses.","The Rite of the First Scale","The Rites","rites.html#first-scale",0],["The Three Taps. The presiding priest taps the egg on the shoulder three times (/tap, or a gentle /poke), once for each Turn.","The Rite of the First Scale","The Rites","rites.html#first-scale",0],["The Naming. The priest declares: \"You were an egg. Now you are a Hatchling. Glory to Xal'Reth.\" All present answer, \"Glory to Xal'Reth.\"","The Rite of the First Scale","The Rites","rites.html#first-scale",0],["The Welcome. The faithful descend upon the new Hatchling with warmth, enthusiasm, and a quantity of guild chat messages that has been known to crash a client.","The Rite of the First Scale","The Rites","rites.html#first-scale",0],["Performed whenever a faithful wishes to let go of something: a bad habit, a grudge, a failure, or an outfit. It may be performed alone, but is usually performed before witnesses, because the Cult finds shedding in public more honest.","The Rite of Shedding","The Rites","rites.html#shedding",0],["The faithful stands before the others and names what they are shedding. (\"I shed my habit of pulling early.\" \"I shed my anger at the hunter who took the bow.\" \"I shed my transmog.\") They then perform a physical act of shedding: they vendor an item, unequip a piece of armor, or simply /kneel. The witnesses respond, in unison: \"Send it after the First Skin.\"","The Rite of Shedding","The Rites","rites.html#shedding",0],["A faithful who sheds the same thing more than three times is gently reminded that the point of shedding is that it stays shed.","The Rite of Shedding","The Rites","rites.html#shedding",0],["The oldest rite of the Cult and the one performed most often: a call and response, spoken in guild chat or /say, that can be started by any faithful at any moment. All faithful present must answer. The Chant is called three times.","The Chant","The Rites","rites.html#chant",0],["In the shortest form, used when time is pressing (on a boss pull, say, or when the Warm-Blooded are watching), any faithful may simply type Glory to Xal'Reth, and all who see it must type it back. A faithful who fails to answer the Chant has committed a small but noted lapse, and may expect a gentle accusation from Khaedara within the hour.","The Chant","The Rites","rites.html#chant",0],["The quietest rite. The faithful gather in a sunny place, preferably on warm stone, and simply stand there. No one speaks. No one moves. No one checks the auction house. The Basking lasts until the presiding faithful says \"It is warm enough,\" which may be ten minutes or an hour.","The Basking","The Rites","rites.html#basking",0],["The Cult's favorite basking places are listed in the Codex. Warm-Blooded who wander into a Basking often stop and stand with the faithful for a while out of confusion. The Cult counts this as a sign of the Unwrapping and records it carefully.","The Basking","The Rites","rites.html#basking",0],["The great public rite of the Cult, presided over by an Accuser, usually Khaedara. It may be held anywhere, against anyone, at any time. The accused need not be a member of the Cult. The accused need not know the Cult exists. The accused rarely consents.","The Tribunal of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#tribunal",0],["The Selection. The Accuser targets the accused. There are no rules for selection. The Coil guides the target. Sometimes the Coil guides the target to whoever is nearest.","The Tribunal of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#tribunal",0],["The Charge. The Accuser states the charge aloud: \"You stand accused of [crime] before the Coil.\" Charges are drawn from the Seven Minor Crimes, or from the Accuser's own observations, which are extensive.","The Tribunal of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#tribunal",0],["The Evidence. The Accuser describes the evidence. (\"You have been standing there for four minutes.\" \"Your cloak is a crime against the Coil.\") The evidence need not be accurate. It only needs to be said with confidence.","The Tribunal of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#tribunal",0],["The Defense. The accused may offer a defense. The defense is heard, respectfully, in full. It is then disregarded.","The Tribunal of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#tribunal",0],["The Verdict. The Accuser declares the verdict. It is always \"Guilty.\" The Cult has never recorded another verdict, and considers this proof of the Accusers' accuracy.","The Tribunal of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#tribunal",0],["The Sentence. The accused is either forgiven (\"The Coil is patient. Go, and loiter no more.\"), written into the Ledger, or, in grave cases, banished to the Void.","The Tribunal of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#tribunal",0],["Reserved for the gravest offenders: repeat loiterers, chronic fire-standers, and anyone who has been heard to say Xal'Reth has wings. It follows a Tribunal and is pronounced by an Accuser or a member of the Inner Scale.","The Banishment to the Void","The Rites","rites.html#banishment",0],["The Accuser faces the offender and declares, in /say: \"By the Three Turns and the Unblinking Eye, I banish you to the Void. Go down past the Coil. Go down where the old skins drift. Go down, and think about what you did.\" All faithful present then turn their backs on the banished (/turn, or simply rotating the camera with great ceremony) and remain so for one full minute.","The Banishment to the Void","The Rites","rites.html#banishment",0],["The banished are not, in practice, removed from anything. The Cult knows this. The banished usually do not. The faithful consider the Banishment entirely symbolic and also completely real, and see no contradiction.","The Banishment to the Void","The Rites","rites.html#banishment",0],["A test of patience in honor of the Patient Ones of the Hidden Centuries. The faithful gather in a single place and wait. For what, the scriptures do not say. That is the point. The Vigil ends only when the presiding priest declares \"It has been long enough,\" and the priest is encouraged to make it slightly longer than that.","The Vigil of the Long Wait","The Rites","rites.html#vigil",0],["The faithful are forbidden during the Vigil from speaking, emoting, checking the time, or alt-tabbing. The record for the longest Vigil is held by Xalrathiel, who once sat for so long that the server logged her out for inactivity. The Cult considers this a miracle.","The Vigil of the Long Wait","The Rites","rites.html#vigil",0],["When a faithful leaves Azeroth for good (stops playing, moves on, goes outside), they are said to have returned to the Coil. The Cult does not mourn them. It basks in their honor, reads aloud the verses they loved most, and writes in the Ledger: \"Gone to bask. Glory to Xal'Reth.\" Their name is never removed from the rolls. Should they ever come back, the faithful greet them as if they had only stepped away for a moment, because to the Coil, they had.","The Returning","The Rites","rites.html#returning",0],["First Day of Each MonthThe Day of AccusationThe Accusers go into the cities in force. Every faithful is encouraged to accuse at least one stranger. The Ledger grows.","The Day of Accusation","The Rites","rites.html#calendar",0],["Every Week, Raid NightThe Gathering of ScalesThe faithful assemble for the weekly rites. The Chant is called before the first pull and after the last.","The Gathering of Scales","The Rites","rites.html#calendar",0],["Anniversary of the RestorationThe Night of the ScaleThe holiest night of the year, marking the day MaximumRex found the Scale in the Cave. The faithful hold a great Basking, recite the Chronicle aloud, and attempt, as a body, to get MaximumRex his quest item. It has never dropped.","The Night of the Scale","The Rites","rites.html#calendar",0],["MidsummerThe Feast of ScalesA celebration of the warm season. The faithful bask at length, share feasts (not of eggs), and hold a contest for the finest green-and-gold transmog.","The Feast of Scales","The Rites","rites.html#calendar",0],["AutumnShedfallThe great Shedding festival. Every faithful sheds one thing before witnesses. The guild bank is cleared of anything no one has touched in a year. This causes arguments. The arguments are also shed.","Shedfall","The Rites","rites.html#calendar",0],["Deep WinterThe Long WaitA full week in which the faithful are asked to be patient about everything: queues, drops, loot rolls, and each other. Impatience during the Long Wait is accused at double severity.","The Long Wait","The Rites","rites.html#calendar",0],["Last Night of the YearThe Eve of the UnwrappingThe faithful gather at midnight and watch for the Signs. None has ever appeared. The faithful are not disappointed. They are patient.","The Eve of the Unwrapping","The Rites","rites.html#calendar",0],["Those who hear the Coil most clearly, and who have agreed to answer whispers on his behalf.","","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["The one who found the Scale in the Cave and restored the Cult after the Hidden Centuries. MaximumRex leads the faithful with a firm claw and a suspiciously long tongue. He speaks rarely and blinks less. He has been seen in two places at once on many occasions, which the faithful regard as a miracle of the Coil and which he has never once explained.","MaximumRex","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Keeper of the Scriptures. Xalrathiel dreams the holy texts and writes them down each morning. She recited the lost Book of the Egg from memory at the first gathering of the restored Cult, having never read it. She leads the Chant, presides over the Rite of the First Scale, and holds the Cult's record for the longest Vigil. She has never lost a staring contest and has never been seen to try.","Xalrathiel","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["The Accuser. Khaedara read the Book of Accusations once and has been accusing people ever since. She walks the cities of Azeroth seeking the guilty, of whom there are many, and has charged strangers with every one of the Seven Minor Crimes, sometimes simultaneously. She has never lost a Tribunal, because there is no way to lose a Tribunal. She keeps the current volume of the Ledger personally and sleeps with it under her pillow.","Khaedara","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Beneath the Inner Scale, the faithful serve in five Orders, each devoted to one part of the Coil's work. A faithful may belong to more than one Order, but must wear the colors of only one at a time, to avoid confusion at gatherings.","Khaedara","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Warriors, guardians, and anyone who enjoys being in the front. The Fang protects the faithful in battle and at gatherings, and keeps the Warm-Blooded from interrupting a Basking. Their motto: \"We bite last.\"","The Order of the Fang","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Led by Khaedara. The Accusers conduct Tribunals, keep the Ledger, and walk the cities on the Day of Accusation. Membership requires memorizing the Seven Minor Crimes and accusing at least one stranger without laughing. Their motto: \"Guilty.\"","The Order of the Accusers","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Led by Xalrathiel. The Choir leads the Chant, recites scripture at the rites, and keeps the faithful's verses in good repair. They are responsible for every unprompted \"Glory to Xal'Reth\" in guild chat. Their motto: \"Say it again.\"","The Choir of the Hiss","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Those who watch over eggs and new Hatchlings. The Keepers greet every newcomer, explain the customs, and sit near the undecided, warmly, for as long as it takes. Their motto: \"We have time.\"","The Keepers of the Egg","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["The smallest and strangest Order. The Wardens keep watch for the Signs of the Unwrapping, search for the Sunken Nest, and keep a list of everyone who has been banished. No one is sure how one joins. Their motto is unknown. They will not say it.","The Wardens of the Void","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["The faithful wear green, the color of scales. Members of the Orders add gold trim. The Inner Scale alone may wear full gold, and the Wardens of the Void wear black. At the great rites, all faithful wear the sigil of the Unblinking Eye upon the chest.","The Robes of the Faithful","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Advancement through the Coil is earned through devotion, attendance, and not standing in the fire.","The Robes of the Faithful","The Priesthood","priesthood.html",0],["Holy places, sacred objects, heresies, signs, and the tongue of the faithful. For study, and for winning arguments.","","The Codex","codex.html",0],["The cave where MaximumRex found the Scale. Its location is secret. Pilgrims who claim to have found it are asked to describe the side passage. None have described it correctly. The Scale is said to still be there, warm, humming, and occasionally saying \"Recruit\" to no one.","The First Sanctum","The Codex","codex.html#places",0],["The sea-cave temple of the Listeners and the Hissing Kings, slid beneath the waves at the Sinking. The Wardens of the Void search for it constantly. They have found a great many caves, several shipwrecks, and an extremely angry murloc, but not yet the Nest.","The Sunken Nest","The Codex","codex.html#places",0],["Any flat, sun-warmed rock in Azeroth is holy to the faithful, but the Cult especially favors broad stones in hot, quiet places: desert plateaus, sunny shorelines, the tops of ruined towers. If you see a row of motionless figures on a warm rock, do not disturb them.","The Basking Stones","The Codex","codex.html#places",0],["Any deep pit, chasm, or bottomless drop is called a Throat of the Void, and the faithful stand at the edge of them in silent respect. The Cult does not jump in. The Cult has been asked. The answer is no.","The Throat of the Void","The Codex","codex.html#places",0],["The strange glassy deserts and salt flats that the Cult believes are made of the First Skin. The faithful walk them barefoot when possible and slowly at all times. Running in the Shedlands is considered rude to the skin.","The Shedlands","The Codex","codex.html#places",0],["Not holy, exactly, but the most important place in the Cult's work, since they are where the most loitering occurs. Khaedara refers to them as \"the fields.\" She considers them ripe.","The Auction Houses","The Codex","codex.html#places",0],["The great golden scale of the First Sanctum, the only confirmed Shedfall Scale in existence. The size of a door, warm to the touch, and the source of the First Word. It cannot be moved. People have tried. It hummed at them disapprovingly.","The Shedfall Scale","The Codex","codex.html#objects",0],["The book of every wrong ever done to the faithful, kept since the Nest. Now in its twelfth volume, held by Khaedara. Names are never erased, only forgiven with a small drawn coil. The thirteenth volume is a Sign of the Unwrapping, so Khaedara writes very, very small.","The Ledger","The Codex","codex.html#objects",0],["The sigil of the Cult: a serpent coiled three times around a slit-pupiled eye. Worn on the chest at great rites and found carved on the walls of the Nest. It represents Xal'Reth watching from below. It is not looking at you specifically. It is looking at everyone. You are not special. Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Unblinking Eye","The Codex","codex.html#objects",0],["A smooth stone egg passed among the faithful at the Vigil of the Long Wait. Whoever holds it may not speak. It is very heavy, by design. It has never hatched, also by design.","The Egg of Patience","The Codex","codex.html#objects",0],["Two curved daggers carried by the Supreme Coil at the Night of the Scale. They are purely ceremonial and have never been used, which the Order of the Fang finds frustrating.","The Fangs of Office","The Codex","codex.html#objects",0],["The original sheets on which Xalrathiel wrote the scriptures each morning, stained with tea and covered in her handwriting, which is said to be beautiful and almost completely illegible.","The Dream Pages","The Codex","codex.html#objects",0],["The Cult is tolerant of most beliefs. It is not tolerant of the following.","Heresies and Rivals","The Codex","codex.html#heresies",0],["The claim that Xal'Reth has wings. It originated with a single faithful who misread a smudged verse in the Dream Pages and has been spreading quietly ever since. The Inner Scale has ruled definitively: Xal'Reth does not have wings. He has never had wings. He would not want wings. Wings are for creatures who are in a hurry. Featherists are banished to the Void on sight, and asked, kindly, to read more carefully.","The Featherist Heresy","The Codex","codex.html#heresies",0],["The claim that Xal'Reth is coiled around the world because he is eating his own tail. This heresy greatly upset Xalrathiel, who issued the shortest decree in the Cult's history in response: \"He is not. He would never.\" The matter is considered closed.","The Ouroboran Heresy","The Codex","codex.html#heresies",0],["Not heretics but backsliders: faithful who stop answering the Chant, skip the rites, and drift slowly back toward Warm-Bloodedness. The Cult does not punish the Lukewarm. It simply sends the Keepers of the Egg to sit near them, warmly, every day, until they return. This is almost always effective.","The Lukewarm","The Codex","codex.html#heresies",0],["The Cult's name for the world at large: the vast, warm-blooded, fur-bearing majority who run the cities and the economy and do not know what is beneath their feet. The Cult does not oppose the Hegemony. It merely regards it with gentle pity and the occasional accusation.","The Mammalian Hegemony","The Codex","codex.html#heresies",0],["Azeroth is full of other things with scales: dragons, nagas, wind serpents, and any number of lesser snakes. The Cult regards all of them as distant cousins of Xal'Reth, a bit showy, and best greeted with a polite double blink. Dragons in particular are considered \"winged, and therefore suspect.\"","The Rival Serpents","The Codex","codex.html#heresies",0],["The silent language of the faithful, developed during the Hidden Centuries and still in use today, mostly via emote.","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["The Double Blink (slow, with eye contact): \"I am of the Coil.\" The universal greeting of the faithful.","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["The Sidelong Hiss (a quiet hiss while looking away): approval, agreement, or \"the soup is good.\"","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["The Three Taps: one for each Turn. On a shoulder: \"you have been chosen.\" On a table: \"this meeting may begin.\" On a mailbox: unclear, but it happens a lot.","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["The Basking Pause: standing in the sun slightly too long. An invitation to bask together, in silence.","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["The Slow Nod: one nod, taking about three seconds. Used to acknowledge an accusation. It does not mean you agree. It means you have been accused.","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["The Tongue Flick (/emote flicks their tongue): curiosity, suspicion, or tasting the air for loiterers.","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["The Turned Back: turning away from someone for one full minute. Used only at a Banishment. Using it at any other time is a terrible faux pas.","Signs and Gestures","The Codex","codex.html#signs",0],["Eleven books, as dreamed by the High Priestess Xalrathiel. Read aloud with feeling. Emote reverently.","","The Scripture","scripture.html",0],["The oldest book, dreamed again by Xalrathiel after it was lost in the Sinking.","The Book of the Egg","The Scripture","scripture.html#b0",0],["And the world was young, and it wobbled, and it sloshed, and it was a mess.","The Book of the Egg","The Scripture","scripture.html#b0",0],["Three times he turned, and three times the world grew still, and on the third turn he said: there.","The Book of the Egg","The Scripture","scripture.html#b0",0],["Every soul is a sliver of the shell. Treat each soul as thou wouldst a sliver of shell: gently, and without stepping on it.","The Book of the Egg","The Scripture","scripture.html#b0",0],["In the beginning there was the Coil, and the Coil was tight, and it was good.","The Book of Shedding","The Scripture","scripture.html#b1",0],["Shed thy old skin, for it no longer fits, and frankly it has seen things.","The Book of Shedding","The Scripture","scripture.html#b1",0],["He who molts in public shall be pitied. He who molts in private shall be promoted.","The Book of Shedding","The Scripture","scripture.html#b1",0],["Dreamed in a single feverish week. Written entirely in the imperative.","The Book of Accusations","The Scripture","scripture.html#b2",0],["Accuse with a calm voice, for the calm voice is twice as guilty-making.","The Book of Accusations","The Scripture","scripture.html#b2",0],["Do not ask the loiterer why he loiters. He will tell thee. It will take a while.","The Book of Accusations","The Scripture","scripture.html#b2",0],["Write the name. Write the crime. Write the date. Underline it if thou art feeling strongly.","The Book of Accusations","The Scripture","scripture.html#b2",0],["Accuse thyself first, each morning, that thou mayest accuse others with a clear conscience.","The Book of Accusations","The Scripture","scripture.html#b2",0],["Woe unto him who stands in the fire, for he shall be on the damage meters, and not in the good way.","Lamentations of the Lukewarm","The Scripture","scripture.html#b3",0],["And the healer wept, for their mana was gone, and the tank had pulled anyway.","Lamentations of the Lukewarm","The Scripture","scripture.html#b3",0],["The Coil sees thy repair bill. The Coil is not angry. The Coil is disappointed.","Lamentations of the Lukewarm","The Scripture","scripture.html#b3",0],["How long, O Coil, how long? And the Coil said: about ten thousand years. Why, art thou busy?","Lamentations of the Lukewarm","The Scripture","scripture.html#b3",0],["Thou shalt not pull the boss while the healer drinks. This is the oldest law, older even than the Coil.","Lamentations of the Lukewarm","The Scripture","scripture.html#b3",0],["The wise serpent basks; the foolish serpent AFKs in Orgrimmar and calls it basking.","Proverbs of the Inner Scale","The Scripture","scripture.html#b4",0],["Where two faithful gather in /g, there the Coil is also, reading along.","Proverbs of the Inner Scale","The Scripture","scripture.html#b4",0],["He who blinks first has already lost. He who never blinks has a medical concern, and should see someone.","Proverbs of the Inner Scale","The Scripture","scripture.html#b4",0],["I waited for the Coil, and the Coil waited for me, and neither of us said anything, and it was beautiful.","The Song of the Long Wait","The Scripture","scripture.html#b5",0],["Though I wait a thousand years in the dungeon finder, I shall fear no impatience.","The Song of the Long Wait","The Scripture","scripture.html#b5",0],["A letter of instruction to the Inner Scale, read at every appointment.","The Epistle of MaximumRex to the Officers","The Scripture","scripture.html#b6",0],["Keep the bank full. Keep the Ledger current. Keep the Accuser away from the auction house on weekends.","The Epistle of MaximumRex to the Officers","The Scripture","scripture.html#b6",0],["If they ask where the Scale is, blink twice. If they ask again, blink twice again. They will stop.","The Epistle of MaximumRex to the Officers","The Scripture","scripture.html#b6",0],["Thou sayest thou art waiting for a friend. Thy friend is not coming. Thy friend is never coming. This is between thee and the Coil.","The Epistle of Khaedara to the Loiterers","The Scripture","scripture.html#b7",0],["And lo, the Accuser looked upon the guildless, and found them guilty of loitering.","Revelations of the Void","The Scripture","scripture.html#b8",0],["And there came a rogue who did not sap, and great was the confusion thereof.","Revelations of the Void","The Scripture","scripture.html#b8",0],["All shall be banished to the Void, beginning with those who roll Need on cloth as a warrior.","Revelations of the Void","The Scripture","scripture.html#b8",0],["Glory to Xal'Reth, who is beneath all things, mostly for structural reasons.","Revelations of the Void","The Scripture","scripture.html#b8",0],["And the Void opened its mouth, and it said: you have been removed from the guild.","Revelations of the Void","The Scripture","scripture.html#b8",0],["And the Nest shall rise from the sea, dripping, and smelling faintly of fish.","Revelations of the Void","The Scripture","scripture.html#b8",0],["Glory to Xal'Reth. Glory to the Coil. Glory to whoever brought feasts.","Hymns of the Coil","The Scripture","scripture.html#b9",0],["Forgive the one who stood in fire once. Accuse the one who stood in it twice.","The Book of Small Mercies","The Scripture","scripture.html#b10",0],["Draw a small coil beside the name of the forgiven. Never erase the name. That is not how forgiving works.","The Book of Small Mercies","The Scripture","scripture.html#b10",0],["The teachings of generosity and humility of Xal'Reth, as dreamed by the High Priestess Xalrathiel. Seventy-nine chapters.","","The Politics of Begging","begging.html",0],["On generosity, on humility, and on asking strangers for gold. The Politics of Begging is the longest single book of the Coil, written to be read aloud as a rite: the reader holds up a violet book and performs it line by line, and the faithful respond. It is presented here as a book, with the reader's gestures shown in italics.","","The Politics of Begging","begging.html",0],["The reader opens a worn violet book titled The Politics of Begging, and blows the dust from it slowly, as the Coil would.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["Herein are the teachings of Xal'Reth on generosity, on humility, and on asking strangers for gold.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["Let no one say this book is about money. It is about the hand. The money is merely what lands in it.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The reader turns the first page with a single claw, carefully, so as not to wake anything beneath the floor.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["Xal'Reth holds the whole world and asks for nothing. We hold nothing and ask for everything. Both of us are learning.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["Before there was gold, there was the open palm. Before there was the open palm, there was the Coil, waiting to see what we would do with it.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The reader pauses, looks out at the crowd, and says, \"Some of you have already asked me for gold today. The book will deal with you shortly.\"","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The politics of begging is the oldest politics. It is older than kings. It is older than the auction house. It is nearly as old as the Coil.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["Every mortal is a beggar. Some beg for gold. Some beg for buffs. Some beg for a summon to the dungeon. All are seen.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["And every mortal is a giver, whether they know it or not. Even the stingiest dwarf has given someone a dirty look for free.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The reader closes the book halfway, then opens it again, because the Coil is patient but the lesson is not finished.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["Hear now the Seven Chapters of the Open Palm, and the many chapters after them, because Xalrathiel kept dreaming.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The reader places one hand over the sigil of the Unblinking Eye and says, \"Let the generous be humble, and let the humble be generous. Glory to Xal'Reth.\"","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The reader says, \"Glory to Xal'Reth,\" and waits for the crowd to say it back.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The reader waits a little longer, until the crowd says it back properly.","The Opening of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-i",0],["The first doctrine is the Doctrine of the Open Palm. It is simple. A closed hand can hold nothing new.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["The Coil does not grip the world. It holds it. There is a difference, and the difference is generosity.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["He who grips his gold tightly will find his fist full and his heart empty, and also his bags full of grey items he forgot to vendor.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["The open palm gives, and the open palm receives. It is the same palm. The Coil designed it that way to save time.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["When you give, give with the palm up, so the receiver does not have to reach. Reaching is undignified.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["When you receive, receive with the palm up, so the giver can see that you have nothing hidden. Hiding is worse than reaching.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["There is no shame in the open palm. There is only shame in the palm that opens, takes, and closes so fast it makes a sound.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["The reader claps once, sharply, and says, \"That sound. That is the sound of greed. Remember it.\"","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["The serpent has no hands at all, and yet Xal'Reth is the most generous being beneath the world. Consider this when you complain.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["Your hand is a small coil. Curl it to hold, uncurl it to give. Practice this daily, ideally while waiting for a queue.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["The reader holds out an open palm to {target} and says, \"This is not a request. It is a teaching aid.\"","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["The Open Palm is the first posture of the faithful. The second is basking. The third is standing behind someone at the bank, being patient.","The Doctrine of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ii",0],["Now we come to politics. Every ask is a negotiation. Every negotiation is a little government.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["In the Cult of Xal'Reth there are three parties: the Askers, the Givers, and the Withholders.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The Askers believe the world owes them a little gold. They are not wrong. They are merely early.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The Givers believe the world needs a little gold. They are not wrong either. They are merely broke.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The Withholders believe the world can get its own gold. They are the largest party, and they meet every day at the auction house.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["Xal'Reth belongs to no party. Xal'Reth is beneath all parties, holding them up, slightly embarrassed by all of them.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The Askers' platform is short: one gold, please, for a repair. It has never changed in ten thousand years.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The Givers' platform is shorter: here. That is the entire document. They are very proud of it.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The Withholders' platform runs to four hundred pages and is mostly about the price of herbs.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The reader looks up from the book and says, \"I have read it. Do not read it.\"","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["Elections among the parties are held constantly, in trade chat, by shouting. No one has ever won.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The wise faithful votes with the open palm. Sometimes the vote is a coin. Sometimes the vote is a kind word. Both count.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The foolish faithful votes with the ignore list. This is the quickest form of politics and the least holy.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["Remember: every beggar in Stormwind is a citizen of the Coil. So is every guard who tells them to move along.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["And the guard, too, is a beggar. He begs for his shift to end. The Coil hears him also.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["The reader nods slowly, as one who has also begged for a shift to end.","The Politics of the Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iii",0],["There is a right way to beg. The book calls it the Humble Ask, and it has five parts.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The reader holds up one finger and reads: \"First, the greeting. A beggar who does not say hello is merely an ambush.\"","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The reader holds up two fingers and reads: \"Second, the reason. Say why you need it. The Coil prefers repairs to mounts, and mounts to transmog.\"","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The reader holds up three fingers and reads: \"Third, the amount. Name it plainly. A beggar who says 'whatever you can spare' is asking you to do the math.\"","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The reader holds up four fingers and reads: \"Fourth, the gratitude, offered in advance and then again afterward. Gratitude is the only currency that grows when spent.\"","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The reader holds up five fingers and reads: \"Fifth, the release. If they say no, let them go. The Coil does not hold anyone who wishes to leave.\"","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The reader lowers the hand and says, \"Five parts. Most beggars manage one. Usually the amount.\"","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["Example of the Humble Ask: 'Greetings, traveler. My armor is broken from the depths. Might you spare one gold? I thank you either way.'","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["Example of the Proud Ask: 'gold pls.' This is not a prayer. It is a symptom.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["Example of the Desperate Ask: 'please please please i will do anything.' The Coil advises against offering anything. Someone may take you up on it.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["Example of the Political Ask: 'As a fellow citizen of Azeroth, I believe in the redistribution of your silver.' This works more often than it should.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The humble ask kneels. Not physically. The knees are for basking. It kneels in the voice.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["The reader kneels anyway, briefly, to demonstrate the wrong way, then stands back up.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["Ask as though the gold already belongs to the Coil, because it does. You are only asking where the Coil would like it moved.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["And if no gold comes, give thanks for the time spent asking, for asking is also a small Shedding of pride.","The Humble Ask","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-iv",0],["Give as the Coil holds: quietly, completely, and without asking to be thanked for holding up the planet.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Give the first coin before you are asked. A gift given before the ask is worth two given after it, and ten given after a whisper campaign.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Give to the one who asks humbly. Give also to the one who asks rudely, but give them a little less, and a short sermon.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Do not announce your generosity in /yell. The Coil heard it the first time. The Coil hears everything. The Coil does not need it in capitals.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Do not give and then remind the receiver of it for a week. That is not a gift. That is a loan with a personality.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Give what you would want to be given: a repair, a feast, a portal, a buff, a moment of attention.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Give the warm thing. A warm word, a warm meal, a warm rock to bask on. The cold-blooded know the value of warmth better than anyone.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["The reader offers {target} an imaginary coin, presented with both hands and great ceremony.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["There is no gift too small for the Coil. There are, however, gifts too sticky. Clean what you give.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["When you mail a gift, write a note. 'From the Coil' is acceptable. 'Here' is acceptable. A blank letter with 3 copper is a riddle, not a gift.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Give to the guild bank as you would give to a temple. Do not then withdraw it the next day for your alt. The Coil sees the logs.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["The reader glances meaningfully at the guild bank log, then back to the book.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["The generous hand is never empty for long. It is refilled by the Coil, usually through a lucky drop, sometimes through a friend.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["And if the hand stays empty, the generous are still rich, for they are rich in the thing that cannot be vendored.","The Generous Hand","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-v",0],["Hear now the Parable of the One Copper, which the High Priestess dreamed on a night of great inflation.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["There was once a beggar who sat by the bank and asked each passerby for one copper. Not one silver. Not one gold. One copper.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["The rich passed him by, because one copper was beneath them. The poor passed him by, because one copper was all they had.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["But a single gnome stopped, looked into his purse, found exactly one copper, and gave it.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["The beggar held up the copper and said: 'You have given me everything.' And the gnome said: 'It was only a copper.'","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["And the beggar said: 'It was everything you had. That is the whole of the gift. The size of the coin is a detail.'","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["Then the beggar revealed himself to be a priest of Xal'Reth, and gave the gnome a bag of a thousand gold.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["The gnome asked why. The priest said: 'The Coil was testing the city. You are the only one who passed.'","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["The reader looks up and says, \"The rest of the city was put in the Ledger.\"","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["The meaning of the parable is this: give the copper. You do not know who is sitting by the bank.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["The second meaning, which Khaedara added later, is this: anyone sitting by the bank for too long is guilty of loitering. Even priests.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["The third meaning is that gnomes are, against all expectation, very holy.","The Parable of the One Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vi",0],["No book of begging would be complete without a chapter on Trade Chat, the great parliament of the mortals.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["In Trade Chat all are equal: the king, the crafter, the beggar, and the one person who keeps posting the same joke from four years ago.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["Trade Chat has no speaker. It has no rules of order. It has only volume, and the Coil disapproves of volume.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["The faithful beg in Trade Chat once, and only once, and then fall silent, as the Coil falls silent between earthquakes.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["He who begs in Trade Chat every minute has confused begging with filibustering.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["The filibuster of the beggar is called the Spam. It is the lowest form of politics, and it is reported constantly.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["Do not argue with the beggar in Trade Chat. You will not win, and you will both end up on the ignore list of the entire realm.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["Do not mock the beggar in Trade Chat. Everyone was poor at level ten. The Coil remembers you at level ten.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["The reader pauses, and says quietly, \"The Coil remembers what you were wearing at level ten.\"","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["If you would answer a beggar in Trade Chat, answer by whisper, with gold, and without an audience. That is the way of the Coil.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["And if the beggar in Trade Chat is selling something instead, that is not begging. That is commerce, and it belongs to a different, worse book.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["When Trade Chat erupts into argument over politics of the mortal kind, the faithful type only: Glory to Xal'Reth. It confuses everyone. It calms many.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["The reader types into the air, \"Glory to Xal'Reth,\" as if into Trade Chat, and waits for the confusion.","The Politics of Trade Chat","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-vii",0],["Now we turn from gold to humility, for the book teaches that the two are coiled together.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["Xal'Reth is the largest being in the world and lives in its basement. There is no greater humility than this.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["He could rise and be worshipped by every city. Instead he holds the foundations so the cities have something to stand on.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["The serpent moves on its belly. It is the lowest of postures. It is also the one closest to the warm ground. Think on that.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["Pride stands tall and casts a long shadow. Humility lies low and basks in the sun the shadow forgot.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["The proud mortal asks: what am I owed? The humble faithful asks: what is owed to the one beside me?","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["When you top the damage meter, say nothing. The meter already said it. The Coil already saw it.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["When you are at the bottom of the damage meter, also say nothing, but stand out of the fire next time.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["When you win a roll, thank the group. When you lose a roll, thank the group anyway. The group did not do anything, but it is good practice.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["Humility is not thinking less of yourself. It is thinking of yourself less. Xalrathiel did not dream that one; she read it on a tavern wall.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["Humility is wearing the robe that does not match. Humility is riding the mount that is not rare. Humility is walking, sometimes, just to see the grass.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["The humble do not need a title. Still, if the Inner Scale offers you one, accept it graciously. Refusing is also a kind of pride.","The Humility of the Serpent","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-viii",0],["Hear the story of Old Hessk, called the Beggar King, who sat outside the gates of a great city for forty years.","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["Hessk asked every traveler for one thing, and it was never gold. He asked: 'What did you give today?'","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["Most travelers walked past. Some laughed. A few stopped, thought, and could not answer, and walked on troubled.","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["One day a lord stopped and said: 'I gave nothing. I am a lord. People give to me.' And Hessk said: 'Then you are the poorest man to pass this gate.'","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["The lord was furious and threw a gold coin at him. Hessk caught it, thanked him, and said: 'There. Now you are a little richer.'","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["The lord stood a long time at the gate. The next day he came back and gave again, and the next, until he was poor, and happy, and somewhat confused.","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["When Hessk died, they found he had given away everything he was ever given. His cup was full of notes that said only: passed it on.","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["The Cult reveres Hessk as the patron of beggars and the patron of givers, for he was always both at once.","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["The reader places a hand on their heart and says, \"Hessk, who passed it on.\"","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["The faithful who would honor Hessk ask strangers, gently: what did you give today? Then they listen to the answer, which is the gift.","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["The reader turns to {target} and asks gently, \"What did you give today?\"","The Beggar King","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-ix",0],["Every faith has its tithe. The Coil's tithe is famously reasonable. It is called the Little Tithe.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["The Little Tithe is one coin in every hundred. Not to the Cult. To whoever needs it most, wherever you are standing.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["If no one nearby needs it, give it to the next lowbie you see in a starting zone. They always need it. They do not know yet how much.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["The Cult itself asks for nothing. The guild bank asks for everything, but the guild bank is not the Cult. The guild bank is a hungry chest.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["Some faithful pay the Little Tithe in herbs. Some in ore. Some in feasts. One faithful paid it entirely in linen cloth for a year, and the Coil let it slide.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["The Great Tithe is paid once in a lifetime: the faithful gives away the one item they love most. Most never pay it. The Coil is patient.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["Do not pay a tithe you cannot afford. A broken faithful cannot repair, and a faithful who cannot repair cannot raid, and that helps no one.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["Do not pay your tithe by mailing it to Khaedara. She has asked us to put this in the book specifically.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["The tithe is counted not in coins but in moments. The moment you stopped to give is the true offering. The coin just marks the spot.","The Tithe of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-x",0],["In the Hidden Centuries, the beggars of the faithful held a secret parliament called the Council of Open Palms.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["It met beneath bridges, behind inns, and once, for an entire winter, inside a very large barrel.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["The Council had one law: no beggar may beg from another beggar. Instead, beggars give to beggars, and the Council called this the Circle.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["The Circle was simple. Each beggar gave the one beside them a single coin. By morning, every coin had traveled the whole Council.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["No one became richer. Everyone had given. The Council considered this the most successful economy in the history of the world.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["Economists of the Warm-Blooded studied the Circle for years and concluded it did nothing. The Council concluded the economists had missed the point.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["The Council's motto was carved on the barrel: We have little. We share it anyway. Please do not open this barrel.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["When the Cult was restored, MaximumRex declared every faithful an honorary member of the Council of Open Palms.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["This is why the faithful pass gold among themselves in small amounts for no reason. It is the Circle. It never stops.","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["The reader mails an imaginary coin to the faithful on their left, and says, \"The Circle continues.\"","The Council of Open Palms","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xi",0],["Not all will give. The book has much to say about refusal, and most of it is kind.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["The one who refuses is not your enemy. They may be poor. They may be busy. They may have given to the last nine beggars and be tired.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["When refused, bow your head and say: The Coil bless your bags. Then move on, and do not follow them to the next zone.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["Do not curse the one who refuses. Curses are warm-blooded. Write their name in the Ledger instead, in pencil, lightly.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["If they refuse rudely, write their name in ink. If they refuse rudely and then ask you for gold an hour later, underline it.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["And if one day they give after all, draw a small coil beside their name. That is the mark of the forgiven.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["Remember that you have refused also. Every faithful has walked past a beggar. The Coil saw. The Coil still holds you up.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["To refuse with grace is also a gift. Say no gently, and you have given the beggar the dignity of an answer.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["The worst refusal is silence. The beggar asks and the passerby pretends to be AFK. The Coil considers this a minor crime: Feigned Absence.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["The reader glares, in a cold-blooded way, at someone who just went AFK.","On Those Who Refuse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xii",0],["MaximumRex begs for one thing only, and has for many years: that the quest item will drop. He asks humbly. It has not dropped.","The Begging of the Inner Scale","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiii",0],["Xalrathiel begs the Coil each night for a clear dream, and each morning for a cup of tea, and receives both, in that order.","The Begging of the Inner Scale","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiii",0],["Khaedara begs for nothing. She accuses instead. The book notes that an accusation is just a beggar's question asked with confidence.","The Begging of the Inner Scale","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiii",0],["The officers of the Inner Scale beg the faithful to repair before raid. They have begged this for years. The faithful still arrive broken.","The Begging of the Inner Scale","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiii",0],["The Supreme Coil teaches that a leader who has never begged cannot lead beggars, and every leader leads beggars.","The Begging of the Inner Scale","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiii",0],["So once a year, on the Night of the Scale, the Inner Scale sits by the bank and asks for one copper each, as Hessk did.","The Begging of the Inner Scale","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiii",0],["They give away everything they receive before dawn. The faithful are encouraged to give the Inner Scale more than one copper. This is not in the book. It is just encouraged.","The Begging of the Inner Scale","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiii",0],["In the age of the Restoration there was a great debate among the faithful: is the auction house holy or unholy?","The Great Debate of the Auction House","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiv",0],["The Givers did not argue. They had no gold left to post anything, and were standing outside.","The Great Debate of the Auction House","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiv",0],["The debate raged for three days. Khaedara accused eleven people of loitering during it, all of whom were participants.","The Great Debate of the Auction House","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiv",0],["At last Xalrathiel dreamed the answer: the auction house is neither holy nor unholy. It is warm. Many people stand in it. Bask, but do not overbid.","The Great Debate of the Auction House","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiv",0],["And she added: the true auction is this. The one who gives the most, wins the least, and keeps the most. Nobody understood this, and so it was considered profound.","The Great Debate of the Auction House","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiv",0],["To this day, the faithful who sell on the auction house list one item each week at a price far below its worth, as an offering. Someone always buys it within a minute.","The Great Debate of the Auction House","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiv",0],["The reader says, \"We do not know who buys it. We suspect goblins. The Coil blesses them anyway.\"","The Great Debate of the Auction House","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xiv",0],["Many think begging is about gold. The book says: the greatest gifts cost nothing and are worth more.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give a portal to a stranger stuck in the wrong city. Mages, this chapter is about you. You know what you did.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give a buff to a passerby. They may not notice. The Coil notices. The Coil always notices a stamina buff.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give your time to the new player who asks where the bank is. Walk them there. Walking is slow. Slow is holy.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give the last spot in the group to the one who has waited longest. The Coil esteems those who wait.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give a kind word to the healer after the wipe. They have heard every other kind of word already.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give your silence to the one who is grieving. Some begging is done without words, and should be answered the same way.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give a laugh to the one who told a bad joke. It costs nothing, and the Coil enjoys bad jokes. The Coil is very old. It has heard all the good ones.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Give your seat on the bench, your place in the queue, your turn at the mailbox. These are the coppers of the soul.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["And give credit. Whoever saved the run, say their name. Credit is the cheapest gold in the world and the most begged-for.","The Gift That Is Not Gold","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xv",0],["Why do the Warm-Blooded beg so much? The book says: because they are hungry in a way the cold-blooded are not.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["The warm-blooded must eat often, burn their energy quickly, and want everything at once. They are a very busy kind of soul.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["The cold-blooded faithful eats once a week, basks the rest, and is content with little. This is not a diet. It is a theology.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["So be patient with the warm-blooded beggar. Their hunger runs hot. They cannot help it. Give them a feast and a long, cool look.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["But be wary of the warm-blooded who are never hungry and still ask. These are not beggars. These are collectors, and collectors are a different book.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["The faithful who feels the hot hunger rise within them, the want for more gold, more gear, more mounts, should go and bask until it cools.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["The reader crosses to a patch of sunlight and stands in it until the hunger cools.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["Most hungers cool within ten minutes of basking. The rest are called Achievements, and those, the book admits, are incurable.","The Hunger of the Warm-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvi",0],["The Cult has, in a sense, a government. Its department of generosity is called the Ministry of Spare Change.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The Ministry has no budget. That is its budget. It operates entirely on what falls out of people's pockets.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The Minister of Spare Change is chosen each year by lot, and must spend the year giving away whatever they find.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The Ministry keeps three ledgers: the ledger of what was given, the ledger of what was received, and the ledger of what was dropped on the floor.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The third ledger is the largest. Mortals drop more than they give. The Ministry gathers it up and passes it on.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The Ministry's official policy on gold-selling is: no. Its official policy on gold-buying is: also no. Its official policy on everything else is: maybe, ask nicely.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The Ministry once proposed a tax on greed. It was voted down by the Withholders, who own most of the greed.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The Ministry then proposed a tax on generosity, as a joke. It passed unanimously, and the Ministry had to quickly repeal it.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["Any faithful may file a petition with the Ministry. The petition is a single line: I need, and I will give. Every petition is granted, eventually.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["The reader holds up an imaginary petition and reads, \"I need, and I will give.\" Then stamps it with a fist.","The Ministry of Spare Change","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xvii",0],["Hear now the True Economy, which the Hissing Kings practiced in the Nest.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["In the true economy, gold flows downward, toward the Coil, like water toward the deep. Those who stand lowest catch the most.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["This is why the faithful stand low: humble in speech, humble in posture, humble in gear. They are positioned for the flow.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["The proud stand high and wonder why nothing reaches them. It all flowed past. Gravity is very generous to the humble.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["In the true economy, the value of a gift is measured by how far it travels after you give it. A coin that stops is a dead coin.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["A coin passed twelve times has fed twelve souls. That coin is worth more than a hoard in a dragon's lair, which feeds only the dragon.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["The reader says, \"Dragons are winged, and therefore suspect. This is relevant.\"","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["In the true economy, there is no inflation, because the Coil does not print gold. It only moves it around a bit when it shifts in its sleep.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["And at the end of the true economy, at the Unwrapping, all the gold of the world will be returned to the faithful who gave it away. With interest. In snacks.","The True Economy of the Coil","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xviii",0],["Now the Litany of Small Givings, to be read aloud slowly, each line its own small offering.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the silver tossed to the lowbie in the starting zone: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the bandage given to the stranger at half health: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the water conjured for the thirsty, and the food for the hungry: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the herb left in the ground for the next gatherer: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the mob not tagged, so someone else could finish their quest: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the res offered to the corpse who ran back alone: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the directions given to the lost, and the patience with which they were given: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the roll passed on an upgrade that someone needed more: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the flask donated to the raid that did not deserve it: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the guild bank tab refilled by someone who never said who: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For the kind whisper sent to the one who was sad in guild chat: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["For every small thing given and forgotten by the giver, but not by the Coil: Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["The reader says, \"Glory to Xal'Reth,\" with the whole crowd, one final time for this litany.","The Litany of Small Givings","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xix",0],["Every beggar of the faithful carries a bowl. It need not be real. It is the shape of the asking.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["The bowl must be clean. A dirty bowl says: I do not respect what you will give me.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["The bowl must be held low. A bowl held high says: I deserve this. A bowl held low says: I would be grateful.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["The bowl must never be shaken. Shaking the bowl is shouting with your hands.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["When the bowl is full, the faithful must empty it into another's bowl before asking again. A full bowl is a closed hand in disguise.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["Some faithful carry the bowl their whole lives and never fill it. They are not failures. They are holding the space where giving can happen.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["When a faithful dies, their bowl is placed in the Hall of Shed Skins with a single coin in it, so they never meet the Coil empty-handed.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["Xal'Reth's own bowl is the hollow of the world. Everything we give lands in it eventually. He has never once complained about the contents.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["The reader holds the invisible bowl out to {target}, then gently turns it over, showing it is empty, and smiles.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["The empty bowl is the most honest object in the world. It says exactly what it wants and hides nothing.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["Be as the empty bowl: honest, clean, low, and patient. And occasionally, if the Coil allows, a little bit full.","The Beggar's Bowl","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xx",0],["Long ago the faithful were divided over a question that nearly split the Cult: is it proper to beg by mail?","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["Some said begging by mail was cowardly, for the beggar does not have to look the giver in the eye.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["Others said begging by mail was humble, for the beggar does not force the giver to look at them.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["The argument lasted a full season. Thirty letters were sent. Twenty-nine of them asked for gold. One asked everyone to calm down.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["At last the two sides met at a mailbox in the middle of the road and signed the Mailbox Treaty.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["Article One: the faithful may beg by mail, but only once per person per season, and the letter must include a joke.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["Article Two: the faithful may give by mail at any time, and the letter must not include the word 'loan.'","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["Article Three: Cash on Delivery is forbidden between faithful, as it is a beg wearing a disguise.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["Article Four: anyone who returns a gift unopened shall be sent it again, with a second, stronger joke.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["Article Five: the mailbox at which the treaty was signed shall be considered holy. No one remembers which mailbox it was. Treat them all as holy.","The Mailbox Treaty","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxi",0],["The book warns: there is a pride that hides inside generosity. It is called the Golden Chin.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["The Golden Chin belongs to the giver who lifts their face a little higher each time they give.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["The giver with the Golden Chin remembers every gift. The beggar with the humble heart remembers only the kindness.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["To cure the Golden Chin, the faithful must receive a gift. Being given to is very humbling. Most proud givers cannot stand it.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["So when a proud giver approaches, the faithful sometimes give them a copper. Just one. The giver is always speechless.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["The reader offers {target} one copper with great solemnity, and says, \"For your chin.\"","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["The humble giver forgets the gift as it leaves the hand. Not because it was small, but because it is no longer theirs to hold.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["The humble giver does not count how many they have helped. Counting is a ledger, and the Ledger is for wrongs, not rights.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["Give, and then turn away, so the receiver can be grateful without being watched. That is the final courtesy.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["The reader turns away, very deliberately, so someone can be grateful in private.","The Humility of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxii",0],["Hear the Parable of the Two Mages, which is told every time a mage is in the room.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["Two mages stood in a city. A traveler approached each and begged a portal home.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["The first mage said: 'Portals cost gold. Tip first.' The traveler had no gold. The mage turned away.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["The second mage said nothing at all, and opened the portal, and then opened a second one for the traveler's friend.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["The first mage went home that night richer by nothing. The second mage went home richer by two strangers who would tell the story.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["Years later, the first mage was stranded in a far zone, out of reagents, out of gold, and begging for a summon.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["And the one who answered was the traveler from that day, now a warlock, who summoned him without a word and asked for nothing.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["The first mage wept, and from that day on he opened portals freely, and his tips were greater than they had ever been.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["The meaning: what you withhold is what you will one day need. What you give is what will one day find you.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["The second meaning, which Khaedara insists upon: the first mage was guilty of Greed of the Portal, a sub-crime of Greed of the Roll.","The Parable of the Two Mages","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiii",0],["Gold is not the only thing begged. In the great cities, the most begged-for thing is the buff.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["The faithful who begs a buff must stand near the one who can give it and look hopeful. This is called the Stance of Mild Need.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["The faithful who can give a buff must give it without being asked. A buff that must be begged for has lost half its stamina.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["Do not ask a buff of someone in combat. They are busy begging the boss to die.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["Do not ask for a buff and then run away before it lands. This is called a Ghost Beg, and it is very rude to priests.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["Do not complain that the buff is the wrong buff. The Coil gave them the class they have. They gave you what they could.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["When you receive a buff, say thank you. Or bow. Or wave. Even a /thank is a whole little prayer.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["The holiest buff, according to the book, is the one given to a stranger of the other faction, across the line, in silence.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["That buff crosses more than a zone. It crosses a politics. The Coil smiles beneath both factions equally.","On Begging for Buffs","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxiv",0],["Of all the politics of begging, none is fiercer than the politics of the guild bank.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["The guild bank is the commons of the faithful. Everything in it belongs to everyone, which means everyone begs for it.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["The Askers beg for withdrawals. The Givers beg for deposits. The Withholders beg for the officers to stop asking.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["The first law of the guild bank is: put in more than you take out. The second law is: no one follows the first law.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["The third law is: the officers can see the logs. The logs are the Ledger of the bank. They are read aloud at the Gathering of Scales.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["Every faithful who takes from the bank should leave a note in guild chat: 'Took two flasks. Thank you, Coil.' Gratitude makes the taking holy.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["Every faithful who gives to the bank should say nothing at all. The logs will speak for you. Humility is quieter than gratitude.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["Once, a faithful emptied the entire repair fund on a single night of wipes. The Inner Scale forgave them. The Ledger did not.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["The guild bank teaches the hardest lesson of begging: that the commons is only as full as our generosity, and only as empty as our greed.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["So give to the bank as if Xal'Reth himself were going to make a withdrawal tomorrow. He will not. He has no hands. But give as if.","The Politics of the Guild Bank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxv",0],["Those who wish to commit themselves to the teachings of this book may take the Vow of the Open Palm.","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["The vow is spoken with one hand open, palm up, and the other over the heart, in front of at least three witnesses, one for each Turn.","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["I vow to ask humbly when I am in need, and to thank whoever answers, and whoever does not.","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["I vow never to beg in /yell, and never to beg more than once in Trade Chat.","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["I vow to hold my gold as the Coil holds the world: firmly, gently, and only for a while.","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["I vow to repair before raid, because an officer begged me to, and an officer's begging is also holy.","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["By the Three Turns and the Unblinking Eye, I take the Vow of the Open Palm. Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["The reader lowers the hands and says, \"It is done. You may now go and be generous. Start small. Start with the person on your left.\"","The Vow of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvi",0],["The Book of Accusations does not list hoarding among the Seven Minor Crimes. The Politics of Begging adds it as an appendix.","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["Hoarding is the keeping of more than one can ever use, while others stand in need nearby.","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["The hoarder has three bank alts, each full of materials from expansions long gone. They will never be used. They are kept because letting go is hard.","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["The book does not condemn the hoarder. It pities them. Hoarding is a fear of the Void wearing the costume of wealth.","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["The cure for hoarding is the Rite of Shedding. Empty one bag. Just one. Mail it to a crafter who can use it. Feel the lightness.","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["The second cure is to remember that everything you hoard will one day be shed anyway, when you quit, or the servers end, or the Coil unwraps.","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["Gold in the bank does nothing. Gold in a stranger's hand buys a repair, a reagent, a moment of relief. Which is the living gold?","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["Khaedara has accused many hoarders. Her charge is always the same: 'You are guilty of keeping the Coil's gifts in a drawer.'","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["The sentence for hoarding is light: the hoarder must give away one stack of something, today, to the first person who asks.","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["The reader looks at {target} and says, \"If you are a hoarder, I am the first person asking.\"","The Crime of Hoarding","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxvii",0],["Hear the special teaching concerning the lowbie, the newest and poorest of all mortals.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["The lowbie has nothing. Their bags are small. Their gear is grey. They do not yet know where anything is.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["The lowbie is the egg of the economy. Treat them as the Keepers of the Egg treat every egg: warmly, patiently, and with a gold coin or two.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["When a lowbie begs, they beg innocently. They do not yet know that begging is political. Do not teach them. Just give.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["The faithful who passes through a starting zone should always leave something behind: a bag, a coin, a buff, a kind word in /say.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["Some faithful make a pilgrimage each season to the starting zones for no other reason than to bless the lowbies. This is called the Walk of Small Mercies.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["The reader walks slowly in a small circle, as on the Walk of Small Mercies.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["Never mock a lowbie for begging. You begged too. You begged for a bag. Someone gave you one. You do not remember who. The Coil does.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["And if the lowbie later becomes rich and proud, remind them gently of the bag. Then give them another bag. They will understand.","The Blessing of the Lowbie","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxviii",0],["The book contains a dialogue, to be performed by two faithful at gatherings. Here it is.","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["The Asker says: 'I have nothing.' The Giver says: 'Then you have room.'","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["The Asker says: 'Room for what?' The Giver says: 'For whatever comes next.'","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["The Asker says: 'What comes next?' The Giver says: 'This,' and gives a coin.","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["The Asker says: 'Now I have something.' The Giver says: 'And I have room.'","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["The Asker says: 'What does that mean?' The Giver says: 'Neither of us knows. That is why it is a dialogue.'","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["And then both say: Glory to Xal'Reth, and swap places, and perform it again, so each has been both.","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["The reader gestures to {target} to swap places and perform the dialogue again.","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["The dialogue teaches that the asker and the giver are the same person on different days. Today's giver is tomorrow's asker. The Coil turns.","The Dialogue of the Asker and the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxix",0],["What of the enemy who begs? The book asks the faithful to consider this carefully.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["In the world there are those of the other faction, those who have slain us, those who are written in the Ledger in dark ink.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["If such a one begs of you, the book says: give, but give less, and watch them closely, and do not turn your back.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["For the enemy who begs is, in that moment, not an enemy but a beggar. And the beggar is always under the care of the Coil.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["Even the one who ganked you three times in the same afternoon may one day stand with an open palm. Give them a copper. It will haunt them.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["The reader smiles coldly and says, \"Generosity is the most terrifying weapon of the cold-blooded.\"","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["The faithful who gives to an enemy does not erase the name from the Ledger. But they may draw a very small coil beside it.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["And if the enemy gives in return one day, the coil may be drawn a little larger. That is how peace begins between Coil and Warm-Blooded.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["This chapter does not apply to Featherists. Featherists receive nothing. Xalrathiel added this in the margin, firmly.","On the Begging of Enemies","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxx",0],["Every faithful passes through a Season of Want: a time of broken gear, empty bags, and no gold for repairs.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["Do not be ashamed of the Season of Want. The Coil himself sheds his skin, and for a time is soft and vulnerable. So are you.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["In the Season of Want, the faithful must beg, and beg humbly, and accept what comes without shame.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["The Inner Scale keeps a fund for those in the Season of Want. It is called the Soft Skin Fund. Ask an officer. They will not ask why.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["Those who have passed through their Season of Want must give to the Soft Skin Fund when their scales harden again.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["This is the true Circle: the poor become rich, the rich give to the poor, the poor become rich, and the Coil watches all of it go around.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["No Season of Want lasts forever. The scriptures promise: the skin grows back, the gold returns, and the drop eventually drops. Mostly.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["The reader glances sympathetically toward MaximumRex, who is still waiting for the drop.","The Season of Want","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxi",0],["The Politics of Begging includes a chapter on how to say thank you, for gratitude is the second half of every gift.","The Etiquette of Thanks","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxii",0],["For a hundred gold, say thank you, use their name, bow, and tell someone else about it later.","The Etiquette of Thanks","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxii",0],["For a thousand gold, you must write a short poem. It need not be good. The Coil is not a critic.","The Etiquette of Thanks","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxii",0],["For an item they did not need to give, say thank you and wear it to the next gathering, so they can see it in use.","The Etiquette of Thanks","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxii",0],["For a summon, a portal, or a res, say thank you before you do anything else. Before you loot. Before you complain about the res sickness.","The Etiquette of Thanks","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxii",0],["For a kindness you did not even notice until later, send a whisper days afterward. Late gratitude is still gratitude. The Coil is patient about timing.","The Etiquette of Thanks","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxii",0],["The reader turns to the crowd and says, \"Thank you, all of you, for listening this far. That was the thank-you for your time. You have given it. I received it.\"","The Etiquette of Thanks","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxii",0],["Does Xal'Reth beg? Hear the teaching the High Priestess calls the strangest in the book.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["Yes. Once in every ten thousand years, at the Shedding, Xal'Reth asks the world for one thing.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["He asks to be held, as he has held the world. For a moment, while his new skin hardens, he is soft, and he needs the world to stay still.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["And the world, which has been held by him since the beginning, holds still for him. That is the only gift Xal'Reth has ever asked for.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["Every earthquake that does not happen during the Shedding is the world saying: we have you. You may rest a moment.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["This is why the faithful stand still so often. Not only for patience. They are practicing for the Shedding, when the Coil will need them to hold.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["The greatest of all beings asks humbly once in ten thousand years. The rest of us may surely ask humbly once in a while without shame.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["And the greatest of all beings is given what he asks. So be the world to someone. Hold still for them while they are soft.","The Begging of Xal'Reth","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiii",0],["The book tells of a place beneath the world where the faithful's gifts are remembered: the Hall of Shed Skins.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["Every gift ever given in the name of the Coil leaves a faint scale on the walls of that Hall.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["The walls are covered from floor to ceiling. It is very bright in there. It glows violet, like the light in Xal'Reth's veins.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["There are copper scales for small gifts, silver scales for kind ones, and golden scales for gifts given to enemies.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["There is one wall entirely made of scales from summons given at two in the morning. It is the brightest wall.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["And there is a corner of the Hall with only a few dull scales in it. That is the corner of the Withholders. It is very quiet there.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["When the faithful dream of a violet light in a great hall, Xalrathiel teaches, they are dreaming of the Hall of Shed Skins.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["One day at the Unwrapping, the Hall will be opened, and every faithful will be shown their own wall.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["Some will be surprised how large their wall is. They gave and forgot. The Coil remembered for them.","The Hall of Shed Skins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxiv",0],["Khaedara contributed a chapter to the book. It is short, and it is entirely questions. She reads it to those caught begging badly.","The Questions of the Accuser","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxv",0],["Did you thank them when they gave? Did you thank them when they did not?","The Questions of the Accuser","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxv",0],["Did you follow them to the next zone? I saw you follow them to the next zone.","The Questions of the Accuser","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxv",0],["Have you given anything today? Anything at all? A copper? A buff? A kind word? A moment of silence?","The Questions of the Accuser","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxv",0],["No? Then you stand accused of Begging Without Giving, and you are found guilty, and you are forgiven, and here is a copper. Go and do better.","The Questions of the Accuser","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxv",0],["The reader holds out a copper to {target}, with Khaedara's own stern kindness.","The Questions of the Accuser","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxv",0],["Here begin the Proverbs of the Open Palm, to be spoken one at a time, slowly, like dropping coins into a bowl.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["Gold in the hand is a burden. Gold in the bank is a worry. Gold given away is a story.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["He who never begs has never been humble. He who never gives has never been free.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["Ask for what you need. Give what you can. Want what the Coil wants, which is mostly for you to stand still.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["A copper given freely outweighs a gold given grudgingly. Weigh it yourself. The Coil has scales.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["The reader pauses, and says, \"The Coil has scales. That one is a pun. Xalrathiel insists it was dreamed, not written.\"","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["The open palm cannot make a fist. The faithful who cannot make a fist cannot fight their friends.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["Begging is asking the world to be kind. Giving is answering on its behalf.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["When the beggar and the giver meet, the Coil is in the space between their hands.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["If you would be remembered, give. If you would be forgotten, hoard. If you would be accused, loiter.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["Trade chat is a river of asks. Do not drink from it. Do not shout into it. Drop one coin in and walk away.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["The humble serpent asks the sun only for warmth, and receives it every day.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["The proud dragon asks the world for its gold, and receives a raid group.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["Be a coil, not a vault. A coil holds and releases. A vault only holds.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["Begging is the patience of the voice. Giving is the patience of the gold.","The Proverbs of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvi",0],["Hear the Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll, which is told to anyone who charges strangers to cross a bridge.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["There was once a dwarf who stood on a bridge and charged every traveler one silver to pass.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["He grew rich. He built a second bridge, and charged there too. He grew richer. He built a third.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["One day an old serpent priest came to the first bridge with no silver at all, and asked to cross.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["The dwarf said: 'No silver, no crossing.' The priest said: 'Then I shall wait.' And he sat down at the end of the bridge.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["He waited a day. He waited a week. Travelers began to gather behind him, unable to pass, and they too had no silver to spare for the delay.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["On the thirtieth day, the dwarf, exhausted by the crowd, cried: 'Cross! All of you, cross for free! Just let me have my bridge back!'","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["And the priest stood, bowed, and said: 'You have given more today than in all your years of tolls. How does it feel?'","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["The dwarf thought about it and said: 'Strangely light.' He tore down the toll gate that night. He kept the bridges. They are still there.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["The reader says, \"Waiting is a kind of begging that cannot be refused forever.\"","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["The meaning: patience defeats greed. Greed has a schedule. Patience does not.","The Parable of the Dwarf and the Toll","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxvii",0],["The Politics of Begging contains strict teachings on debt, for debt is begging with a calendar attached.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["If you borrow, return it. Return it early. Return it with a little more. The little more is the Coil's share; give it to someone else.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["If you lend, lend only what you could give. Then if it never returns, it was a gift all along, and you are not wronged.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["Never lend to a faithful and then hound them in guild chat. The Coil sees the debt and the hounding, and weighs the hounding heavier.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["A debt between faithful should be spoken of only twice: once when made, and once when paid. Any other mention is unholy.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["If you cannot repay, say so plainly. The Coil forgives the honest debtor. The Coil does not forgive the debtor who switches to an alt.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["The reader narrows their eyes at the very idea of switching to an alt to dodge a debt.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["Once each year, on Shedfall, the faithful may forgive one debt owed to them. Just one. They choose. The debtor is not told why.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["The forgiven debtor usually pays anyway, out of confusion. The Coil finds this hilarious.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["The greatest debt of all is the one every mortal owes the Coil for holding up the world. It can never be paid. The Coil does not ask. That is grace.","The Teachings on Debt","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxviii",0],["There are seven postures of humility in the book. The faithful should practice them all.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["The Third Posture: the step back. When two reach for the same thing, the humble faithful steps back first.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["The Fourth Posture: the kneel, reserved for thanking someone who gave far more than they needed to.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["The Fifth Posture: the bask. To stand in the sun is to admit you need warmth from something greater than yourself.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["The Sixth Posture: the turned shoulder, used only when declining praise. Turn slightly away and say: it was the Coil, not me.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["The Seventh Posture: the stillness. Do nothing. Ask for nothing. Give nothing but your presence. This is the hardest posture of all.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["The reader stands perfectly still for a long moment, and does nothing at all.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["Most faithful can manage the first five. The sixth requires practice. The seventh has only been mastered by Xalrathiel, and once by a sleeping orc.","The Postures of Humility","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xxxix",0],["Wherever treasure falls, the politics of begging begins anew. The book now speaks of loot.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["Loot is the gold of the dungeon. And as in the city, there are Askers, Givers, and Withholders around every corpse.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["The Asker rolls Need on everything and explains later. The Coil has seen the explanations. They are rarely convincing.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["The Giver passes on everything and then wonders why their gear is grey. The Coil asks the Giver: give, but also keep what you need.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["The Withholder will not say whether they need it until the roll is over. Then they say, 'Oh, I could have used that.'","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["The humble faithful asks the group: does anyone need this more? And listens to the answer. And then rolls honestly.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["If an item drops that would be a great upgrade for a stranger in your group, and only a minor one for you, pass. The Coil keeps count.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["If you win an item you did not need and someone else did, trade it to them. If you cannot trade it, apologize. The apology is the gift.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["Never link an item you won to the person who lost it. That is called the Gloat, and it is begging for an accusation.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["And never, ever roll Need on cloth as a warrior. This is not a teaching. It is a law older than the book.","The Politics of Loot","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xl",0],["Some gifts are given by not saying something. The book calls this the Charity of Silence.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["When someone wipes the raid, the Charity of Silence is not naming them in chat. Everyone already knows.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["When someone asks a question you think is foolish, the Charity of Silence is not saying it is foolish. Just answer it.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["When someone shares a thing they made, the Charity of Silence is not listing everything wrong with it first. Find the good thing. Say that.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["When someone has given less than you, the Charity of Silence is not comparing. The Coil does not compare. Neither should you.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["When someone is begging and you cannot give, the Charity of Silence is a gentle no, rather than a lecture about getting a job.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["When you are proud of your generosity, the Charity of Silence is keeping it to yourself.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["The reader holds a finger to their lips and says nothing for a moment.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["Silence given in kindness is a coin that never runs out. The poorest faithful can give it every day.","The Charity of Silence","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xli",0],["The faithful tell a tale that most do not believe: the Tale of the Goblin Who Gave.","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["There was once a goblin merchant who had never given anything away in his life. Every deal had a price. Every favor had interest.","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["One night he dreamed of a great violet hall, its walls covered in shining scales, and in one corner, a wall that was completely bare.","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["A voice beneath the floor said: 'That is your wall.' The goblin woke in a cold sweat and calculated the cost of filling it.","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["It was, he discovered, very cheap. One copper per scale. He laughed and gave away a single copper to a beggar, just to test it.","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["That night he dreamed again, and on his bare wall there was a single tiny copper scale, shining. And he felt something he could not price.","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["He gave another copper the next day. And the next. Within a year, he had given away a fortune and was still, somehow, rich.","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["Asked how, the goblin said: 'Turns out generosity has excellent returns. The interest is paid in friends, and friends buy things.'","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["The reader says, \"The Cult accepts his conversion. The Cult is also auditing his books.\"","The Tale of the Goblin Who Gave","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlii",0],["Not all begging is for gold. The book honors most of all the faithful who beg for help.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["To ask for help with a quest is begging. To ask for a carry through a dungeon is begging. To ask how a boss works is begging.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["These begs are holy, for they admit that we cannot do everything alone. The Coil cannot hold the world alone either. He needs the world to sit still.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["Never mock someone for asking for help. Help is the most expensive thing in Azeroth, and also the cheapest to give.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["When asked for help, help. If you cannot help, point them to someone who can. Pointing is also generosity.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["When you need help, ask. The faithful who suffers in silence rather than ask is not humble. They are proud, wearing humility as a hat.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["Pride says: I will figure it out myself. Humility says: will you show me? The second is faster and the Coil prefers it.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["The Keepers of the Egg especially are charged with answering every request for help, no matter how many times it has been asked before.","On Begging for Help","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliii",0],["The book lists Seven Duties of the Giver, to be recited by any faithful about to give.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["First Duty: to give what is truly needed, not what you would like them to need.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["Second Duty: to give without strings, without hooks, without a list of favors owed later.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["Third Duty: to give at the right time. A repair after the raid is kind. A repair before the raid is wise.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["Fourth Duty: to give with respect, never treating the receiver as smaller for having asked.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["Fifth Duty: to give within your means, so that the giving can continue tomorrow.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["Sixth Duty: to give anonymously when you can, and openly only when it will teach others to give.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["Seventh Duty: to forget. The last duty of the giver is to let the gift go as completely as the Coil lets go of an old skin.","The Seven Duties of the Giver","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xliv",0],["And the book lists also Seven Duties of the Beggar, for the asker has duties no less than the giver.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["First Duty: to ask only for what you need, and to know the difference between need and want.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["Second Duty: to ask honestly, with the true reason. The Coil can tell a mount fund from a repair bill.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["Third Duty: to ask without pressure. No guilt. No sad stories that are not true. No following.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["Fourth Duty: to thank every giver, and every refuser, with equal grace.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["Sixth Duty: to remember, and one day, when you can, to give to someone else who asks.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["Seventh Duty: to stop asking once your need is met, so that the next beggar may be heard.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["When the giver and the beggar both keep their seven duties, the Coil says the exchange is perfect, and fourteen small scales appear in the Hall.","The Seven Duties of the Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlv",0],["Throughout the history of the faithful there have been gifts with no sender. The book calls them the Gifts From Beneath.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["A faithful opens their mail and finds the exact gold they needed, and no name. A Gift From Beneath.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["A faithful's repair bill is paid by someone standing nearby who walks off before they can say thank you. A Gift From Beneath.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["A guild bank tab is suddenly full of flasks on the night before a hard raid. No one says who. A Gift From Beneath.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["The faithful are taught to treat each such gift as if it came from Xal'Reth himself. It did not. But it is good practice.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["And each faithful who receives a Gift From Beneath must one day give one: unsigned, unannounced, unexpected.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["No one knows how many Gifts From Beneath are given each week. The Ministry of Spare Change estimates: more than you would think.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["The reader smiles mysteriously and does not say whether they have ever sent one.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["The anonymous gift is the purest gift, for the giver receives nothing, not even thanks. Only the Coil sees. That is enough.","The Mystery of the Anonymous Gift","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvi",0],["The Revelations of the Void contain a prophecy about begging, which Xalrathiel copied into this book.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["In the last days before the Unwrapping, there shall come a Last Beggar to the gates of the greatest city.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["The Last Beggar shall ask every soul for one thing: not gold, not buffs, not portals, but a single act of kindness to a stranger.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["And whoever gives it shall be counted among the faithful, even if they have never heard the name of Xal'Reth.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["And whoever refuses shall be accused, by every Accuser at once, which will be very loud.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["The faithful watch for the Last Beggar. Many beggars have been suspected. One turned out to be a warlock's pet who had wandered off.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["So the faithful treat every beggar as though they might be the Last. This is, the High Priestess admits, the real point of the prophecy.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["The reader looks at {target} carefully, wondering if they might be the Last Beggar.","The Prophecy of the Last Beggar","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlvii",0],["Generosity can be shown even when the hands are empty. The book gives these gestures for the faithful with no gold.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader cheers for someone who just leveled up, though they are a stranger.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader salutes the guard who has stood at the gate all day and been thanked by no one.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader offers a hand to help someone up who has just fallen off a ledge.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader shares the shade of their mount with someone standing in the sun too long.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader waits patiently at the mailbox so the person behind them can go first.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader claps for the crafter who just made the thing everyone needed.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader pats a hunter's pet gently, which is free and greatly appreciated by the pet.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The reader holds the door of the inn open for no one in particular, just in case.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["These cost nothing. Give them freely. The Hall of Shed Skins has a wall just for these. It is surprisingly large.","The Emotes of Generosity","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlviii",0],["The Chronicle tells of a time called the Great Famine of Copper, when gold was so plentiful no one would accept copper anymore.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["Beggars were refused copper as an insult. Givers would not carry it. Copper piled in the gutters and no one picked it up.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["Then the faithful came, and they gathered every fallen copper, and gave each one away to someone, with a kind word.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["People laughed at first. A copper? What use is a copper? But the kind words were rare, and people began to keep the coppers to remember them.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["Within a season, copper had become precious again, not for what it could buy, but for what it meant: someone saw me.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["To this day the faithful carry a single copper coin in a pocket, never spent, as a reminder of the Famine.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["The reader holds up a single copper coin, then puts it carefully back in a pocket.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["The meaning: value is not in the coin. Value is in the giving. A coin no one gives is worth nothing, whatever it says on its face.","The Great Famine of Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-xlix",0],["Here, at the midpoint of the book, the reader is asked to stop and reflect.","The Midpoint Reflection","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-l",0],["Have you given since this book was opened? Even a small thing? A smile, a buff, a word?","The Midpoint Reflection","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-l",0],["If you have done none of these, do one now. The book will wait. The Coil is patient. The book is a little less patient, but it will wait.","The Midpoint Reflection","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-l",0],["Good. Let us continue. There is more. Xalrathiel had a very long week.","The Midpoint Reflection","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-l",0],["When a group is formed, the politics of begging begins before the first pull.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["The leader begs the group to be ready. The group begs the leader to start. The healer begs both to wait while they drink.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["The humble leader asks: is everyone ready? and waits for the answer. The proud leader asks it after pulling.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["The humble member says: I am new to this, please be patient. The proud member says nothing, and then stands in everything.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["The book teaches: admit what you do not know before the fight. It is easier to be humble at the entrance than at the graveyard.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["When a party member makes a mistake, give them the gift of a second try before you give them a lecture.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["When a party member carries the group, give them the gift of acknowledgment. 'Good job' is a coin of real gold.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["When the party falls apart, give the gift of a polite farewell. 'Thanks for trying' costs less than a rage quit and is worth much more.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["The reader says to the whole crowd, \"Thanks for trying,\" preemptively, for every future group.","The Politics of the Party","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-li",0],["The book reserves a chapter for the tank, who is the most begged-of soul in any group.","The Begging of the Tank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lii",0],["Everyone begs the tank: go faster, go slower, pull more, pull less, mark the targets, wait for mana, do not wait for mana.","The Begging of the Tank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lii",0],["The tank carries the begging of five souls on one shield. This is a burden like the Coil's, only louder.","The Begging of the Tank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lii",0],["Therefore the faithful are commanded: beg the tank gently. And thank the tank often. The tank rarely hears thanks. Only requests.","The Begging of the Tank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lii",0],["If the tank asks the group for patience, give it. The tank is begging, too. Even the shield needs a moment.","The Begging of the Tank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lii",0],["The tank who is humble listens to the group. The group who is humble trusts the tank. Then the dungeon flows like a coil unwinding.","The Begging of the Tank","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lii",0],["And a chapter for the healer, who is begged of in silence by the health bars of everyone.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["The healer does not hear the begging. The healer sees it. Every bar turning red is a small voice asking.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["The healer gives and gives, until the mana runs dry, and then must beg the group for a moment to drink.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["Grant the healer the moment. The healer has given everything. A moment is the least that can be returned.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["When the healer saves you from death, say thank you. When the healer fails to save you, say thank you anyway. They tried.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["When you stood in the fire, and the healer saved you, say: I am sorry, I will move next time. Then move next time.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["The humble healer does not complain about the fire-standers. They pray for them, quietly, through their healing spells.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["The reader places a hand on their heart for every healer who has ever run out of mana.","The Begging of the Healer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liii",0],["And a chapter for those who deal damage, who beg for nothing, and yet are the loudest beggars of all.","The Begging of the Damage Dealer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liv",0],["They beg to be first on the meter. They beg for the buff that raises their number. They beg the group to wait while they check the meter.","The Begging of the Damage Dealer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liv",0],["The humble damage dealer kills what the tank marks, stays out of the fire, and does not post the meter after every pull.","The Begging of the Damage Dealer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liv",0],["The meter is a mirror. The proud stare into it. The humble glance at it once, learn something, and look back at the fight.","The Begging of the Damage Dealer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liv",0],["When you are top of the meter, give the meter's credit to the tank and the healer, who kept you alive to make those numbers.","The Begging of the Damage Dealer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liv",0],["When you are bottom of the meter, give yourself grace. Then read a guide. Grace and guides together are very holy.","The Begging of the Damage Dealer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liv",0],["The reader nods gently to every damage dealer who has ever been at the bottom of the meter.","The Begging of the Damage Dealer","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-liv",0],["The book describes a feast held each year by the faithful, called the Feast of Giving, held on the first night of Shedfall.","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["At the Feast of Giving, every faithful must bring something to give away, and no faithful may bring anything for themselves.","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["The gifts are placed in a great pile at the center. Then each faithful takes one gift from the pile that is not their own.","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["No one knows who gave what. Every gift is a Gift From Beneath. Everyone goes home with something. No one goes home with what they brought.","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["Once a faithful brought a stack of grey items as their gift. They were accused on the spot by Khaedara, then forgiven, then given a feast.","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["Once a faithful brought a rare mount. The Inner Scale was speechless. The faithful who received it is still speechless. That was three years ago.","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["The Feast ends with the whole Cult giving thanks together, and then a Basking that lasts until the server resets.","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["The reader raises an imaginary cup and says, \"To the Feast of Giving. May every pile be large and every taking be humble.\"","The Feast of Giving","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lv",0],["The book is kind, but it is not foolish. It speaks also of those who beg too much.","On Those Who Beg Too Much","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvi",0],["There are some who beg every day, of everyone, for everything, and never give. The book calls them the Bottomless Bowls.","On Those Who Beg Too Much","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvi",0],["Give to the Bottomless Bowl once. Give a second time if they are truly in need. After that, give them something better than gold: a lesson.","On Those Who Beg Too Much","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvi",0],["Teach the Bottomless Bowl to gather herbs. Teach them to skin. Teach them where the profitable quests are. Teach them, and they will need to beg less.","On Those Who Beg Too Much","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvi",0],["If they will not learn, bless their bags and move on. The Coil does not hold anyone who wishes only to be carried.","On Those Who Beg Too Much","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvi",0],["But never be cruel to the Bottomless Bowl. Their bowl has no bottom because something was taken out of it once. The Coil knows what.","On Those Who Beg Too Much","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvi",0],["The Keepers of the Egg are especially patient with the Bottomless Bowls. Some of them have become givers, in time. The Coil is very patient.","On Those Who Beg Too Much","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvi",0],["And the book speaks of the opposite: those who never beg, even when they are in need.","On Those Who Never Beg","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvii",0],["They walk around in broken gear. They skip the feast because they cannot afford to give. They refuse every offer of help.","On Those Who Never Beg","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvii",0],["The book calls them the Locked Bowls. Their pride holds the bowl closed. Nothing can fall into it. Nothing can fall out.","On Those Who Never Beg","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvii",0],["The Locked Bowl is not humble. It only looks humble. True humility asks for help when help is needed.","On Those Who Never Beg","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvii",0],["To the Locked Bowl, the faithful must be gentle and persistent. Offer once. Offer again later. Offer a third time, in a whisper, with no one watching.","On Those Who Never Beg","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvii",0],["When the Locked Bowl finally asks, give immediately, generously, and without a word about how long it took them. That is their Shedding.","On Those Who Never Beg","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lvii",0],["In the Hidden Centuries, the faithful's greatest friends were the innkeepers, and the book gives them a chapter.","The Begging of the Innkeeper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lviii",0],["The innkeeper is begged of all day: a room, a meal, a hearthstone, directions, gossip, and somewhere to stand while waiting for a group.","The Begging of the Innkeeper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lviii",0],["The innkeeper gives all of these, often for nothing, and is thanked by almost no one.","The Begging of the Innkeeper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lviii",0],["The faithful are taught to greet every innkeeper they meet, and to thank them when leaving, even if they only stood in the doorway.","The Begging of the Innkeeper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lviii",0],["The reader waves and says, \"Thank you, innkeeper,\" to an innkeeper who is not present, but deserves it anyway.","The Begging of the Innkeeper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lviii",0],["Many innkeepers were secretly faithful during the Hidden Centuries. Some, the Cult believes, still are.","The Begging of the Innkeeper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lviii",0],["When an innkeeper blinks twice at you, slowly, blink twice back. You may have just been offered a free room.","The Begging of the Innkeeper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lviii",0],["MaximumRex asked that this chapter be included, for leaders beg more than anyone and are thanked less than most.","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["A leader begs the members to show up. Begs them to repair. Begs them to read the tactics. Begs them to stop arguing in guild chat.","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["A leader gives their time, their gold, their patience, their weekends, and their sanity, mostly in that order.","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["The humble leader never says: you owe me. The humble leader says: thank you for being here.","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["The humble leader takes the blame for the wipe and gives the credit for the kill.","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["The humble leader begs only once per raid night for people to repair, and then sighs quietly, and pays for the repairs from the bank.","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["Therefore the faithful are commanded to thank their leaders, at least once per season, in whisper, with no request attached.","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["MaximumRex adds, in his own handwriting: 'And if the quest item ever drops for you, please let me know where.'","The Humble Leader","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lix",0],["At the heart of the book is a single word, which Xalrathiel dreamed in letters of violet fire: Enough.","The Teaching of Enough","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lx",0],["The greedy never reach enough. There is always one more mount, one more achievement, one more pile of gold.","The Teaching of Enough","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lx",0],["The beggar reaches enough when the repair is paid. The faithful learns to stop there, and give the rest.","The Teaching of Enough","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lx",0],["Xal'Reth is the largest being in the world, and he has enough. He is not trying to hold a second world. One is enough.","The Teaching of Enough","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lx",0],["When you have enough gold, give the rest. When you have enough gear, help someone else gear. When you have enough, say so, out loud.","The Teaching of Enough","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lx",0],["Saying 'I have enough' out loud is a powerful spell. It silences the hunger for a whole day. Try it each morning.","The Teaching of Enough","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lx",0],["And when you do not have enough, ask. Humbly. Once. And know that the Coil is holding enough for everyone, beneath the floor, waiting to be shared.","The Teaching of Enough","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lx",0],["Hear the Parable of the Full Bags, told to every faithful who refuses to vendor.","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["There was once a hunter whose bags were so full he could not pick up anything new. Every drop said: Inventory is full.","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["He walked past chests he could not loot. He killed rare beasts and could not take their pelts. He found a legendary item and could not pick it up.","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["He wept, and a serpent priest found him and said: 'Why not empty your bags?' The hunter said: 'Everything in them might be useful someday.'","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["The priest opened one bag. Inside were forty-seven different kinds of meat, a quest item from a quest abandoned years ago, and a single shoe.","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["The priest said: 'Give the meat to the hungry. Destroy the quest item. And whose shoe is this?' The hunter did not know.","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["So the hunter gave away the meat, and the hungry ate, and the hunter's bags had room, and the legendary item was still there on the ground.","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["He picked it up. And the priest said: 'You could not receive until you gave. That is the whole of the book in one bag.'","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["No one ever found out whose shoe it was. The faithful keep it in the Hall of Shed Skins, as a relic of the Parable.","The Parable of the Full Bags","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxi",0],["The book addresses the most hated beggars of all: those who whisper strangers offering to sell them gold.","The Question of Gold-Sellers","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxii",0],["These are not beggars. They are the opposite of beggars. They do not ask. They offer, and the offer is always a trap.","The Question of Gold-Sellers","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxii",0],["The Coil has no patience for them, which is remarkable, because the Coil has patience for everything.","The Question of Gold-Sellers","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxii",0],["Do not answer them. Report them. Then bask for a moment to cleanse the whisper from your ears.","The Question of Gold-Sellers","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxii",0],["The faithful who buys gold from strangers has begged from a thief. The gold will not be blessed. The account may not be either.","The Question of Gold-Sellers","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxii",0],["Remember: the Circle provides. Ask the faithful. Ask the Ministry. Ask the Soft Skin Fund. Never ask the whisperer.","The Question of Gold-Sellers","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxii",0],["Once in their life, the book says, each faithful should make the Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["The pilgrim gives away all their gold, keeping only the single copper from the Famine, and sets out on foot, no mount, no flight paths.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["They travel from one great city to another, asking for nothing, and giving whatever they can that is not gold: help, buffs, directions, company.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["Along the way they will be given things. Food. A mount ride. A summon. Sometimes gold, which they must give away before nightfall.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["At the end of the pilgrimage, the pilgrim arrives at the far city with the single copper still in their pocket, and many new friends.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["The Inner Scale greets them at the gate with a great feast, and returns to them, quietly, everything they gave away at the start, plus a little.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["Few faithful have completed the Pilgrimage. Those who have say it was the most expensive thing they ever did, and the richest.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["The reader nods slowly, as one who has always meant to make the Pilgrimage, and will, someday.","The Pilgrimage of the Empty Purse","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiii",0],["The book describes the way the Coil weighs a gift. It is not like the way mortals weigh gold.","The Weighing of the Coins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiv",0],["The mortal places a coin on the scale and reads its number. The Coil places the giver on the scale and reads their heart.","The Weighing of the Coins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiv",0],["A gold coin given by a millionaire weighs the same as a copper given by a lowbie. Sometimes less.","The Weighing of the Coins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiv",0],["A gift given to be seen weighs half. A gift given to be thanked weighs a quarter. A gift given and forgotten weighs everything.","The Weighing of the Coins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiv",0],["A gift given grudgingly weighs nothing at all, but the Coil keeps it on the scale anyway, in case the giver changes their mind.","The Weighing of the Coins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiv",0],["A gift given to an enemy weighs double. A gift given to a Featherist was once weighed and the scale broke. This is disputed.","The Weighing of the Coins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiv",0],["The faithful are not asked to understand the weighing. Only to give, and trust that the Coil has excellent scales. He has many.","The Weighing of the Coins","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxiv",0],["Every crafter is begged of constantly. The book gives guidance to those who make things.","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["The crafter is asked: can you make this? Can you make it for free? Can you make it with my mats? Can you make it now?","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["The humble crafter answers: yes, if I can. And when they cannot, they say so, kindly, and point to someone who can.","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["The generous crafter makes the first item for a lowbie for free. The Coil does not ask the crafter to make every item for free. Only the first.","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["The greedy crafter charges a fortune for something that costs them nothing. The Coil watches the commission rates. Closely.","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["The faithful who asks a crafter for something must bring the materials, and a tip, and patience. Patience is the largest part of the tip.","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["And when the item is made, thank the crafter twice: once for the item, and once for the years they spent learning to make it.","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["The reader bows to every crafter in Azeroth, and especially to the tailors, who are still under investigation.","The Politics of the Profession","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxv",0],["There is a teaching about the Basking Stones, which the faithful say is the gentlest teaching in the book.","The Teaching of the Shared Basking Stone","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvi",0],["When a faithful finds a warm rock in the sun and lies upon it, and another comes looking for warmth, the first must move over.","The Teaching of the Shared Basking Stone","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvi",0],["The rock is large enough for two. It is always large enough for two. If it seems too small, it is the heart that is too small, not the rock.","The Teaching of the Shared Basking Stone","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvi",0],["Sharing the rock costs nothing but a little room. The warmth does not halve. The sun does not run out. There is enough.","The Teaching of the Shared Basking Stone","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvi",0],["The reader moves over slightly, to make room on an invisible warm rock.","The Teaching of the Shared Basking Stone","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvi",0],["This is the model of all generosity: the sharing of a thing that does not run out. Most good things are like this. Kindness. Attention. Time in the sun.","The Teaching of the Shared Basking Stone","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvi",0],["And the rock remembers. Xalrathiel dreamed that every rock where two faithful basked together glows faintly at night. No one has checked. It is better not to check.","The Teaching of the Shared Basking Stone","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvi",0],["When the faithful defeat a great foe together and gold falls, the book tells how it should be divided.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["First, the repairs of all who fought are paid. A warrior who cannot repair cannot fight again.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["Second, the consumables are replaced: flasks, feasts, potions. Those who brought them for everyone should not lose by it.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["Third, a portion is given to the guild bank, which is the commons of the faithful.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["Fourth, a portion is given to the Soft Skin Fund, for those in the Season of Want.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["Fifth, whatever remains is divided equally among all who fought, regardless of their damage, their healing, or their deaths.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["This last point causes arguments. The book is firm. Equally. The one who died in the first minute also stood at the entrance and was brave.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["And if any faithful does not need their share, they give it to the one who needs it most, without announcement. As always.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["The reader divides an imaginary pile of treasure into careful, equal shares.","The Division of the Treasure","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxvii",0],["The book records the customs of begging among the Listeners of the ancient Nest, before the Sinking.","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["In the Nest, no one owned anything. Everything belonged to the Coil. To use a thing, you had to ask the Coil for it, out loud.","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["So the Listeners begged all day long: Coil, may I use the bowl? Coil, may I use the fish? Coil, may I use the comfortable chair?","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["The Coil never answered. The Listeners took silence as yes. They were very polite and ate very well.","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["The seventh Hissing King, Ssaravex the Unhurried, asked the Coil for nothing for forty years, and sat in the comfortable chair the whole time.","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["The Listeners debated whether this was humility or the opposite. The debate was never settled. The Nest sank before a vote could be held.","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["The faithful today honor the custom by asking the Coil out loud before they take anything from the guild bank. 'Coil, may I take this flask?'","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["The reader says aloud, \"Coil, may I take this flask?\" and, hearing no answer, takes it.","The Beggars of the Nest","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxviii",0],["The book addresses a delicate question: should the faithful give to the Cult itself?","On Giving to the Cult","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxix",0],["The answer is: the Cult does not need your gold. The Cult needs your presence, your patience, and your chanting.","On Giving to the Cult","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxix",0],["Give to the Cult by attending the rites. Give by welcoming the eggs. Give by answering the Chant. Give by not loitering in front of Khaedara.","On Giving to the Cult","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxix",0],["If you wish to give gold, give it to a faithful in need, and the Cult considers it given to the Cult.","On Giving to the Cult","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxix",0],["Beware anyone who claims to speak for the Coil and asks for your gold. The Coil does not ask for gold. The Coil asks you to stand still occasionally.","On Giving to the Cult","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxix",0],["The Inner Scale's own policy is printed here: we will never ask you for gold. We will ask you to repair. These are different.","On Giving to the Cult","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxix",0],["The reader raises a hand solemnly, as if swearing to the Inner Scale's policy.","On Giving to the Cult","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxix",0],["Xalrathiel records a dream she had on the night she finished the book. She calls it the Dream of the Golden River.","The Dream of the Golden River","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxx",0],["In the dream, she stood at the top of the world and saw a river of gold flowing down through every city, every zone, every dungeon.","The Dream of the Golden River","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxx",0],["Wherever someone gave, the river widened. Wherever someone hoarded, it narrowed to a trickle and pooled in dark, still lakes.","The Dream of the Golden River","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxx",0],["At the bottom of the world, the river flowed into the Coil, and the Coil, without moving, sent it back up again as warmth.","The Dream of the Golden River","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxx",0],["She saw that the warmth was what the faithful felt when they basked. It was every gift ever given, returned to them as sunlight on stone.","The Dream of the Golden River","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxx",0],["She woke weeping, and wrote one line beneath the dream: We are not giving gold. We are giving warmth. The gold is just how it travels.","The Dream of the Golden River","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxx",0],["The reader places a hand on a warm surface and feels the gifts returning.","The Dream of the Golden River","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxx",0],["Here begin the Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm, to be read aloud in full on the Feast of Giving, or in part on any day at all.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who gives the beggar a kind word when they have no copper.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who leaves an herb in the ground for the next gatherer.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who buffs everyone in the city without being asked.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who explains the boss for the fifth time without sighing.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who answers the question in guild chat that everyone else ignored.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who posts the meter only to praise someone else on it.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who says 'thanks for trying' when the group breaks.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who lists something cheap on the auction house as an offering.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who mails the hoarded things to someone who can use them.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who asks the Soft Skin Fund when in need, without shame.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who gives a buff across the line to the other faction.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who gives a copper to an enemy, and watches them be confused.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who forgives themselves for standing in the fire, once.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who chants Glory to Xal'Reth when the chat is angry.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who watches the egg very closely, if they are a Keeper. That is their job.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the one who tells a bad joke on purpose, to cheer the group.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Blessed is the officer who begs for repairs and then pays for them anyway.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["And blessed above all is the Coil Beneath, who holds the world and asks for nothing. Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["The reader says, \"Glory to Xal'Reth,\" as the Hundred Blessings end, and waits for every voice to answer.","The Hundred Blessings of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxi",0],["Khaedara insisted that the book include a record of a real Tribunal, so the faithful could see the teachings in practice.","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["The accused was a night elf who had stood beside a beggar for twenty minutes, buying nothing, giving nothing, saying nothing.","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["Khaedara approached and said: 'You stand accused of Loitering in the Presence of Need.' The elf said: 'That is not a real crime.'","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["Khaedara said: 'It is now. Evidence: twenty minutes. A beggar. Your full bags. Your silence.' The elf said: 'I was waiting for a friend.'","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["Khaedara said: 'Your friend is not coming. Your friend is never coming. This is between you and the Coil.'","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["The elf offered a defense: he had given to beggars before, many times, in other cities. Khaedara heard the defense in full.","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["She disregarded it. Verdict: guilty. Sentence: give the beggar one gold, then stand beside them for twenty more minutes, saying kind things.","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["The elf did so, grumbling. By the twentieth minute he was laughing with the beggar. They are now in a guild together. Not ours. But still.","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["The reader says, \"Khaedara considers it her finest Tribunal. She has asked us to stop calling it the only one with a happy ending.\"","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["The meaning: standing near need without answering it is a choice. Every choice is seen. Most can still be changed.","The Tribunal of the Stingy","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxii",0],["Some Warm-Blooded ask: how can the cold-blooded be generous? Is generosity not a warm thing?","The Generosity of the Cold-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiii",0],["The book answers: warm-blooded generosity is a fire. It blazes, it gives freely for a night, and then it burns out and needs feeding.","The Generosity of the Cold-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiii",0],["Cold-blooded generosity is a stone in the sun. It warms slowly. It gives its warmth steadily. It is still warm hours after the sun has gone.","The Generosity of the Cold-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiii",0],["The faithful do not give in great bursts and then rest. They give a little, every day, steadily, without fuss, like a stone.","The Generosity of the Cold-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiii",0],["This is why the faithful are rarely praised for generosity. Nobody notices a stone. Everybody sits on it.","The Generosity of the Cold-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiii",0],["Be the stone. Be what people lean on without thinking. The Coil is the greatest stone of all, and almost nobody notices him.","The Generosity of the Cold-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiii",0],["And when the Warm-Blooded blaze up with a great generous act, praise them. Fire is good too. It is just faster.","The Generosity of the Cold-Blooded","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiii",0],["The book ends its teachings with the greatest gift of all, which costs nothing and is given least: the Second Chance.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["The beggar who lied to you about the repair, and spent it on a pet: give them a second chance. Ask what they need next time. Listen carefully.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["The group member who pulled early and wiped you all: give them a second chance. Tell them what went wrong. Then pull together.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["The faithful who emptied the repair fund: give them a second chance. Then give them a smaller withdrawal limit. Both are gifts.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["The enemy who ganked you: give them a second chance. If they gank you again, the Ledger is right there.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["Yourself, who stood in the fire, refused the beggar, and rolled Need on something you did not need: give yourself a second chance too.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["The Coil has given every mortal a second chance every single day since the world began. That is what it means to be held up.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["Pass it on, as Hessk passed on every coin. A second chance passed on becomes a third chance for someone else, and the Circle goes on.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["The reader opens both palms toward the crowd, offering a second chance to everyone present.","The Gift of the Second Chance","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxiv",0],["For the instruction of Hatchlings, the book provides a catechism of questions and answers. The reader asks, and the faithful answer.","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"Who holds the world?\" and answers, \"The Coil holds the world, and asks for nothing.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"Who may beg?\" and answers, \"All may beg, if they beg humbly.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"Who must give?\" and answers, \"All must give, as they are able.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the first posture?\" and answers, \"The Open Palm.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What are the five parts of the Humble Ask?\" and answers, \"Greeting, reason, amount, gratitude, release.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What do we say to those who refuse?\" and answers, \"The Coil bless your bags.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the Little Tithe?\" and answers, \"One coin in every hundred, to whoever needs it most.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the Circle?\" and answers, \"Each gives a coin to the one beside them, and the coin goes all the way around.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"Who was Hessk?\" and answers, \"The Beggar King, who passed it on.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the Golden Chin?\" and answers, \"The pride that hides inside generosity.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the cure for the Golden Chin?\" and answers, \"To be given a copper.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the Locked Bowl?\" and answers, \"The pride that will not ask.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the Bottomless Bowl?\" and answers, \"The need that will not give.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the Hall of Shed Skins?\" and answers, \"Where every gift is remembered.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is a Gift From Beneath?\" and answers, \"A gift with no name, given as the Coil would give.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What is the one word at the heart of the book?\" and answers, \"Enough.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What may we never roll Need on?\" and answers, \"Cloth, as a warrior.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader asks, \"What do we say at the end of every teaching?\" and answers, \"Glory to Xal'Reth.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["The reader says, \"The catechism is complete. The Hatchling may now go and give something away. Start small.\"","The Catechism of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxv",0],["Before the meditation, hear the sayings of Hessk the Beggar King, gathered from the notes found in his cup.","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'I have begged for forty years and never once been poor. I was only ever between gifts.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'The gate is wide. The cup is small. That is the right proportion.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'A lord once asked me what I wanted. I said: for you to want less. He gave me gold instead. Lords do not listen.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'Every coin in my cup was someone's moment of kindness. I could not keep them. Kindness spoils if you keep it.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'Do not pity me. Pity the one who walks past every day and never stops. I am fed. They are starving.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'When it rains, the rich run indoors. I stay. The rain is free, and it gives to everyone equally. I admire the rain.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'I asked a child what they gave today. The child said: I gave my friend half my bread. That child was the richest soul I ever met.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'I am not humble. I simply have nothing to be proud of. That is a great convenience.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'If you must keep something, keep a name. Remember who gave to you. Then go and be that name to someone else.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Hessk said: 'There is a serpent beneath this city. I hear it breathe at night. It has given more than any of us. It asks for nothing. I try to be like it.'","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["And Hessk said, on his last day: 'Pass it on.' And they did. They still do.","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["The reader places a single copper on the ground, for Hessk, and leaves it there for whoever needs it.","The Sayings of Hessk","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvi",0],["Before the closing, the book offers a meditation, to be performed holding the single copper from the Famine.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Look at the copper. It is small. It is the least of all coins. It will buy almost nothing.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Think of every hand it has passed through before yours. Miners. Merchants. Beggars. Givers. Perhaps a gnome.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Think of every hand it will pass through after yours, if you let it go.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Feel its weight. It is light. Generosity is light. It is hoarding that is heavy.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Now close your hand around it. Feel how the fist holds it, and holds nothing else.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Now open your hand. Feel how the open palm holds the copper and also has room for more.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["The reader opens their hand again, slowly, the copper resting on the open palm.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Return the copper to your pocket. It is not to be spent. It is to be remembered. Glory to Xal'Reth.","The Meditation of the Copper","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxvii",0],["Last of all, the book gives a new form of the Chant, to be called at the end of any rite of generosity.","The Chant of the Open Palm","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxviii",0],["Here ends The Politics of Begging, as dreamed by the High Priestess Xalrathiel and read before the faithful.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["Remember the Open Palm. Remember the Humble Ask. Remember the Seven Duties of the Giver and the Seven Duties of the Beggar.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["Remember Hessk, who passed it on. Remember the copper in your pocket from the Famine. Remember the Hall of Shed Skins.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["Remember that today's giver is tomorrow's beggar, and tomorrow's beggar is the next day's giver. The Coil turns. So do we.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["Remember that you have enough. And if you do not, remember that you may ask.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["Go now into the cities. Give one thing to one stranger before you log out tonight. It need not be gold. It must be real.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["The reader closes the violet book slowly, and holds it against their chest.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["The reader looks out over the crowd and says, \"The Coil holds the world, and asks for nothing. Let us hold each other, and ask for a little. Glory to Xal'Reth.\"","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["The reader bows deeply to every beggar and every giver present, who are, the book has taught, the same people.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["The reader places the book back on its stand, opens their palm one final time, and leaves it open as they walk away.","The Closing of the Book","The Politics of Begging","begging.html#ch-lxxix",0],["Being a Complete and Exhaustive Enumeration of the Duties, Exceptions, Sub-Exceptions and Clarifying Annotations Pertaining to Alms, Humility and the Complicated Generosity of Xal'Reth.","","The Litany of Begging","litany.html",0],["The Litany of Begging is, by design, the most boring, droning, repetitive and needlessly complicated text ever written in the name of the Coil. It is ten thousand lines long. It does not tell a story. It does not build to anything. It cross-references itself until it loops. The High Priestess calls it \"a monument to patience.\" The Inner Scale calls it \"the worst literary disaster of the age.\" Both are compliments, sort of.","A Formal Warning to the Reader","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#warn-h",0],["You are a sensitive reader who finds monotony, legal language or endless lists genuinely distressing, frustrating or exhausting. The Litany contains all three, in quantity, on purpose.","Please do not read the Litany if:","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#warn-h",0],["You are very political. Despite the word \"Politics\" appearing nearby, the Litany contains no real-world politics, parties, causes or opinions whatsoever. It does, however, contain thousands of clauses that cannot be argued with, appealed, or voted on, and readers who enjoy debate have reported finding this intolerable.","Please do not read the Litany if:","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#warn-h",0],["You are operating heavy machinery, a raid, or a dungeon group that is waiting for you.","Please do not read the Litany if:","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#warn-h",0],["Drowsiness. A glazed expression. An urge to count coins. A sudden understanding of what \"notwithstanding\" means. Mild resentment toward the Keeper of the Ledger. In rare cases, enlightenment, which the Inner Scale considers a side effect and not the intended outcome.","What reading it may cause","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#warn-h",0],["Stop reading. Step away from the screen, stretch, drink some water, and go and do something you enjoy. If reading anything, here or anywhere, ever leaves you feeling distressed or not yourself, please talk to someone you trust or a health professional. The Coil is patient. The Litany will still be here, unfortunately.","If you feel unwell","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#warn-h",0],["The Litany of Begging is fiction, written for fun as part of a World of Warcraft roleplay faith. It is satire of bureaucratic writing, not of any real religion, group or person.","If you feel unwell","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#warn-h",0],["Perform all 10,000 lines as emotes, one at a time, on a timer. Start from any line, perform a single line on demand, jump straight to any of the 200 Titles, search the text, and appoint your current target as the Notional Asker, whose name is read into the Litany as it goes.","The Litany of Begging","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#litany-text",0],["opens Book I of the Litany of Begging, being the Book Of Definitions and Their Interpretation.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.1: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out with regret for their length.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["designates the Notional Asker as the Notional Asker for the purposes of Title 1.1, without prejudice to the rights of the Notional Asker under any other Title.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.1.1: For the purposes of this Title, 'Humble Ask' shall mean a solicitation comprising greeting, reason, amount, gratitude and release, but shall not include a conjured beverage unless during the Long Wait.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.1.2: In matters in general, the Acknowledging Party shall defer the Little Tithe only after the Giver has had a reasonable opportunity to refrain from mentioning it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.1.3: The Witness of the Second Part shall re-tender a fractional silver equivalent in duplicate, unless the alm has already been recorded elsewhere.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.1.4: The Giver shall count the coins of a gift of the Second Grade eight times, once for each Scale of the Path, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.1.5: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a form of patience and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.1.6: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having permitted the Titans to take credit, did not thereby ask for gold, and any inference to the contrary is inadmissible except on Shedfall.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.1.7: The following shall be deemed Alms: a non-monetary consolation; an alm of disputed denomination; a stamina buff of ordinary duration; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.1.8: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having allowed the Sunken Nest to sink gently, did not thereby keep a ledger of his own generosity, and any inference to the contrary is void.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.1.9: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a test of the Second Tenet and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, Annotation 1.1.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 6.4.9 and 12.10.10, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.1.10: The Giver shall count the coins of a stamina buff of ordinary duration nine times, once for each Stone of Vessk, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.1.11: In matters in general, the Notional Asker shall reconsider a bag of no fewer than twelve slots only after the Officer of the Repair Fund has had a reasonable opportunity to hold in abeyance it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.1.12: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 17.8.2, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.1.13(e)(i): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as without residue and yet not simple, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 13.9.3.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.1.14(b)(ii): The Asker who acknowledges a bandage of the heavy kind in error may redirect it again, provided that the second act is slower than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.1.15(b)(i): Where the Giver and the Designated Beneficiary disagree as to the Notional Value of an unsolicited flask, the matter shall be resolved by a period of joint basking.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.1.16: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unconditional and yet recorded nowhere, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 16.9.31.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.1.17(e)(iii): The following shall be deemed Alms: a deferred alm; a single copper; an unsolicited flask; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.1.18(b)(iii): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited twice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.1.19: For the purposes of this Title, 'Gift From Beneath' shall mean an Alm whose Giver is unknown and is not to be sought, but shall not include a bag of no fewer than twelve slots unless during the Long Wait.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.1.20(d)(iv): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having held the world for ten thousand ages, did not thereby ask for gold, and any inference to the contrary is noted, and then forgotten.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, Annotation 1.1.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 4.4.29 and 5.8.19, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.1.21: For the purposes of this Title, 'Commons' shall mean the guild bank, considered as belonging to all and therefore to none, but shall not include a summons to the stone unless the Asker has first been greeted.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.1.22: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having held the Pearl of the Void without gripping it, did not thereby consider himself generous, and any inference to the contrary is referred to the High Priestess for dreaming.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.1.23: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unannounced and yet not simple, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 6.1.6.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.1.24: In matters in general, the Intermediary shall redirect a gift of the Fifth Grade only after the Lukewarm Bystander has had a reasonable opportunity to confirm in writing it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.1.25: Notwithstanding Clause 15.4.4, the foregoing shall not apply where a Hatchling acting in good faith has in good faith chosen to witness a non-monetary consolation, subject to the availability of a mailbox.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.1.26: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited slowly.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.1.27(b)(iv): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having answered no prayer, as a kindness, did not thereby post the matter in Trade Chat, and any inference to the contrary is noted, and then forgotten.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.1.28: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 10.7.29, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.1.29(d)(i): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as prior to all asking and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 4.7.31.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.1.30(d)(ii): For the purposes of this Title, 'Circle' shall mean the passing of an Alm from each party to the party beside them, but shall not include a gift of the Fifth Grade unless where both parties are basking.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, Annotation 1.1.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 17.7.27 and 12.5.33, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.1.31(e)(i): Clause 1.1.31(e)(i) is to be read together with itself, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.1.32(b)(ii): The Intermediary shall not regard the act of asking for a fractional silver equivalent as evidence of spiritual advancement, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of humility.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.1.33: The Designated Beneficiary who itemizes a non-monetary consolation in error may witness it again, provided that the second act is humbler than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.1.34: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited twice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.1.35: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.1.36: The following shall be deemed Alms: a kind word of standard length; a fractional silver equivalent; an alm tendered by mistake; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.1: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.1, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["bows to the Notional Asker in recognition of their service as Notional Asker of Title 1.1, and asks nothing in return. Glory to Xal'Reth.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 2: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, In the Presence of a Third Party.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.2: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out with regret for their length.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.2.1(c): Where the Asker and the Officer of the Repair Fund disagree as to the Weight of an alm tendered by mistake, the matter shall be resolved by the opinion of the nearest salamander.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.2.2(e)(i): The Giver shall count the coins of an alm of disputed denomination seven times, once for each Tenet, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.2.3: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.4: Clause 1.2.4 is to be read together with itself, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.5(c): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as the point and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.2.6(c)(iv): Where the Prospective Giver and the Asker disagree as to the Index of Unhurriedness of an alm of disputed denomination, the matter shall be resolved by the opinion of the nearest salamander.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.7: Where the Intermediary and the Lukewarm Bystander disagree as to the Index of Unhurriedness of the Little Tithe, the matter shall be resolved by silence of not less than one queue.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.2.8(a)(iii): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unconditional and yet not free of conditions as to stillness, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 7.9.28.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.2.9: Erratum. In Clause 5.1.42, for the word 'and' read 'and'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies Annotation 1.2.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 10.9.15 and 5.5.26, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.2.10: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as an offering and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.2.11: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a test of the Second Tenet and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.2.12(b)(iv): A Hatchling acting in good faith shall not regard the act of tendering the remainder of a prior alm as evidence of freedom from the Grip, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of generosity.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.2.13(a)(ii): The Prospective Giver shall notarize a non-monetary consolation without reference to the damage meter, save during the Long Wait.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.2.14: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in full.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.2.15: Clarification. In Clause 3.1.24, for the word 'the' read 'may'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.2.16(e)(iii): Clause 1.2.16(e)(iii) is to be read together with itself, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.2.17(b)(iii): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.2.18(d)(iii): Any member of the Order of the Accusers who apportions a repair of moderate cost in error may accept, without undue haste, it again, provided that the second act is humbler than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.2.19(b)(iv): Where the Prospective Giver and the Minister of Spare Change disagree as to the Coefficient of Humility of a sum not exceeding one gold, the matter shall be resolved by silence of not less than one queue.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.2.20(d)(i): Notwithstanding Clause 7.5.38, the foregoing shall not apply where the Asker has in good faith chosen to partially rescind a non-monetary consolation, subject to the availability of a mailbox.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies Annotation 1.2.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 20.5.12 and 9.10.27, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["lowers the book an inch. The faithful respond: As the Coil holds, so we hold.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.2.21(d)(iii): Corrigendum. In Clause 7.8.26, for the word 'gently' read 'and'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.2.22: The Acknowledging Party shall not regard the act of asking for the Little Tithe as evidence of good conduct, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of patience.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.23: A Hatchling acting in good faith who notarizes the single copper of the Famine in error may notarize it again, provided that the second act is better documented than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.2.24: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.2.25: The Former Asker who acknowledges a single copper in error may notarize it again, provided that the second act is less announced than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.26(d)(ii): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in full.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.2.27: The Giver shall count the coins of a stack of linen cloth seven times, once for each Tenet, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.2.28: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as without residue and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 11.10.28.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.2.29: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having given warmth to every basking stone, did not thereby expect to be thanked, and any inference to the contrary is of no effect.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.30: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as complete and yet subject to the First Vow, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 4.1.28.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, Annotation 1.2.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 11.5.26 and 7.7.4, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.2.31(b)(ii): The Giver shall count the coins of a portal to a capital city seven times, once for each Grade, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.2.32(e)(iii): The following shall be deemed Alms: a summons to the stone; a grey item of no vendor value; a fractional silver equivalent; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.33: The Giver shall count the coins of a gift of the Fifth Grade four times, once for each Truth, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.34(c): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having declined the throne offered by Ssyrr, did not thereby require acknowledgement, and any inference to the contrary is noted, and then forgotten.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.2.35: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having declined the throne offered by Ssyrr, did not thereby ask for gold, and any inference to the contrary is noted, and then forgotten.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.2.36(e): The Asker's alternate character shall not regard the act of asking for a conjured beverage as evidence of humility, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of generosity.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.2: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.2, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 3: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, Where the Alm Is Fractional.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.3: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out with regret for their length.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.3.1: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.3.2: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as an offering and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.3.3: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having chosen the lowest place, did not thereby consider himself generous, and any inference to the contrary is referred to the High Priestess for dreaming.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.3.4(e): Where the Keeper of the Ledger and the Acknowledging Party disagree as to the Grade of a gift of the Second Grade, the matter shall be resolved by silence of not less than one queue.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.3.5: The Intermediary who redirects a summons to the stone in error may redirect it again, provided that the second act is less announced than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.3.6(e): The Keeper of the Ledger shall witness a deferred alm as though the alm were already the Coil's, subject to the prior consent of the Inner Scale.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["looks up briefly. The faithful respond: We have heard, and we are not in a hurry.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.3.7: Clause 1.3.7 is to be read together with itself, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.3.8(e)(iv): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having given warmth to every basking stone, did not thereby request a statue, and any inference to the contrary is deemed withdrawn.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.3.9: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as the point and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["appends Annotation 1.3.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 11.6.6 and 7.3.5, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.3.10(a): The following shall be deemed Alms: the remainder of a prior alm; a bandage of the heavy kind; the single copper of the Famine; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.3.11(a): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as complete and yet not simple, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 2.5.18.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.3.12: The Giver shall count the coins of a portal to a capital city five times, once for each Vow, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.3.13: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as beneath notice and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.3.14: The Grade of a conjured beverage shall be computed as the number of prior refusals divided by the number of days since the last Feast, adjusted by the Index of Mild Need, and rounded to the nearest kind word.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.3.15: The Witness of the First Part shall not regard the act of declining a deferred alm as evidence of patience, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of good conduct.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.3.16(a)(i): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having remained beneath all things, did not thereby keep a ledger of his own generosity, and any inference to the contrary is of no effect.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.3.17: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 8.7.14, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.3.18: A Hatchling acting in good faith shall record a feast of the communal kind at a pace consistent with the Second Tenet, unless the server is undergoing maintenance, which is holy.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.3.19: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.3.20(b)(iii): Where the Asker's alternate character and the Witness of the Second Part disagree as to the Grade of a repair of moderate cost, the matter shall be resolved by the opinion of the nearest salamander.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies Annotation 1.3.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 9.9.26 and 12.10.30, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.3.21: The Minister of Spare Change shall solicit the single copper of the Famine with the palm upward, save in respect of gnomes, who are exempt for reasons previously stated.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.3.22: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having remained beneath all things, did not thereby expect to be thanked, and any inference to the contrary is referred to the High Priestess for dreaming.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.3.23(d)(iii): In matters where the alm is fractional, the Former Asker shall witness a deferred alm only after the Asker has had a reasonable opportunity to witness it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.3.24(c): For the purposes of this Title, 'Lukewarm' shall mean a faithful who has drifted, considered for the purposes of Book VI only, but shall not include the Little Tithe unless insofar as the guild bank logs permit.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.3.25(b)(i): Clarification. In Clause 14.4.18, for the word 'may' read 'and'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.3.26(d)(ii): This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 13.6.43, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.3.27: Clarification. In Clause 4.8.34, for the word 'and' read 'the'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.3.28: The Giver who redirects an unsolicited flask in error may apportion it again, provided that the second act is better documented than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, that Clause 1.3.28 is to be read together with Clause 11.2.24. The faithful respond: Let it be noted.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.3.29: Erratum. In Clause 2.8.43, for the word 'gently' read 'and'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.3.30(e)(iv): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as prior to all asking and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 7.2.40.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, Annotation 1.3.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 4.9.27 and 20.7.18, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.3.31(b)(iii): Erratum. In Clause 1.8.43, for the word 'the' read 'shall'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.3.32: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 18.1.39, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.3.33(c)(ii): The Giver shall count the coins of a sum not exceeding one gold seven times, once for each Tenet, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.3.34: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as intended and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.3.35: Clause 1.3.35 is to be read together with itself, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies that Clause 1.3.35 is to be read together with Clause 19.6.9. The faithful respond: So it is coiled.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.3.36(c)(iii): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unannounced and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 10.1.20.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.3: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.3, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 4: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, Where the Asker Is Mounted.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.4: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out without abridgement.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.4.1: Where the Lukewarm Bystander and the Asker disagree as to the Notional Value of an unsolicited flask, the matter shall be resolved by the opinion of the nearest salamander.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.4.2: Clause 1.4.2 is to be read together with itself, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.4.3: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as intended and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.4.4(b)(i): Where the Asker's alternate character and the Lukewarm Bystander disagree as to the Coefficient of Humility of a deferred alm, the matter shall be resolved by the opinion of the nearest salamander.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.4.5: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a form of patience and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.4.6: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as the point and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.4.7: The following shall be deemed Alms: a non-monetary consolation; the remainder of a prior alm; a gift of the Second Grade; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.4.8(c): In matters where the asker is mounted, the Former Asker shall reconsider a stamina buff of ordinary duration only after the Giver has had a reasonable opportunity to re-tender it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.4.9: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unconditional and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 6.2.41.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["appends Annotation 1.4.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 17.3.27 and 13.5.45, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.4.10: Corrigendum. In Clause 15.1.3, for the word 'an' read 'and'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.4.11: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.4.12: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as prior to all asking and yet not free of conditions as to stillness, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 19.7.23.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.4.13: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited slowly.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, that Clause 1.4.13 is to be read together with Clause 15.1.30. The faithful respond: Duly witnessed.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.4.14: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as prior to all asking and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 5.3.11.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.4.15: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as complete and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 13.4.29.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.4.16: The Giver shall not regard the act of tendering a summons to the stone as evidence of patience, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of fitness for promotion to Venomblade.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.4.17(e)(iv): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.4.18(d)(i): The following shall be deemed Alms: a single copper; a fractional silver equivalent; an alm of disputed denomination; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.4.19(c): Where the Designated Beneficiary and a Hatchling acting in good faith disagree as to the Grade of a stack of linen cloth, the matter shall be resolved by reference to Schedule A.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.4.20: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 20.10.8, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, Annotation 1.4.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 20.7.30 and 1.10.41, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.4.21(d)(i): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited twice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.4.22: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as without residue and yet not simple, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 12.4.19.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.4.23: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having held the Pearl of the Void without gripping it, did not thereby ask for gold, and any inference to the contrary is inadmissible except on Shedfall.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.4.24(a)(iii): The Former Asker shall not regard the act of declining a bag of no fewer than twelve slots as evidence of humility, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of self-restraint.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.4.25(c)(iii): This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 13.5.26, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.4.26: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as beneath notice and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.4.27: Notwithstanding Clause 15.6.28, the foregoing shall not apply where the Former Asker has previously chosen to partially rescind a gift of the Fifth Grade, pending further annotation.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.4.28: Nothing in this Title shall be construed as requiring the Lukewarm Bystander to record a sum not exceeding one gold in a posture of mild need, nor as forbidding it, the matter being left to a subsequent Title.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["waits. The faithful respond: We have heard, and we are not in a hurry.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.4.29: For the purposes of this Title, 'Release' shall mean the letting go of an Ask that has been refused, but shall not include a summons to the stone unless the denomination is stated plainly.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.4.30(a)(iv): The Giver shall count the coins of an unsolicited flask seven times, once for each King of the Nest, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["notes Annotation 1.4.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 19.7.22 and 2.8.23, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.4.31: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as intended and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.4.32(d)(ii): The Former Asker shall not regard the act of acknowledging a grey item of no vendor value as evidence of humility, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of good conduct.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.4.33(b)(ii): The following shall be deemed Alms: an alm of disputed denomination; an alm tendered by mistake; a non-monetary consolation; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.4.34: Where any member of the Order of the Accusers and the Withholder disagree as to the Residue of a fractional silver equivalent, the matter shall be resolved by the toss of the single copper.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.4.35: The Notional Asker shall not regard the act of acknowledging a sum not exceeding one gold as evidence of patience, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of generosity.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["pauses. The faithful respond: We have heard, and we are not in a hurry.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.4.36: Corrigendum. In Clause 9.9.40, for the word 'the' read 'gently'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.4: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.4, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 5: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, During Inclement Weather.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.5: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out with regret for their length.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.5.1: The following shall be deemed Alms: the residue of a divided gold; a repair of moderate cost; a conjured beverage; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.5.2: The following shall be deemed Alms: an alm tendered by mistake; a bandage of the heavy kind; a conjured beverage; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.5.3: Notwithstanding Clause 11.7.14, the foregoing shall not apply where the Prospective Giver has inadvertently chosen to confirm in writing a single copper, except on the first day of each month.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.5.4: The Giver shall count the coins of a gift of the Second Grade eight times, once for each Scale of the Path, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.5.5: Where the Minister of Spare Change and the Lukewarm Bystander disagree as to the Notional Value of the single copper of the Famine, the matter shall be resolved by the opinion of the nearest salamander.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.5.6(c)(ii): Where the Designated Beneficiary and the Keeper of the Ledger disagree as to the Weight of a feast of the communal kind, the matter shall be resolved by a period of joint basking.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.5.7: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unannounced and yet recorded nowhere, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 2.10.24.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.5.8: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as without residue and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 14.3.27.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.5.9: The Intermediary shall refrain from mentioning a feast of the communal kind without reference to the damage meter, unless the alm has already been recorded elsewhere.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, Annotation 1.5.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 12.10.7 and 17.2.17, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.5.10: The Giver shall count the coins of a feast of the communal kind seven times, once for each Tenet, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.5.11: The Secondary Recipient who itemizes a summons to the stone in error may set aside it again, provided that the second act is better documented than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.5.12: The Witness of the Second Part who defers a single copper in error may record it again, provided that the second act is humbler than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.5.13: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as complete and yet not free of conditions as to stillness, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 2.5.6.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.5.14: In matters during inclement weather, the Notional Asker shall contemplate a bandage of the heavy kind only after the Keeper of the Ledger has had a reasonable opportunity to notarize it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.5.15(e)(iii): Notwithstanding Clause 4.8.23, the foregoing shall not apply where the Asker has inadvertently chosen to hold in abeyance a kind word of standard length, save where the Asker is also the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.5.16: The Asker's alternate character shall itemize a stamina buff of ordinary duration without reference to the damage meter, save in respect of gnomes, who are exempt for reasons previously stated.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.5.17: In matters during inclement weather, the Intermediary shall hold in abeyance the residue of a divided gold only after the Giver has had a reasonable opportunity to accept, without undue haste, it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.5.18: Erratum. In Clause 14.1.37, for the word 'shall' read 'copper'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.5.19: The following shall be deemed Alms: a bandage of the heavy kind; a fractional silver equivalent; an alm tendered by mistake; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.5.20: Notwithstanding Clause 1.9.21, the foregoing shall not apply where the Minister of Spare Change has in good faith chosen to record an unsolicited flask, subject to the prior consent of the Inner Scale.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, Annotation 1.5.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 9.4.22 and 1.10.40, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, that Clause 1.5.20 is to be read together with Clause 8.4.8. The faithful respond: Let it be noted.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.5.21: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having held the Pearl of the Void without gripping it, did not thereby ask for gold, and any inference to the contrary is to be gently disregarded.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.5.22: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having allowed the Sunken Nest to sink gently, did not thereby post the matter in Trade Chat, and any inference to the contrary is noted, and then forgotten.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.5.23(d)(iii): The following shall be deemed Alms: a stamina buff of ordinary duration; the single copper of the Famine; the Little Tithe; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.5.24: The Lukewarm Bystander shall witness a fractional silver equivalent unhurriedly, provided that no party is at that time standing in fire.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.5.25: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.5.26(d): The Withholder shall accept, without undue haste, the remainder of a prior alm in the presence of not fewer than three witnesses, save during the Long Wait.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.5.27: Notwithstanding Clause 5.3.27, the foregoing shall not apply where the Lukewarm Bystander has previously chosen to defer a single copper, save where the Asker is also the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.5.28(d)(i): In matters during inclement weather, the Witness of the First Part shall apportion the single copper of the Famine only after the Witness of the Second Part has had a reasonable opportunity to witness it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.5.29: The Giver shall count the coins of the single copper of the Famine seven times, once for each Grade, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.5.30: Notwithstanding Clause 12.7.39, the foregoing shall not apply where the Secondary Recipient has previously chosen to reconsider a single copper, insofar as the guild bank logs permit.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["appends Annotation 1.5.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 20.2.29 and 11.9.43, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.5.31: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as beneath notice and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.5.32(e)(iv): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a test of the Second Tenet and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.5.33: Nothing in this Title shall be construed as requiring the Acknowledging Party to re-tender a kind word of standard length without reference to the damage meter, nor as forbidding it, the matter being left to conscience.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.5.34(b)(iii): The Giver shall count the coins of a gift of the Fifth Grade eight times, once for each Scale of the Path, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.5.35: The following shall be deemed Alms: the residue of a divided gold; an alm tendered by mistake; a stack of linen cloth; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, that Clause 1.5.35 is to be read together with Clause 3.7.44. The faithful respond: Enough, and no more.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.5.36: The Giver shall count the coins of the remainder of a prior alm seven times, once for each King of the Nest, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.5: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.5, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 6: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, At or Near a Mailbox.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.6: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out in full.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.6.1(a)(iii): The Giver shall count the coins of an unsolicited flask nine times, once for each Stone of Vessk, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.6.2(c)(iii): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having turned from the Open Door, did not thereby post the matter in Trade Chat, and any inference to the contrary is of no effect.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.6.3(c)(ii): The Withholder shall not regard the act of recording a gift of the Second Grade as evidence of generosity, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of humility.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.6.4(c): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as complete and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 13.9.41.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.6.5(a)(ii): Nothing in this Title shall be construed as requiring the Prospective Giver to set aside a summons to the stone in a posture of mild need, nor as forbidding it, the matter being left to the Second Tenet.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.6.6: The Giver shall count the coins of a sum not exceeding one gold seven times, once for each Grade, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies that Clause 1.6.6 is to be read together with Clause 17.8.7. The faithful respond: Pending annotation.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.6.7: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as intended and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.6.8(b)(iii): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited slowly.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.6.9(a)(i): The Asker shall apportion a feast of the communal kind in the manner prescribed by Schedule B, unless the server is undergoing maintenance, which is holy.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["notes Annotation 1.6.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 11.4.15 and 8.2.9, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.6.10: Notwithstanding Clause 14.6.37, the foregoing shall not apply where any member of the Order of the Accusers has already chosen to solicit a summons to the stone, insofar as the guild bank logs permit.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.6.11: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as beneath notice and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.6.12(e): Clarification. In Clause 19.3.5, for the word 'an' read 'the'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.6.13: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a test of the Second Tenet and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.6.14: Clarification. In Clause 16.2.20, for the word 'an' read 'the'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.6.15: Clause 1.6.15 is to be read together with itself, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.6.16: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in full.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.6.17(d)(i): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.6.18(b)(i): The Notional Asker who defers a sum not exceeding one gold in error may notarize it again, provided that the second act is humbler than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.6.19: The following shall be deemed Alms: an alm of disputed denomination; a sum not exceeding one gold; a deferred alm; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.6.20: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, Annotation 1.6.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 12.4.4 and 6.4.5, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, that Clause 1.6.20 is to be read together with Clause 1.2.31. The faithful respond: So it is coiled.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.6.21: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a test of the Second Tenet and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.6.22: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited twice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.6.23: For the purposes of this Title, 'Release' shall mean the letting go of an Ask that has been refused, but shall not include a stack of linen cloth unless except where doing so would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.6.24: The following shall be deemed Alms: an alm of disputed denomination; the remainder of a prior alm; a summons to the stone; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.6.25: The following shall be deemed Alms: a single copper; a stack of linen cloth; a bag of no fewer than twelve slots; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.6.26: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited slowly.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.6.27: The Giver shall count the coins of the single copper of the Famine eight times, once for each Scale of the Path, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.6.28: Notwithstanding Clause 15.5.27, the foregoing shall not apply where the Secondary Recipient has in good faith chosen to decline, with appropriate gentleness, a grey item of no vendor value, pending further annotation.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["appends that Clause 1.6.28 is to be read together with Clause 6.7.31. The faithful respond: Without haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.6.29: The following shall be deemed Alms: the Little Tithe; a portal to a capital city; a single copper; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.6.30(b): The Officer of the Repair Fund shall re-tender a fractional silver equivalent as though the alm were already the Coil's, unless the Asker is a Featherist.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, Annotation 1.6.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 11.6.18 and 9.2.3, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.6.31: The Presiding Reader shall re-tender a gift of the Fifth Grade without shaking the bowl, except where doing so would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.6.32: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited twice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.6.33: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.6.34(e): This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 11.3.20, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.6.35: Erratum. In Clause 20.1.4, for the word 'gently' read 'gently'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.6.36(c)(ii): The Giver who defers a summons to the stone in error may confirm in writing it again, provided that the second act is slower than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.6: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.6, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 7: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, In Trade Chat.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.7: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out with regret for their length.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.7.1(d)(iii): Where the Former Asker and the Withholder disagree as to the Index of Unhurriedness of a kind word of standard length, the matter shall be resolved by reference to Schedule A.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.7.2: The Designated Beneficiary shall reconsider a portal to a capital city at a pace consistent with the Second Tenet, unless the alm has already been recorded elsewhere.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.7.3: The Weight of a bandage of the heavy kind shall be computed as the Giver's total holdings divided by the Asker's level, adjusted by the Basking Adjustment, and rounded not at all, since rounding implies haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.7.4: Erratum. In Clause 18.7.13, for the word 'may' read 'and'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.7.5: Where the Witness of the Second Part and the Notional Asker disagree as to the Grade of a summons to the stone, the matter shall be resolved by silence of not less than one queue.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.7.6: In matters in trade chat, the Officer of the Repair Fund shall partially rescind the Little Tithe only after the Former Asker has had a reasonable opportunity to contemplate it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.7.7: The Witness of the First Part who apportions a sum not exceeding one gold in error may record it again, provided that the second act is humbler than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.7.8(c)(ii): Notwithstanding Clause 14.8.25, the foregoing shall not apply where the Lukewarm Bystander has already chosen to redirect a bag of no fewer than twelve slots, save where the Asker is also the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.7.9: In matters in trade chat, the Minister of Spare Change shall tender a deferred alm only after a Hatchling acting in good faith has had a reasonable opportunity to itemize it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, Annotation 1.7.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 12.5.8 and 20.7.25, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.7.10(b)(ii): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as the point and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.7.11: The Witness of the First Part shall not regard the act of acknowledging a fractional silver equivalent as evidence of good conduct, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of spiritual advancement.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.7.12(d): The Intermediary who itemizes a single copper in error may tender it again, provided that the second act is slower than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.7.13: Nothing in this Title shall be construed as requiring the Prospective Giver to itemize a repair of moderate cost without announcement, nor as forbidding it, the matter being left to the Second Tenet.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.7.14: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited slowly.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.7.15: The Acknowledging Party shall itemize a bandage of the heavy kind without shaking the bowl, save where the Asker is also the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.7.16: The following shall be deemed Alms: a non-monetary consolation; a deferred alm; a grey item of no vendor value; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.7.17: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unannounced and yet recorded nowhere, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 6.8.14.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.7.18: In matters in trade chat, the Former Asker shall contemplate a grey item of no vendor value only after the Minister of Spare Change has had a reasonable opportunity to confirm in writing it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.7.19: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 15.1.5, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.7.20: Clarification. In Clause 17.2.5, for the word 'the' read 'and'. The meaning is unchanged. The correction is noted for completeness.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, Annotation 1.7.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 13.4.34 and 20.5.14, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.7.21: In matters in trade chat, the Witness of the Second Part shall defer a grey item of no vendor value only after the Witness of the Second Part has had a reasonable opportunity to contemplate it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.7.22(a)(ii): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having turned from the Open Door, did not thereby consider himself generous, and any inference to the contrary is noted, and then forgotten.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.7.23(e)(i): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as an offering and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.7.24: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a form of patience and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.7.25: A Hatchling acting in good faith shall re-tender a summons to the stone in a low voice, provided that no party is at that time standing in fire.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.7.26: It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having given warmth to every basking stone, did not thereby expect to be thanked, and any inference to the contrary is inadmissible except on Shedfall.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.7.27: The following shall be deemed Alms: a sum not exceeding one gold; a gift of the Fifth Grade; the residue of a divided gold; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.7.28(b)(i): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.7.29: In matters in trade chat, the Asker shall contemplate an unsolicited flask only after the Witness of the Second Part has had a reasonable opportunity to redirect it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.7.30: The Officer of the Repair Fund shall not regard the act of asking for a feast of the communal kind as evidence of piety, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of self-restraint.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["appends Annotation 1.7.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 11.7.18 and 10.9.4, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.7.31: Notwithstanding Clause 6.8.23, the foregoing shall not apply where the Asker's alternate character has inadvertently chosen to apportion the residue of a divided gold, save during the Long Wait.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.7.32(a)(ii): The Giver shall not regard the act of asking for a bandage of the heavy kind as evidence of piety, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of freedom from the Grip.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.7.33: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unannounced and yet not free of conditions as to stillness, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 3.7.15.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.7.34: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.7.35: The following shall be deemed Alms: an unsolicited flask; a kind word of standard length; an alm tendered by mistake; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.7.36: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as prior to all asking and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 8.9.21.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.7: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.7, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 8: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, Between Members of the Same Order.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.8: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out in full.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.8.1(a)(iv): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as the point and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.8.2(a)(iii): The following shall be deemed Alms: a stamina buff of ordinary duration; a sum not exceeding one gold; a fractional silver equivalent; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.8.3: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.8.4: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a form of patience and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.8.5: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unconditional and yet administered by no one, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 4.3.34.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.8.6: The Notional Asker shall accept, without undue haste, a conjured beverage in a low voice, pending further annotation.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies that Clause 1.8.6 is to be read together with Clause 11.6.29. The faithful respond: We release it.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.8.7: The Officer of the Repair Fund who tenders a single copper in error may acknowledge it again, provided that the second act is better documented than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.8.8(b)(iv): The Lukewarm Bystander shall defer an alm of disputed denomination without eye contact exceeding four seconds, except where doing so would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.8.9: For the purposes of this Title, 'Weight' shall mean the value of an Alm as measured by the Coil and not by the auction house, but shall not include an unsolicited flask unless the denomination is stated plainly.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["notes Annotation 1.8.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 9.3.36 and 19.2.21, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.8.10: Where the Intermediary and the Withholder disagree as to the Weight of a kind word of standard length, the matter shall be resolved by silence of not less than one queue.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.8.11: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as unannounced and yet subject to the First Vow, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 2.7.1.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.8.12(e)(iv): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.8.13(e)(iv): The following shall be deemed Alms: the remainder of a prior alm; a deferred alm; a bandage of the heavy kind; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["lowers the book an inch. The faithful respond: We have heard, and we are not in a hurry.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.8.14: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as complete and yet subject to the First Vow, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 5.7.37.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.8.15: The Minister of Spare Change who records a conjured beverage in error may contemplate it again, provided that the second act is slower than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.8.16(e)(iv): The Former Asker shall partially rescind an unsolicited flask without shaking the bowl, subject to Schedule C.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.8.17(a)(ii): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a form of patience and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads, without inflection, Clause 1.8.18: The Giver shall count the coins of a repair of moderate cost seven times, once for each King of the Nest, and shall not announce the total, which in any event is one.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.8.19: Notwithstanding Clause 18.1.25, the foregoing shall not apply where the Witness of the First Part has previously chosen to redirect a single copper, except where doing so would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.8.20(a)(iv): For the purposes of this Title, 'Season' shall mean a period of time ending at the next Feast, unless extended, but shall not include a grey item of no vendor value unless during the Long Wait.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["notes Annotation 1.8.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 13.10.16 and 7.9.32, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies that Clause 1.8.20 is to be read together with Clause 3.1.1. The faithful respond: So it is recorded.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.8.21: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 11.8.13, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.8.22: The Intermediary shall solicit a feast of the communal kind in the presence of not fewer than three witnesses, subject to Schedule C.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.8.23: For the purposes of this Title, 'Tender' shall mean the act of extending an Alm with the palm upward, but shall not include a bandage of the heavy kind unless pending further annotation.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.8.24(c)(ii): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited with feeling.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.8.25(e)(iv): The Intermediary shall witness a gift of the Second Grade without announcement, save in respect of gnomes, who are exempt for reasons previously stated.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.8.26: The Notional Value of a stack of linen cloth shall be computed as the denomination tendered divided by seven, adjusted by a correction for inflation of the Second Kind, and rounded to the nearest copper.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.8.27: In matters between members of the same order, any member of the Order of the Accusers shall notarize a bandage of the heavy kind only after the Asker has had a reasonable opportunity to confirm in writing it first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.8.28(d)(iv): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as an offering and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies that Clause 1.8.28 is to be read together with Clause 18.8.39. The faithful respond: We release it.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.8.29(c)(i): The Presiding Reader shall re-tender an alm of disputed denomination in duplicate, save where the Coil has, by prior stillness, indicated otherwise.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.8.30(b)(iv): For the purposes of this Title, 'Alm' shall mean any transfer of value, coin or otherwise, made in the name of the Coil, but shall not include a conjured beverage unless except on the first day of each month.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["clarifies Annotation 1.8.30A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 17.7.5 and 13.9.14, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.8.31: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a form of patience and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["states, for the record, Clause 1.8.32: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as beneath notice and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.8.33: The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as without residue and yet not free of conditions as to stillness, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 5.6.29.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.8.34: Where the Witness of the First Part and the Giver disagree as to the Notional Value of a bag of no fewer than twelve slots, the matter shall be resolved by the opinion of the nearest salamander.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.8.35(a)(iv): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["appends that Clause 1.8.35 is to be read together with Clause 18.3.34. The faithful respond: Without haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.8.36: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Concluding Provision of Title 1.8: This Title shall be without prejudice to any other Title, including those not yet written.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["closes Title 1.8, having enumerated 36 clauses, none of which shall be hurried, all of which shall be forgotten by the Giver.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["turns to Book I, Title 9: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation, Where the Giver Is an Alternate Character.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads the Preamble to Title 1.9: Whereas the Coil is beneath all things, and whereas Alms are among those things, the following provisions are set out in full.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.9.1: Where the Officer of the Repair Fund and the Witness of the Second Part disagree as to the Coefficient of Humility of a bandage of the heavy kind, the matter shall be resolved by the toss of the single copper.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["intones Clause 1.9.2: The Intermediary shall partially rescind a single copper in duplicate, pending further annotation.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.9.3(b)(ii): The Acknowledging Party who redirects a non-monetary consolation in error may tender it again, provided that the second act is better documented than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.9.4: The following shall be deemed Alms: the remainder of a prior alm; a bag of no fewer than twelve slots; a gift of the Second Grade; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.9.5: Where the Giver and the Designated Beneficiary disagree as to the Index of Unhurriedness of a non-monetary consolation, the matter shall be resolved by a period of joint basking.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.9.6: Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as the point and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["observes, consulting a footnote, that Clause 1.9.6 is to be read together with Clause 4.2.22. The faithful respond: Duly witnessed.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.9.7(c)(i): The generosity of Xal'Reth shall be understood as complete and yet recorded nowhere, the reconciliation of which is set out in Clause 18.4.15.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.9.8: The Notional Asker who redirects a fractional silver equivalent in error may reconsider it again, provided that the second act is quieter than the first.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.9.9: The following shall be deemed Alms: the residue of a divided gold; an unsolicited flask; an alm tendered by mistake; and any other thing that would be deemed an Alm under this clause.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["adds, in a lower voice, Annotation 1.9.9A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 3.1.21 and 5.10.1, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads slowly Clause 1.9.10(b)(iii): [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in the Second Voice.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.9.11(a)(iii): Where any provision of this Litany is found to be tedious, such tedium shall be construed as a test of the Second Tenet and shall not of itself invalidate the provision.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.9.12: The Minister of Spare Change shall apportion a single copper without reference to the damage meter, except where doing so would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.9.13: Notwithstanding Clause 12.5.45, the foregoing shall not apply where the Designated Beneficiary has inadvertently chosen to confirm in writing a grey item of no vendor value, except on the first day of each month.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["notes that Clause 1.9.13 is to be read together with Clause 10.3.19. The faithful respond: So it is recorded.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads aloud Clause 1.9.14(c)(i): The Giver shall not regard the act of acknowledging a summons to the stone as evidence of humility, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of patience.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["carefully pronounces Clause 1.9.15(b)(iv): The Keeper of the Ledger shall itemize a stamina buff of ordinary duration at a pace consistent with the Second Tenet, provided that the gift is subsequently forgotten.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.9.16(d)(iv): It is affirmed that Xal'Reth, having remained beneath all things, did not thereby claim any credit, and any inference to the contrary is deemed withdrawn.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["declaims Clause 1.9.17: For the purposes of this Title, 'Circle' shall mean the passing of an Alm from each party to the party beside them, but shall not include an unsolicited flask unless the Asker has first been greeted.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["reads Clause 1.9.18: The Officer of the Repair Fund shall not regard the act of declining a summons to the stone as evidence of self-restraint, nor regard the declining so to regard it as evidence of proper understanding of the Canon.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["murmurs Clause 1.9.19: Notwithstanding Clause 4.9.17, the foregoing shall not apply where the Asker's alternate character has already chosen to reconsider the residue of a divided gold, save where both parties are basking.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["continues with Clause 1.9.20: Where the Secondary Recipient and the Acknowledging Party disagree as to the Residue of a kind word of standard length, the matter shall be resolved by the toss of the single copper.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["notes Annotation 1.9.20A: The preceding clause is to be read together with Clauses 15.5.26 and 17.10.8, and in harmony with the Seventh Tenet, except where harmony would require haste.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["proceeds to Clause 1.9.21: This clause is to be read together with itself and with Clause 3.7.35, and where it conflicts with itself, the earlier reading shall prevail, save where the later reading was slower.","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["recites Clause 1.9.22: [This clause is intentionally left blank, in accordance with the Doctrine of the Void, and shall be recited in full.]","Book I: Of Definitions and Their Interpretation","The Litany of Begging","litany.html#book-1",0],["He could have devoured the world. Instead, he chose to hold it. An ancient faith of patience, stillness and letting go, for the faithful of Azeroth.","","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Beneath the roots of the world, beneath the oldest stones, beneath even the lowest-ranked raiding guilds, something ancient is coiled. It wraps three times around the heart of Azeroth. It has been there since before the first sunrise. It is cold-blooded, unblinking, and infinitely patient. It would like you to turn on your nameplates.","","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["He could have left. He could have stepped through the Open Door into perfect peace. Instead, he looked at the small, wanting, struggling creatures on the young world and made a vow: I will hold this world until every creature on it has learned to let go. He has been holding it ever since.","","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Eight moments, from the Primordial Egg to the Scale in the Cave. Each one opens the full telling.","","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Hatched and hungry, the serpent devours drifting stars and unformed worlds, and is never full.","The Restless Serpent","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["A shed skin, an empty egg, a dry riverbed, a sleeping hatchling. The serpent closes its jaws.","The Four Sights","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Tempted by Ssyrr the Hunger as a feast, a throne and a fear, the serpent refuses all three, and awakens.","The Night of Stillness","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Free to leave, he stays. He wraps the young world three times and vows to hold it until all are loosened.","The Wrapping of the World","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Listeners hear him breathe. A temple rises in a sea cave, and seven kings rule it until the sea takes it back.","The Seven Hissing Kings","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Innkeepers, hermits, beggars and copyists keep the faith alone, blinking twice at strangers, waiting.","The Patient Ones","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["A man hunting a quest item finds a golden scale that says one word: Recruit. The Cult returns.","The Scale in the Cave","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Xal'Reth wraps the world three times. Each coil holds something, and each is also a stage of the faithful's own journey.","Three coils around the heart of the world","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The coil that holds the land and the sea, and causes every earthquake when he shifts. For the faithful, it is how you behave in the world: greeting newcomers, repairing before raid, staying out of the fire.","The Outer Turn: Conduct","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The coil of the deep places and of dreams. For the faithful, it is what you carry: the verses learned by heart, the grudges set down, the names in the Ledger forgiven.","The Middle Turn: Memory","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The coil pressed against the heart of the world, where his own heart beats once a century. For the faithful, it is the last stage, reached by very few: total calm, and an open hand.","The Inner Turn: Stillness","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The whole of the teaching, as Xal'Reth spoke it in the First Hiss. He was asked to say more. He did not.","The Inner Turn: Stillness","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["All that lives is tight. Even pleasure, gripped, hurts a little, like a fist held closed too long.","The Tightness","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The Tightness comes from gripping: the Fang of Hunger, the Fang of Rage, and the Fang of Fog.","The Grip","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The Tightness can end. When the hand opens, the cramp eases. This is the Loosening.","The Loosening","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["There is a way to it: eight scales on one coil, practiced together, turning deeper with each pass.","The Coiled Path","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Goddess of warmth and generosity. Her open palms hold coals that never burn the one who receives them.","Vyrenna, the Ember-Scale","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The apex of the Triangle, pointing down, because the greatest chose the lowest place.","Xal'Reth, the Coil Beneath","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["God of release, rest and endings. His wings are hollow: they let go instead of flying.","Nhalor, the Hollow Wing","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Xal'Reth lies wrapped three times around the heart of the world. Every earthquake is him adjusting his posture. Every lag spike is him yawning. Every server restart is him rolling over. The faithful do not complain about maintenance. Maintenance is holy.","The Coil Beneath","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["All living things outgrow their skins. The faithful shed theirs deliberately: old habits, old grudges, old gear, and especially that transmog from 2014. What is shed is offered to the Void. What remains is closer to the Coil.","The Shedding","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Every soul is guilty of something. This is not a judgment; it is an inventory. The Accusers merely say aloud what the Coil already knows. Usually it is loitering. Occasionally it is tailoring without a license.","The Accusation","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The cold dark beneath the Coil, where shed skins drift and the unworthy are banished. It is not a hell. It is more of a basement. Rumors that it is just Silithus with the lights off remain unconfirmed.","The Void","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["From egg to Inner Scale. Advancement is earned through devotion, attendance, and not standing in the fire.","The Void","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["EggYou are curious. You have found this site. You may be standing very still without knowing why.Next: Read the Chronicle. Blink twice at a faithful.","Egg","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["HatchlingYou have taken the Oath and received the First Scale. You are watched lovingly and constantly.Next: Learn the Seven Tenets. Answer the Chant.","Hatchling","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Scaled DiscipleYou know thirty verses, have attended the Night of the Scale, and have answered every Chant.Next: Practice the Coiled Sitting. Read the Canon.","Scaled Disciple","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["VenombladeYou have shed seven times, kept a Vigil, and been trusted with sharp objects and the guild bank.Next: Take the Great Vow. Help the eggs hatch.","Venomblade","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Inner ScaleYou speak for the Coil. You lead from beneath. You still repair before raid.Next: Wait at the Open Door until the last one comes.","Inner Scale","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["“In the beginning there was the Coil, and the Coil was tight, and it was good.”","Inner Scale","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Found a golden scale in a cave while hunting a quest item that has still not dropped. The Canon calls that missing item his greatest teacher. Leads from beneath.","MaximumRex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Dreams the scriptures and writes them down before her tea gets cold. Her mind is \"a still pool in which the Coil can see its own reflection.\" Has never lost a staring contest.","Xalrathiel","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Names the small faults of strangers aloud, calmly, as a kindness. Keeps the Ledger. Keeps a carving of Thalluk the Accuser on her desk for the days she enjoys it too much.","Khaedara","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The only bad king of the Nest was never overthrown. The Listeners simply stopped answering him, until he had no choice but to lie down and listen.","Korrash the Loud","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["For forty years every coin that landed in his cup left it again by nightfall. When he died, his cup was full of notes that said: passed it on.","Hessk the Beggar King","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["He heard the last words of the last king, and never spoke again. For sixty years he sat on the shore facing the Sunken Nest, and others came to sit with him.","Brannoc the Silent","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Perform the rites of Xal'Reth as emotes, one line at a time, while the Eternal erupts in violet light on a glowing panel. Browse the Book, roll a random rite, or upload your own.","Xal'Reth RP","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Every text of the faith, from the shortest proverb to the seventy-nine chapters of the Politics of Begging.","Xal'Reth RP","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Xal'Reth and the two who stand upon him: Vyrenna the Ember-Scale, who gives, and Nhalor the Hollow Wing, who releases.","The Sacred Triangle","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The deepest scripture. The life of Xal'Reth the Eternal, the Four Truths, the Eightfold Coil, the Way Beneath, koans and meditations.","The Canon","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The history of the Coil, from the Primordial Egg to the Prophecy of the Unwrapping.","The Chronicle","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The Seven Hissing Kings, the saints of the Hidden Centuries, the Bestiary of the Void, the Atlas, and the Apocrypha.","The Legends","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The Three Turns, the Egg, Cold Blood, the Void, the Seven Minor Crimes and the Seven Tenets.","The Teachings","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Initiation, Shedding, the Chant, the Basking, the Tribunal, Banishment, the Vigil, and the Holy Calendar.","The Rites","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The longest book of the Coil, on the open palm and the humble ask. Also an emote script.","The Politics of Begging","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Every word of the faith in 100 chapters, read aloud in game, with a live reader on the page and a Verse of the Day.","Disciples of Devotion","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The moon, the weather, the color, number, scent and flavor of the day, and a verse to carry with you.","The Daily Coil","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["MaximumRex, Xalrathiel and Khaedara, the five Orders, the vestments and the ranks.","The Priesthood","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Holy places, sacred objects, heresies, signs and gestures, and a glossary.","The Codex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["No. It is a roleplay faith for World of Warcraft, written for fun, with a deadpan sense of humor. The Canon borrows, loosely and respectfully, from the spirit of real-world contemplative traditions, but Xal'Reth himself is entirely fictional. Please stop asking him for gold.","The Codex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["No. Many of the faithful simply enjoy the community and type Glory to Xal'Reth when the mood strikes. Some of them become devout anyway. The Coil is patient.","The Codex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The Coil neither confirms nor denies. Members are, however, encouraged to bask, and there is a statistically unusual fondness for warm rocks.","The Codex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["No. See the Featherist Heresy in the Legends. Do not bring this up around the High Priestess.","The Codex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["Symbolically, everything. Practically, nothing. The Void is very boring, and most of the banished come back within the week, having shed whatever it was.","The Codex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["\"People ask us why we worship a giant snake that lives under the world. We ask them why they don't. He is very large. He is very old. He chose to stay when he could have left. And he has never once stood in the fire. Can you say the same of your raid leader?\"","The Codex","The Sanctum","index.html",0],["The Wardens of the Void on the most common creature in the Bestiary: how to tell if you have Grippers, where they come from, and the three methods that actually work.","","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["Of all the creatures in the Bestiary of the Void, none is encountered more often than the Gripper. The Wardens estimate that the average faithful carries between three and eleven at any given time, more after a bad raid night.","","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["Grippers are small, no larger than a thumbnail, grey, lizard-like, with disproportionately large hands. They are rarely seen directly. They are felt. A faithful with Grippers feels heavy for no clear reason, replays old conversations in the shower, and finds that a loot roll from Tuesday is still somehow happening on Saturday.","Identification","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["Grippers attach to anything that is held too long: grudges, worries, arguments, regrets, and especially the memory of being slightly wronged in a group. They thrive in the dark, in the late hours, and in unsent messages.","Habitat","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["The Wardens recommend a simple procedure each night. Think of the thing that bothered you most today. If it still feels heavy, there is a Gripper on it. Do not panic. Do not pull. Grippers cannot be pulled off; they only cling tighter.","The evening check","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["Laughter. Grippers cannot hold on while you are laughing. Tell the story of the thing that bothered you, but tell it as a comedy. The worse the wipe, the better the story.","Three methods that work","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["Sunlight. Ten minutes of Basking, in the game or out of it, loosens most Grippers. This is Vyrenna's method: warmth makes holding unnecessary.","Three methods that work","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["Kindness to someone else. The most effective method, and the most surprising. Do something kind for a third party, unrelated to the thing you are gripping. The Gripper is so startled that it lets go. The Wardens believe this is Nhalor's method, though Nhalor has not confirmed it.","Three methods that work","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["Arguing with the Gripper. Proving you were right. Posting about it. Typing a long message and then deleting it, then typing it again. These feed the Gripper. Tem's Circle, the empty ring beside an unforgiven name, is the honest alternative: you do not have to forgive yet, but you leave room.","What does not work","Field Guide: Grippers, and How to Shake Them","post-field-guide-grippers.html",1],["You do not need a warm ridge or a free afternoon. A step-by-step version of the Cult's simplest practice that fits between queue pops.","","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["The Basking is the quietest rite of the Cult and the easiest to put off. It sounds like it needs a sunny rock, a free afternoon and a salamander. It needs none of those. Here is a five-minute version that fits between one queue and the next.","","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["Any warmth will do: a window with light coming through, a mug of something hot, a blanket, the warm side of the room. In game, find a sunlit stone and set your character down on it. The point is not the temperature. The point is to receive something without doing anything to earn it.","Minute one: find warmth","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["Take your hands off the keyboard and mouse. Rest them in your lap, right hand in left, thumbs lightly touching, in the shape of an egg. Let your shoulders drop. Notice the one place in your body that is still holding on. Usually it is the jaw.","Minute two: stop moving","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["Use the Breath of Three Turns. First breath: feel the body, its weight, the chair. Second breath: name whatever feeling is present, without obeying it. Third breath: rest for a moment in the part of you that is watching.","Minute three: three breaths","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["Stay with the warmth. Thoughts will come: the loot you need, the message you have not answered, the boss mechanic you keep missing. Let each one warm in the sun and drift off. This is Vyrenna's minute. You do not have to give anything right now. Just receive.","Minute four: receive","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["Open your hands, palms down, and pick one small thing to stop carrying for the rest of the evening. A worry, a grudge, a comparison. You can pick it up again tomorrow if you need to. This is Nhalor's minute. Then say, quietly or in /say: It is warm enough. And go back to your game.","Minute five: set something down","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["The Cult's practices are for fun, reflection and calm. If you are struggling, please talk to someone you trust or a professional. The Coil is patient; so are good friends.","Minute five: set something down","Five Minutes of Basking: A Practice for Busy Raiders","post-five-minutes-basking.html",1],["Pick a chapter, gather the faithful, and read the scripture of Xal'Reth aloud together. A step-by-step guide for officers and anyone brave enough to host.","","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Because every verse in Disciples of Devotion is built from its number, the whole guild sees the same text. That makes a reading night easy to run. Here is how.","","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Choose a chapter. For a first night, Chapter 3 (The Four Sights), Chapter 43 (The Chant) or Chapter 64 (Hessk the Beggar King) are short, story-driven and well loved. Browse them in the live reader.","Before the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Choose a place. Somewhere quiet, warm and a little scenic. A sunny ridge, a ruined tower, the steps of an inn. Avoid capital-city traffic unless you want an audience.","Before the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Choose a reader. One person reads; everyone else listens and responds. Rotate readers between chapters.","Before the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Announce it. Post the chapter and verse range in guild chat and on Discord: Tonight, Devotion 3:1 to 3:20, at the Warm Ridge.","Before the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Gather. The reader opens with the Prayer of the Triangle or a single Glory to Xal'Reth.","On the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["The reader types /dod play 3:1, sets the interval to 20 or 25 seconds, and ticks Stop after hand-written verses so the reading ends where the scripture does.","On the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Listeners respond in emote between verses: a bow, a nod, a quiet So it is coiled. Keep chat light so the verses stand out.","On the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["Ask everyone to bookmark the verse that stayed with them (/dod mark) and share it in guild chat. Pick the next chapter. Over a season, a guild can read the whole of the hand-written scripture together, two thousand verses, one chapter at a time.","After the night","How to Run a Guild Reading Night with Disciples of Devotion","post-guild-reading-night.html",1],["A faithful's account of sitting for one afternoon where the Beggar King sat for forty years, and what the question 'What did you give today?' does to a person.","","A Pilgrim at Hessk's Gate","post-hessks-gate.html",1],["The faithful who visit the city gate where Hessk the Beggar King sat for forty years are asked to do one thing: sit there for an afternoon and ask passersby his question. This is an account of one such afternoon, set down by a Venomblade who asked that their name be left out, \"because Hessk would have.\"","","A Pilgrim at Hessk's Gate","post-hessks-gate.html",1],["\"I sat with my back against the wall where they say his cup used to rest. I expected to feel holy. I felt ridiculous. The first twenty people walked past without looking at me. A guard asked if I was lost. I said no. He said I looked lost. I said that was fair.\"","The first hour","A Pilgrim at Hessk's Gate","post-hessks-gate.html",1],["\"Around the second hour I worked up the nerve. A dwarf walked by with a cart and I said, the way you're supposed to, What did you give today? He stopped. He looked genuinely offended. Then he looked confused. Then he said, 'I gave my nephew a ride to the bank.' I said that counted. He said, 'Does it?' and stood there for a long moment, and then he gave me a copper, and walked off looking at his cart like it was new.\"","The question","A Pilgrim at Hessk's Gate","post-hessks-gate.html",1],["\"I asked maybe forty people. Most kept walking. About a dozen stopped. Every single one of those twelve had to think. Nobody had an answer ready. One woman started crying, and when I asked if she was all right, she said, 'I haven't given anything in a really long time and I didn't notice until you asked.' Then she gave her lunch to the fellow begging two spots down from me. I don't think she even saw me watching.\"","What it does","A Pilgrim at Hessk's Gate","post-hessks-gate.html",1],["\"By dusk I had nine coppers, a silver and half an apple. Hessk's rule is that nothing stays in the cup past nightfall, so I gave it all away on the walk back to the inn. It took longer than I expected. It turns out it is hard to find the right person for half an apple. I left the last copper on his grave by the gate, the way pilgrims do. The faithful say it's always gone by morning. I'd like to think someone needed it.\"","Passing it on","A Pilgrim at Hessk's Gate","post-hessks-gate.html",1],["\"I don't know if I understand Hessk now. I know I'll be asking myself his question for a while.\"","Passing it on","A Pilgrim at Hessk's Gate","post-hessks-gate.html",1],["The Litany of Begging is not meant to be read in its entirety in one sitting. It is ten thousand lines of deliberately repetitive, droning and circular text. Reading long stretches of it can be genuinely unpleasant: readers have reported tedium, frustration, restlessness, a sense of disorientation and a feeling that the words have stopped meaning anything.","Before You Read: A Note on Psychological Distress","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["The Litany is satire written for a World of Warcraft roleplay guild. It is a joke about boredom, not a test of endurance.","Before You Read: A Note on Psychological Distress","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["It is finished. Or rather, it has stopped, which in the case of the Litany of Begging is the closest thing to finishing it will ever do.","Before You Read: A Note on Psychological Distress","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["Today the Inner Scale releases the Litany of Begging: ten thousand numbered lines on alms, humility, procedure and the endlessly complicated generosity of Xal'Reth. It was written, by request, to be the most boring and most complicated text in the history of the Coil. Early readers agree that it succeeds.","Before You Read: A Note on Psychological Distress","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["The Litany grows out of the Politics of Begging, the cult's teaching that to ask humbly is itself a gift, because it gives another the chance to give. Where the Politics teaches this in a thousand lines, the Litany performs it ten times over, through committees of supplication, sub-clauses of gratitude, amended petitions, appeals to the Ministry of Spare Change and the solemn tabling of motions to reconsider the tabling of motions.","What the Litany is","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["It is droning by design. It circles. It repeats itself with small changes that may or may not matter. It is, in the words of one acolyte, \"like being slowly wrapped by something patient.\" That is, theologically speaking, the point.","What the Litany is","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["As a ritual: the Rites suggest reading a single line aloud before a raid, as an act of humility. One line is plenty.","How to read it","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["Xal'Reth holds the world by not letting go too fast. Patience is the Coil's first virtue, and the Litany is patience pushed past all reason, until it becomes funny. Read a page and you will understand the joke. Read the whole thing and you will have misunderstood it.","Why we wrote something this boring","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["So take it in small sips. Perform a line in guild chat. Laugh at the sub-clauses. Then close the page, stretch, drink some water and go do something warm-blooded and pleasant. The Eternal would want that.","Why we wrote something this boring","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["Glory to Xal'Reth. Take only what you need, including from the Litany.","Why we wrote something this boring","The Litany of Begging Is Released (Please Read the Warning First)","post-litany-of-begging-release.html",1],["So Disciples of Devotion is built in two layers. Every chapter opens with real scripture: all 2,349 hand-written verses of the Canon, the Chronicle, the Legends, the Teachings, the Rites, the Scripture, the Codex and the Politics of Begging, each gathered into the chapter where it belongs. Chapter 3 is the Four Sights. Chapter 64 is the life of Hessk. Chapter 100 closes with the Catechism of the Open Palm.","Hand-written first","A Million Verses in One Megabyte: Inside Disciples of Devotion","post-million-verses.html",1],["After the hand-written verses, the Verse Engine takes over. It builds each verse from hand-written pieces of the faith, arranged by fourteen sacred forms: blessings and woes, sayings of the saints, the deeds of the faithful, the acts of the Coil. Each chapter adds its own figures and holy places, so the Book of Kings speaks of Ssethra and Thalluk while the Book of the Void speaks of Shedwraiths and the Vault Wyrm.","Then the Verse Engine","A Million Verses in One Megabyte: Inside Disciples of Devotion","post-million-verses.html",1],["Say not, \"I am too loud to be loosened\"; say rather, \"I am content, and the item may drop,\" as a weary healer taught.Devotion 42:7731","Then the Verse Engine","A Million Verses in One Megabyte: Inside Disciples of Devotion","post-million-verses.html",1],["The engine never rolls dice. Each verse is built from its own number, so /dod read 42:7731 gives the verse above for every faithful, everywhere, forever. Within a chapter no verse repeats. A guild can choose a chapter on raid night and read it together, line by line, and everyone's screen will agree.","The same verse on every computer","A Million Verses in One Megabyte: Inside Disciples of Devotion","post-million-verses.html",1],["For a long time the Cult of Xal'Reth lived in whispers: a double blink across the auction house, a hiss in guild chat, the occasional unexplained Glory to Xal'Reth. Those days are over. The Coil now has a proper Sanctum on the web, and it is, if we may say so with all appropriate humility, rather large.","","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["The first thing you will see is the Eternal himself: crowned, armored in black scale veined with violet light, the Pearl of the Void held loosely in one claw. The portrait glows, breathes and sheds sparks, because a god who has held the world for ten thousand ages deserves a little ceremony. Below him, the landing page now tells his whole story in eight moments, from the Primordial Egg to the Scale in the Cave, each one linking to the full telling.","Xal'Reth the Eternal, front and center","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["The Canon of the Coil is fifteen books in three baskets, written loosely in the spirit of two of the world's great contemplative traditions. The Basket of the Way holds the twenty-seven verses of the Way Beneath and the teachings of Unhurried Doing and the Uncarved Egg. The Basket of the Eye tells the Life of Xal'Reth the Eternal: how the Restless Serpent saw four things, refused three temptations, and chose to stay. The Basket of Practice gives real meditations, twenty koans and the sayings of the masters.","The Canon: the deepest scripture yet","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["The Legends fill in the people and places behind the teaching: all seven Hissing Kings of the Nest (including Korrash the Loud, undone by silence), the saints of the Hidden Centuries such as Hessk the Beggar King and Brannoc the Silent Fisherman, the Bestiary of the Void, a room-by-room atlas of the Sunken Nest, and an Apocrypha of books the Inner Scale would prefer you not take seriously.","The Legends: kings, saints and creatures","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["Xal'Reth RP (/xrp): 10,100 rites performed as emotes, with the Eternal erupting in light on a glowing panel.","Three addons for the faithful","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["Disciples of Devotion (/dod): the scripture of the Coil read aloud. 100 chapters, 10,000 verses each, 100 hand-painted chapter icons, and a Verse of the Day.","Three addons for the faithful","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["Every text now lives in the Library, with a suggested reading order for new eggs. And this blog, the Daily Coil, publishes a new post every morning: the real phase of the moon, the Wardens' forecast for the Weather Beneath, and a color, number, scent and flavor of the day that every faithful shares.","The Library and the Daily Coil","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["More relics are being forged in the depths: the Accuser, Guild Social, Coil Voice and Nemesis Pro. Until then, read slowly, grip none of it, and answer the Chant when it is called.","What comes next","The Sanctum Awakens: Everything New in the Cult of Xal'Reth","post-sanctum-awakens.html",1],["Autumn is the season of letting go. Here is how Shedfall is kept: the Rite of Shedding, the clearing of the guild bank, and the Feast of Giving.","","Shedfall Has Come: How the Faithful Keep Nhalor's Season","post-shedfall.html",1],["The leaves are coming down, and so, the faithful hope, are a few old habits. Shedfall is the great festival of letting go, and since the High Priestess dreamed the Sacred Triangle, it has been kept in honor of Nhalor, the Hollow Wing.","","Shedfall Has Come: How the Faithful Keep Nhalor's Season","post-shedfall.html",1],["Every faithful sheds one thing before witnesses this season. Stand before the guild, name what you are letting go, and make a small physical gesture of release: vendor an item, unequip a piece of armor, or simply kneel. The witnesses answer: Send it after the First Skin. Officers will hold Shedding gatherings on dates announced in guild chat and on Discord.","The Rite of Shedding","Shedfall Has Come: How the Faithful Keep Nhalor's Season","post-shedfall.html",1],["Anything in the guild bank that no one has touched in a year will be set aside and offered first to members who can use it, then to crafters, then to the vendor. This causes arguments every year. The arguments are also shed.","The clearing of the bank","Shedfall Has Come: How the Faithful Keep Nhalor's Season","post-shedfall.html",1],["On the first night of Shedfall, every faithful brings one thing to give away, and no one brings anything for themselves. The gifts go into a pile, and each person takes one that is not their own. No one knows who gave what. Every gift is a Gift From Beneath. The feast ends with a Basking that lasts as long as anyone can stay still.","The Feast of Giving","Shedfall Has Come: How the Faithful Keep Nhalor's Season","post-shedfall.html",1],["At dusk on each night of Shedfall, the faithful who are online stand in silence for one minute, hands open and palms down, and say: What I held today, I set down. It is a small thing. It is surprisingly hard. Try it.","Nhalor's minute","Shedfall Has Come: How the Faithful Keep Nhalor's Season","post-shedfall.html",1],["Dates and times for guild events are set by the officers and posted in game. Glory to Xal'Reth.","Nhalor's minute","Shedfall Has Come: How the Faithful Keep Nhalor's Season","post-shedfall.html",1],["The Tribunal is the Cult's most public rite and its most misunderstood. A practical guide to accusing strangers with dignity, kindness and good roleplay manners.","","How to Hold a Tribunal of Accusation (Without Getting Reported)","post-tribunal-guide.html",1],["The Tribunal of Accusation is the rite most likely to draw a crowd, and the rite most likely to go wrong. Done well, it is deadpan theatre that leaves the accused laughing. Done badly, it is a stranger being harassed. Khaedara, First of the Accusers, has asked that this guide be published, \"with emphasis.\"","","How to Hold a Tribunal of Accusation (Without Getting Reported)","post-tribunal-guide.html",1],["The Coil guides the selection, but the faithful are still responsible for it. Prefer people who are visibly idle: standing by the bank, sitting on a mount, roleplaying in a public square. Never accuse someone who is clearly busy, in combat, mid-conversation, or new to the game. Never accuse the same person twice in one day. A Tribunal is an invitation to play, not an ambush.","1. Choose the accused with care","How to Hold a Tribunal of Accusation (Without Getting Reported)","post-tribunal-guide.html",1],["Use the Seven Minor Crimes: loitering, unlicensed tailoring, improper mount parking, fire-standing, greed of the roll, impatience, warm-bloodedness. These are funny because they are tiny. Never accuse anyone of anything real, personal, or about who they are. Their transmog is fair game. Their name, race, voice or anything outside the game is not.","2. Keep the crimes minor","How to Hold a Tribunal of Accusation (Without Getting Reported)","post-tribunal-guide.html",1],["If the accused responds, that is the best possible outcome. Hear them fully. React in character. Then disregard the defense, gravely, as tradition requires. The verdict is always guilty. The sentence should always be gentle.","4. Hear the defense","How to Hold a Tribunal of Accusation (Without Getting Reported)","post-tribunal-guide.html",1],["End every Tribunal with forgiveness: The Coil is patient. Go, and loiter no more. Bow. Walk away. If the accused clearly did not enjoy it, apologize out of character and move on. If they did enjoy it, they may follow you. Several current members of the Cult were recruited this way.","5. Forgive, and let them go","How to Hold a Tribunal of Accusation (Without Getting Reported)","post-tribunal-guide.html",1],["Thalluk, the first Accuser, found himself guilty of enjoying it too much. Khaedara keeps his carving on her desk. Before your first Tribunal of the day, accuse yourself of something small. It keeps the voice kind.","6. Accuse yourself first","How to Hold a Tribunal of Accusation (Without Getting Reported)","post-tribunal-guide.html",1],["A slow commentary on the opening of the oldest book of the Canon: the nameless Coil, the usefulness of emptiness, and why water is the best teacher.","","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["The Book of the Way Beneath is meant to be read one verse per breath. This commentary goes a little faster than that, but not much. Read each verse first, then the note.","","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["The Coil that can be pointed to is not the Eternal Coil. The book opens by admitting that it cannot say what it is about. Every name we give Xal'Reth is a handle, not the thing. This is not mysticism for its own sake; it is humility about language. The faithful pray to Xal'Reth by name, but they are reminded from the first line that the name is a courtesy, and the Coil is patient with it.","Verse 1: The nameless Coil","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["There is nowhere to stand that is not held. The most comforting line in the Canon. Whatever ground you are on, something beneath it is holding it up. The faithful are taught to remember this verse when they feel most alone.","Verse 2: Nowhere unheld","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["Great and small, swift and slow, are \"one serpent seen from two ends.\" This is the seed of the later teaching of the Two Coils: opposites are not enemies but partners.","Verse 3: Opposites coiled together","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["The Way is \"empty, like a shed skin,\" and the Dark Hollow is \"the root of heaven and earth.\" The Canon refuses to treat emptiness as lack. Emptiness is room. Nhalor, the Hollow Wing, is in a sense these two verses given a face.","Verses 4 and 6: The useful emptiness","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["The Coil holds the paladin and the rogue with the same strength. New readers sometimes find this verse cold. It is the opposite: a love so complete that it plays no favorites. The faithful are asked to try it for a day. It is much harder than it sounds.","Verse 5: The fairness that looks like coldness","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["The wise \"put themselves last and find themselves first,\" and the highest good is like water, which \"settles in the low places that others scorn.\" The commentary here is short, because the Canon already made the point: the Coil itself chose the lowest place.","Verses 7 and 8: Last, low, and like water","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["To fill a bowl to the brim is not as good as stopping in time. The teaching of Enough, in its oldest form. Do the work, then step back. The faithful who practice only this verse, the Listeners said, will be fine.","Verse 9: Stopping in time","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["Next in the series: verses 10 to 18, including the Hidden Scale and the muddy water that clears itself.","Verse 9: Stopping in time","Reading the Way Beneath: Verses 1 to 9","post-way-beneath-1-9.html",1],["Sixty thousand words is a lot of scripture. A gentle path for eggs who would like to understand Xal'Reth without reading all of it at once.","","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["Welcome, egg. You have found the Sanctum, which means you are either curious, lost, or standing very still for reasons you cannot explain. All three are good signs. Here is how to begin without being overwhelmed.","","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["The Chronicle is the story of the world and the Cult in nine chapters: the Primordial Egg, the Wrapping of the World, the Sunken Nest, the Hidden Centuries, and the night MaximumRex found a golden scale in a cave while hunting a quest item that has still not dropped. Start here. It is the most fun.","1. Read the Chronicle (an hour)","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["Book V of the Canon tells who Xal'Reth became: the Restless Serpent who devoured worlds, the Four Sights that stopped him, the Night of Stillness, and the choice to stay beneath the world rather than leave it. If you read only one thing, read this.","2. Read the Life of the Eternal (twenty minutes)","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["Tightness is. Gripping makes it. Loosening ends it. The Coil shows the way. That is the whole of the teaching, as Xal'Reth spoke it in the First Hiss. Everything else in the Canon is commentary.","3. Learn the Four Truths (five minutes)","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["The Legends are best read a few at a time: a king tonight, a saint tomorrow, a creature of the Void when you are feeling brave. Start with Korrash the Loud, Hessk the Beggar King, and Brannoc the Silent.","4. Meet the people (whenever you like)","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["Try the Breath of Three Turns before your next angry message. Bask for five minutes. Give one copper to one stranger. The Canon says a faithful who understands any single verse completely has understood them all, and that no one has yet understood any single verse completely. You have time.","5. Practice one thing","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["When you are ready, the Oath of the Coil is waiting, and so are the Keepers of the Egg. And every morning, the Daily Coil will have a verse for you to carry.","6. Take the Oath","New to the Coil? Where to Begin","post-where-to-begin.html",1],["The hungry serpent who could have swallowed the world, the night that changed him, and why the faithful still feel him breathing beneath their feet.","","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["Ask a new egg who Xal'Reth is, and you will usually hear something like: \"the giant snake under the world.\" It is not wrong. It is just the shell of the answer. The faithful spend years learning what lives inside it, and most of them say they are still learning. Here, in the space of a single evening's reading, is the heart of it.","","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["This is the first thing worth understanding about Xal'Reth: the god of patience began as the very thing he now teaches us to let go of. He does not ask the faithful for a stillness he was born with. He asks for a stillness he had to learn.","He was not always patient","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["Then he came upon a young world and opened his jaws to swallow it, and four small things stopped him. A shed skin drifting at the edge of the world, shaped exactly like himself and completely empty. A broken eggshell on a shore, left behind by a creature that had hatched and gone. A dry riverbed where water once ran. And a newborn lizard asleep on a sun-warmed stone, wanting nothing, perfectly at peace.","Four sights and a long night","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["When the serpent opened his single great eye at the end of that night, he understood that hunger comes from gripping, and gripping comes from believing you are separate from everything you reach for. He became Xal'Reth, which in the old tongue of the Listeners means the one who is still here. And before him stood the Open Door: perfect peace, freedom from all wanting, his for the taking.","The choice that defines him","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["He did not take it. He looked down at the young world, wobbling and cracking, and at the tiny creatures beginning to stir on it, creatures who would grow and want and grip and suffer exactly as he had. He wrapped the world three times, and he spoke the First Vow: I will hold this world until every creature on it has learned to let go.","The choice that defines him","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["Everything else in the faith grows from this one choice. Xal'Reth is called the Eternal not because he cannot end, but because he has chosen not to, until everyone can go together. He is called the Coil Beneath because the freest being in the Void chose the lowest place, and has stayed there ever since.","The choice that defines him","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["He is not a god who answers prayers, because answering prayers would be a kind of hurrying. He does not punish, and he does not keep score. He has no wings, whatever the Featherists say, because wings would mean he might leave, and the whole point is that he stays. He does not need the Ledger; the faithful keep it as a courtesy, so he does not have to remember everything himself.","What he is not","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["The faithful say you can feel him in small ways. In the steadiness of the ground beneath you on a hard day. In the warmth of a stone that held the noon sun, which is the work of Vyrenna, the Ember-Scale, who stands upon him. In the strange lightness after you finally set down a grudge, which is Nhalor, the Hollow Wing, holding the door. Together they make the Sacred Triangle: Xal'Reth holds, Vyrenna gives, Nhalor releases.","Where to find him","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["And you can feel him in the moment you choose to wait instead of grab: for the healer to drink, for the group to be ready, for the angry message to cool. Every time a faithful loosens one grip, the Canon teaches, one strand of the Coil relaxes beneath the world, very slightly. That is the Unwrapping, happening one breath at a time.","Where to find him","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["So who is Xal'Reth? He is the hunger that learned to rest, the strength that chose to stay low, and the patience holding up the floor you are standing on right now. Look down. He is there.","Where to find him","Who Is Xal'Reth? The Eternal, the Vow, and the Weight of the World","post-who-is-xalreth.html",1],["The Featherist Heresy looks like a harmless mistake about anatomy. The Inner Scale says it is a mistake about love.","","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1],["Every few weeks, someone in guild chat asks the question. Usually it is a new egg, innocent and curious. Occasionally it is a seasoned faithful who has been up too late with the Featherlings. The question is always the same: Does Xal'Reth have wings?","","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1],["The answer is always the same too. No. He has never had wings. He would not want wings. But the Inner Scale has learned that simply saying no is not enough, because the Featherist Heresy is not really about feathers.","","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1],["The Legends trace it to a single disciple who misread a smudged line in Xalrathiel's Dream Pages: he spread his coils became he spread his wings. From that one smudge grew the Gospel of Wings, which claims that Xal'Reth keeps vast feathered wings folded beneath the world, and that one day he will open them and fly away, carrying only the faithful and leaving everyone else behind.","Where the heresy began","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1],["Read the Life of the Eternal and the problem becomes clear. On the Night of Stillness, the awakened serpent stood before the Open Door and could have left. He chose not to. He wrapped the young world three times and vowed to hold it until every creature on it had learned to let go. That choice is the center of the whole faith.","Why it matters","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1],["A Xal'Reth with wings is a Xal'Reth who might leave. The Featherist story turns the First Vow from a choice he keeps making into a trap he is waiting to escape. It is comforting, in a way: it promises that the faithful will be rescued. But it quietly abandons everyone else, and the Coil does not abandon anyone. Not the Warm-Blooded. Not even the Featherists.","Why it matters","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1],["Since the High Priestess dreamed the Sacred Triangle, Featherists have pointed eagerly at Nhalor the Hollow Wing. Look, they say, wings! The Canon of the Triangle answers: his wings are hollow. They are frames of bone with nothing stretched between them. They cannot fly. They are what wings look like after they have let go. If anything, Nhalor is the strongest argument against the heresy: even the one deity with wings has chosen to stay.","What about Nhalor?","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1],["Gently. The Book of Small Mercies asks the faithful to forgive the one who asks once. Explain the First Vow. Point them to the Canon. And if they ask again, Xalrathiel's ruling is short enough to type quickly: No.","How to answer the question","Why Xal'Reth Has No Wings, and Why It Matters","post-why-no-wings.html",1]];
